        <head>LADIES' LETTER</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>There are some exquisite gowns being sold just now; indeed, the woman who does not want her mind filled with desires to expend a goodly sum in eostumss had better stay away from the West End of London. Lace insertion, more especially those heavy guipure and point de Vevise in a beige or ecru tint, are largely used on foulards and lawns, and on fancy silks, especially on chine taffeta, the chine effect being again popular. Amongst the washable type of gown, twilled white silks are missing, and the useful grass lawn, but organdie and spotted muslins have taken their place, and the white muslin robe. I saw a gown the other day which was said to be quite in the latest fashion, with its tight back, with the braces of insertion running down to a point, and in front going on each side of both bodice and skirt, and then forming a border to the gown. A stripe of insertion runs down the sleeve, and a tiny epaulette is made of lace. The front of the bodice is full.</p>
        <p>Yes; there are plenty of novelties in headgear. As usual, you say. I quite agree. Dame Fashion is wondrous ehabgeable in this matter. The newest hats are laden with flowers, a,cl hydrangeas and roses seem the most popular blossoms. A very dainty hat of Tuscan straw in its brightest tint had clusters of mauve and white lilac com- pletely outlining the crown, and a big bow of heliotrope ribbon and very high wired groups of the mixed lilac. A pale green straw of the rough nutmeg type ho huge bunches of purple hydrangea, and a black crinoline is completely covered with bunches of cowslips and tall groups of purple iris. Very dainty bonnets for matrons have a raised Stuart front, the recesses on either side filled in with flowers, over which lace or sequined scallops fall softly.</p>
        <p>If you are in want of a blouse or two—and they are always very useful there are just now innuaierable different styles from which you may mak, your selectian. One with which I have been especially struck is of turquoise-blue glace, the cross-over fronts trimmed very prettily with frills of blonde lace, and disclosing a chemisette of tucked white glac4 and. lace. If something more expensive be required, you cannot possibly do better than secure. another model of blue silk covered with ivory lace, which in front makes background for a trailing true-lovers' knot em- broidered in blue velvet baby ribbon. Tee little yoke and collar-band are of transparent lace. Per- haps, however, you desire something simpler. There is a wide range for choice, and you will fled it dillleult to know what to eelect.</p>
        <p>Some of the latest gowns are veritable dreams of delight. Listen to this description : The Prince.es robe of n-u-d!ow-tinted lace ftlliog in deep points over the under-dress o" white satin, which, where it sweeps outwards blow the knm-4, is encircled with wee Vandyked killings of white net elgod with lace. The lac turns back with wind revera from a transparent yoke, where a t.,ne.'n of black tulle and a Cluster o tmask roses are .introduced in deftest fashion, and then it is hell together at the waist with a beautiful buckle of white enamel and diamond:3.</p>
        <p>Your loving JEN NIB.</p>
        <p>FRUIT STONES. —Keep all fruit stones, cooked or uncooked. Dry them slowly in the oven, put in a large jar, and in winter throw a handful on the fire of an eveoing. They will crack for a moment, scud up a bright flAme, and fill the room with a delicious odour.</p>
        <p>PAINT AND PUTTY can bo taken off glass by wetting the glass several times with a strong solu- tion of soda. Wet the glass often with it till the spots soften and can be washed off, and then polish with alcohol.</p>
        <p>FIRE•IRONS.—To preserve bright grates or fire. irons from rust brush them over with linseed oil. Sprinkle over this some unslaked lime; by this simple means all the grates and hreirons in an empty house will be kept for months free from harm without further care or attention. • To WHITEN A DOOR•STEP.—This plan should suit many, as it will not wash off. Mix a little quicklime with half-a-pint of skim milk. Wash the step first, and then smear with this mixture.</p>
