      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>" WOMEN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>" WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>It is said that the King of Portugal will shortly pay a visit to this country in order to thank Queen Victoria for the recent visit of the British Fleet to Lisbon. There has always been considerable intimacy b?tween our Royal Family and the Court of Lisbon, owing to the fact that the late King Consort of Portugal (grandfather of the present King) was a first ceusin of the Queen and of Prince Albert, his Majesty having been the eldest son of Prince Ferdinand of Sae-Coburg Gotha. The Queen of Portugal is the eldest child of the late Comte de Paris, and the only Frenchwoman actually in possession of a throne. As Duchess of Braganza. tbe Princes soon won her way, by her simple, kindly ways, into the hearts of the Portuguese, and since the Dube succeeded his father, both he and his consort have set their whole energies to furthering the welfare of their people. I sojourn at the Portuguese Court means a sud- den return to the middle ages, and at first, the bright French Princess found the stately tedium of many of the Court ceremonies very wearing. But gradually she has become accustomed to her daily round of duties,and has modified to a certain extent the strict etiquette , which once obtained at Lisbon._ _</p>
        <p>There are several fine palaces in Lisbon, including the new Royal Palace,completed thirty-five years agn.which is a magnificent edifice, the Aguda on a hill overlooking Belem, two small palaces In Be'em ; the Bernposta,north of the city, now converted into a military school, and the palace of the Corte, or legislative chambers. The most striking and imposing buildings, howevir, are the vast and massive convents which crown the hill, and look like palaces and fortresses. The climate is variable, though on the whole healthy and general. It is very hot and dry in the Summer months, tut is re- lieved by North-west winds. Heavy rains fall in November and December, cold clear weather prevails in January, but in February the westber becomes mild again and the spring begins. Snow is a very rare occurrence.</p>
        <p>It has been remarked, and frequently of late, how many society men nowadays exhibit a bracelet on their left wrist. The Prince of Wales may often be seen set- ting an example in this direction, as may also WIC Duke of Saxe Coburg and the King of Italy. The late Duke of Albany, and the late Crown Prince Rudolph of AUF- tria. were never without a gold bangle on their left wriq,to which they were wont to attribute Pll the good luck that came in their way. The fashion, is of course, purely Continental in its origin, and is never likely to be extensively followed in these Isles of cure. Heppily, as it gives men an effeminate appearance which none of us appreciate. The only daughter of the late Crown Prince Rudolph of Austria, will next year appear at the great Court fes- tivities but ere her debut takes place, it is expected she will be betrothed to the Archduke Francis Ferdinand, her presumptive to the throne. The Archduchess has been most carefully educated, and is most charming, intellectual, stately, and beautiful girl. The Archduke Ferdinar,d is thirty-six years of ago, and enormously rich. Should he succeed to the throne however, a greet part of his wealth will pass to his brother, the Archduke Otto, whe is married to a daughter of Prince George of Saxony. The pastime of yachting is making remarkable prc- gross in the South of England,tea of the principal Silent clubs, omitting the Royal Yacht Squadron, boasting a total membership of 3,080. It has beeome quits the fashion among women to he the owner of a little white winged " flyer." Mrs. Oliphant, sister of Lord Gerrard often takes part in the " Ladies' Match " at Cowes. Lady Londonderry too is a very practical yachtswoman and the Duchess of Sutherland, Lady Ormonde, Lady de la Warr, Lady Hilda,and Lady Mary Keppel,Derothy Lady Cantelupe,and pretty Mrs. Alwyn Greville, are all devoted to yachting. General books by well-known people will be publshed in the course of the summer. Among them,the "Memoir of the Duchess of Teck," by Mr. Kinloch Cook ; Mr. Winston Churchill's novel "Bayrola," and Lady Currie's volume of poems, called "Betwixt two Sias." The ranks of authoressea are frequently recruited from the roll call of the peerage. To give a few instances, the Marchioness of Dufferin has written several books, the Ccuntcs3 a f Warwick has edited and prefaced " The Life or Soseph Arch," the Countess of Aberdeen has written " Tnrough Canada with a Kodak," the Countess of Munster is re- eponsible for some novels, and for a wierd work entitled " Ghostly Stories," and Lady Violet Greville is of course widely known as the centributor to endless journals. In some mysterious way, the report got about London recently that Lady Warwick was serving behind the counter of her shop in Bond-street. It even bound its way into the remotest corner of Suburbia, and for a day the establishment was besieged by women desirous of speaking with, and purchasing frem a real Counters. Lady Warwick frequently looks to see how her pet scheme is progressing, but has never yet interfered in the department alluded to. Moreover nothing is sold over a counter. Specimens are exhibited and orders taken, which are executed in due course. The work —which consists of the fieeet lingerie. dressmaking., and millinery—is carried out by girls hem the vil'ages around Lady Warwick's old home, Eason, in Essex. In the first named department only costly articles are ob- tainable. All is done by hand, and except for the pro ducts of the Irish convents, there is no place in Ur - British Isles, where such beautiful work is tanned ou e The whole scheme is conducted on business lines, and i t now highly successful. With the talance in hand La,dys Warwick intends starting a girls' model lodging house club.</p>
        <p>Last year, our capes could not be full eneUgh, at the edge, this season we have dina;Aised with fulness alto- gether, and in some of the latest models it is ahnoct an impossibility to raise one's arms,so tightly does the mat- erial fit around them when the care is fastened. 14 Vir garments of this description ate worn hoceever. The dainty little coat is preferred by all but the quite elderly and certainly it has the advantage of adding to the wearer's F youthful appearance. As much can scarcely be slid of the average cape. Hata and toqu,s are soft', pretty, and perishable. They are trimmed far the moq part with silken tette, a fabric that to some extent takes the place of chiffen. The flower of the moment is the hydrangia. It is to be seen in bright pink, blue, a brilliaht mauve, pure white, and pale green. Its charms are further enhanced by leaves such as in its natural state it never saw. There is no sign at all of the return of the bonnet, and there is very little doubt that as fashionable headgear. it is a lost quant ty. Muslin ever silk is a loud and leading note in the chorus of fashion, and the little women as well as the big are being arrayed therein. At all the important out- fitters fer children, the daintiest little muslin frocks em- broidered, and lace inrertioned, are bin g shown over pale blue, or pale pink, pale green or white slips. The slip is always detachable. It releases the muslin frock by the undoing of a button or two, and thereby permits of a pilgrimage to the laundry whenever desirable. Cuing YONDUE.—Take three eggs, three ounces of grated cheese,three ounces of butter, and a little pepper and salt. Break the eggs, teat the yolks lightly. mix the cheese with them, break the butter into small pieces aud add. Beat the whites to a stiff froth, stir them into the mixture, season with pepper and salt, and bake in a well-buttered tin. Serve immediately. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S PERMANENT BENEFIT BUILDING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S PERMANENT BENEFIT BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>THE 29m ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this society was held on Tues- day evening in tue Golborne-itreet schoolroom. There was an excellent attendance. Mr. E. Waliington took the chair on the proposition of Mr. HARDING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY (Mr. Peter Dalton) read the annual re- port which was as follows:—The managers have pleasure in laying the twenty-ninth annual statement of accounts before the shareholders. During the year, the sum of £1276 us 91 has been received as subscriptions, £1946 Els 9d for repayments and intereet, and £2O Os 7d en- trance fees, fines, Sex., making the total receipta, £3242 19s id. The working expenses being £72 53 3d. The withdrawals amount to £784 17s 10d and the sum et £1475 has been advanced to members. There are 205 subscribers holding 2601 shares. and 46 borrowers hold- ing 1851 shares, making 251 members holding 446 i shire. The bonuses tlais year amount to £258 2s 61 on the amount lent, of which sum £47 43 is divided as pro- fit, and £2lO 18, 61 carried to the prospective interest account. Under the Building Societies' Act, 1894, the accounts of all building societies are required to be made up in such a form and to contain eueh particulars as the Chief Registrar of Friendly Societies may, with the approval of the Secretary of State, direct. The form of account which has been pre- scribed by the Chief Registrar requires that the balance of capital remaining due upon the mortgage securities should be stated separately from the prospective interest thereon, in other words the balance ot the £B2 103 (the net amount advanced per £100) remaining unpaid is to be distinguished from the balance of the £l7 10s (the bonus charged) also remaining unpaid. The accounts of the society for the past year have been made up on the basis and work out as follows :—The total amount remaining owing upon mortgages £9,627 193 4d, of which only £8.565 3s 6d can be regarded as capital, the balance of £1,062 153 10d representing the proportionate amount of interest thereon, calculated for the number of years the several mortgages have still to run. The profits for the year,including £2B 14s from last account, after deducting working expenses, amount to £349 is. out of which the managers recommend a dividend of 4 per cent , which will take £333 Os 2d, leaving a balance of £1.6 6s 101, which they direct to be carried forward to next year's account. The whole of the managers re- tire. Messrs. Harding, Wallingten, Fenton, Isherwood, Bouchier,and Baxter are eligible and offer themselves for re-election. Six additional managers will be taken from the _register in rotation.</p>
        <p>The 'CHAIRMAN said he thought they could congratul- ate themselves on having had a very successful year. They were a comparitively small society—they did not do a vary Inge business—but they might flatter them- selves that w hat they did they did well. They had no risky speculations so that they bad no enormous profits and what was better no heavy losses. The money they bad advanced was en good property to persons who were anxious to obtain possession of their own homer; and keep in eider- On the average they had received £62 per week and with drawals £l5 per week so that abaut £77 per week passed through the hands of their manager and secretary which had cost them about £1 6s. 3.1 per week. They would see from the statements that their financial condition was thoroughly sound. Their liabilities were about £8,565 while their assetswere £9,627 ; so they had a ba'ance of £1,062. If their society were to be wouud isp they could pay more than 20s in the £, which a good many ctber societies could not do. The profit this year came to £358 2s Bd.—They were not allowed to take the whole of that for dividend by act of Parliament. Only £47 and the remainder was carried forward to be divided in eight successive years. They would be able to declare a dividend of 4 per cent,whieh was not so bad, and carry forward the balance of £l6 69 10.1 towards next year's interest account. Unfortue- ately, they had a large balance at Bank, nearly £5OO. Be bad great pleasure in premising that the report and balance sheet read be adeptsd. Mr. OREELY asked how they accounted for the in- creased expenditure in the management ? He thought it was £4 more than last year. The SECRETARY said they had spent £3 for new pass books and £1 55 01 for a new ledger. Mr. GRRELY seconded the proposition, which was unanimously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TUE DIVIDEND.</head>
        <p>TUE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>Mr. GREELY mlved that they pay a dividend of 4 per cent. They could not pay more. Mr ISHERWOOD secon&amp;d. He thought they our!ht to be very well satisfied so loOg as they cou:d ray 4 per cent. out of the earuir,gs safely. The proFositiou was carried. _ _ ELECTION OF MANAGERS, ETC.</p>
        <p>Mr. GRIMLY moved that the returiling manageri : Messrs. James Harding, E. Wallington, Edward Fenton, Robert Isherwood, J. W. Boucbier, and J. Baxter, be re- elected.</p>
        <p>Mr. BOWERS sacondEd, Mr. HOPE supported, and it was carried.</p>
        <p>On the pteposition of Mr. BOWERS, Mr. Robert Lets, and Mr. William Bodle, jun., were appointed surveyors. Mr. Bowers taking the place of Mr. J. E. Wright, who did not wish to be re-elected, and Mr. Bodle teing le- elected. Mr. A. W. Howard and Mr. George Armstrong were re-elected auditors, on the proposition of Mr. Greely seconded by Mr. Isherwood. _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN mild the managers had been allowed In years past is. for each meeting they atteLded and it had had a very good effect. Be remembered the time when it was a difficult matter to get the managers tl attend owing to I usiners. Owing to the institution of this is. fee the attendanco . had been very much better. Mr. GREILIX moved they ply the usual allowance. Mr. BOWERS, in seconding, said he believed they honestly earned it. The motion was unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>The meeting concluded witti thanks to the Chairman on the proposition of Mr.lsmanwoon.seconded by Mr. FRNVIN.</p>
      </div>
