      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The ordinary monthly meeting of this Council was held in the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. Tile Mayor (Alderman Wright) presided and there wre pres,nt : Aldermen Fairclough (deputy mayor), &amp;Mon, Roberts, Blackhurst,Hutchinson, Cluncillors Snaw cireen,Piump- ton, Smethurst, Luuutei, Evans, Edi3oo, Duncan, Bur- ton, James Evans, Tinnion, Joiley, Roc.,,ere, S!;iticock, Beaton, John Rooerts, F. R. Roberts, Bennett, with Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy clerk), Mr. Sutcliffa (assiitaut Surveyor), Mr. Haddock (gas engineer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ABSENTEES</head>
        <p>ABSENTEES</p>
        <p>Ms Town Clerk Borougn Surveyor, and B )rJugh Treasurer were absent on business in London connected With the Corporation Bi{l.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. MAGINN'S SHEDS AND STABLES.</p>
        <p>MORE LIVELY DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of tie Street Improvement and Lighting Committee, it wAs resolved that thero be ?eferied to arbitration the question of the amount of compensation to be paid to the Mersey Beidw:ead Company, Limited, for the purchase of their premises in S. James's-street in front of the prescribed building line.—An application having been received from Messrs. Maginn Brc.s , to roof over the space between the sheds and hoareino in Arpley-street, and agreeing to remove the same en three months' notice, and to pay an annual ackaow- ledgment so long as they were allowed to remain, it was resolved that the requcs`, ice granted on condition that the sheds and boarding,.: be taken down and re- moved within five years, the period allowed by Mr. John White for the t".mporary occUpation of the land. Alderman SUTTON. in moving the adoption of the Minutes. referred to the minute relating to the Mersey- Bedstead Company, and said the matter was still pend- ing, and before finally settling the qtvs'ion the Com- mittee were anxious to arrive at a settlement, which would be beneficial both to the company and the Coro Pooation. It was very desirous that the thing should be fairly dealt with, and they holyal to report something, before long. Referring to the minute relatiag to Mesers Magian Bros., he explained that the Committee had fully coneidered the application. and eventually thought it was wise to grant it. The Council would understanct that aithouJi they had power to refuse the erection of any building not kept in the defi,e I line. they had not Power to refuse such building being erected if kept in the proper line, and they had not power to demand the removal of the hoarding which now existed—as a hoard- ing. The Committee decided to concede a point with regard to the shed on condition that Messrs. Ma.inn undertook to remove the whole, including the hoarding, within five years. Alderman ROBERTS asked what power the Corpora tion had to enforce the condition that the sheds should be taken down in five years' time, provided Messrs. Magion could make an arrangement with the ground Landlord. Alderman SUTTON said they would have an under- taking from Messrs Magian that they will remove the buildine. Alderman ROBERTS How can you enforce it.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON : By virtue of the agreement. They had only got permission from Mr. White to allow these wooden buildings to remain up for fire years, and according to their lease they will be boiled to pull them down then. Beyond that we shill have full powers to remove them, as they would not be in accordance with the bylaws. The only question is whether we compel them to act in accordance with the bylaws by making them place the sheds in a line with the defined line of the street. or grant them permission to erect them as at present up to the hoarding, for five years, on condition that they remove them then. Alderman ROBERTS observed that that was not an answer to his question. What power had they to en- force the removal of these buildings at the end of five years provided Messre. Maginn and the ground landlord came to terms for a further extension of time ? He submitted that they had no power at all,and by this pro- position they were sanctioning the erection of buildings which were contrary to the bylaws in the hope of getting rid of them, a hope which was not justified by the position. He moved that the proposition lel referred back for further concideration of the Street improve- ment Committee. He had no objection if they had Sufficient power to deal with it at the end e f the time, but he was advised and believed that it was a purely voluntary matter which they could not enforce. They Were giving something for nothing. They were sanction- ing for the first time in his recollection a deliberate Violation of the bylaws on the prospective chance of getting something which they had not power to enforce. Councillor SHAW GREEN thought the Committee should have gone further that it did, and got the power to en- force a penalty. Then they wou'd be sufficiently pro- tected. Councillor TINNION seconded the amendment. Since the last meeting a great number of residents and pro- perty owners in St. Austin's Ward had presented the case to him very strongly, and objected to that portion of the town being spoiled—that was a mild word—by the erection of buildings of that kind, and used for the Purpose of which it was evident it was going to be used. The last meeting it was "carriage sheds !" now it was " carriage sheds and stables." The scheme was develop- ing. They were at first only going to sell horses; now it was cattle as well. It was a very undesirable thing to have these buildings erected there: Alderman Htercurtesort protested against the use to which the buildings were going to be put, It was unfair to the neighbourhood and Corporation. The Market; Committee had never sanctioned the sala of cattle there. _ . _</p>
        <p>Councillor LONGTON remarked that the committee had quite sufficient difficulty in dealing with the matter with- out being held responsible for the purpose to which these sheds were to be used. The committee had to do with the erection of the buildings, and they had given the matter most careful consideration, and done what they considered to be best. There was no member of the committee in favour of these buildings being erected or used at all. They did not sit there, howeversto carry out their individual feelings. They bad to administer the bylaws. (Hear. hear.) It was quite clear that if the committee's proposition were carried there would be a temporary breach of the bylaws. Why had the com- mittee consented to that? If consent were not given Messrs. Maginn would simply put the building back and leave a space of four or five feet between it and the hoarding. That would an on for fivo years, and at the end of which time Messrs. Maginn would probably make a fresh arrangement with the landlord, and the eyesore would continue. What the committee had done was to instruct the Town Clerk to draft an agreement which would ensure the building and hoarding being taken down at the end of five years. In regard to the ques- tion as to what power they would have to enforce the agreement, he thought the responsibility was entirely on the shoulders of the Town Clerk, who would never allow the Corporation to enter into an agreement which could not be enforced. _</p>
        <p>Councillor JAISES EVANS said that if the buildings were erected in conformity with the bylaws they had no Power to prevent them. Messrs. Maginn were not seek- itlV to override the committee. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS : Have we power to refuse 3 license for this place ? The MAYOR: I cannot answer the question at this point. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS thought the Street Improve- ment Committee had acted wiqely as far as they were Concerned, but if the Markets Ccmmitten had power to refuse the license it was another matter altogether. Councillor BURTON said the passing of the minutes wouldj constitute a violation of the bylaws of the borough. Councillor PLIMPTON said the ilea was that they Should erect a corrugated iron shed, where they had built a wooden one and propnsed to cover it outside with corrugated iron. He did not think they would be in accordance with the bylaws when they were finished. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON, in reply, said tfiere had been a great deal of unneceFelry talk. The question was very simple. It was whether they would grant Megsra. Maginn permission to extend the building a little by- pond the line if they agreed at the end of five yelre to remove the building and hearding. There was no neces- sity to make such a difficulty over the question and he was surprised that a man like Councillor lumpton Should not understand the matter better. Councillor PLIIMPTON : Ido understand it.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON said they had the authority of the Town Clerk and past experience to guide them. Why did they not say at once they did not agree to the pro- Pesal without so much talk? The minute would go back. Meagre. Maginn would simply erect, in accord- ance with the plans already passod, the buildings they Wanted to put up. He did not care twopence about the flatter.</p>
        <p>The amendment was then put,five (Messrs H. RobertE, Butcbinson, Tinnion' Burton, and Plumpton), voting for and eleven against.</p>
        <p>THE DELAYS IN STREET WIDENING. COUNCILLOR SMETHURST IS INDIGNANT, AND</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN ROBERTS MAKES A STARTLING EXPLANATION. THE CORPORATION AS A MILCH COW FOR PROFESSIONAL MEN.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST, referring to the minute having reference to the purchase of the premises of the Mersey Bedstead Company, said he wished he could raise a stern over this question similar to that raised over the last one. It was his intention to move that not another Penny should be spent in connection with street work Until they had Bridge-street, Horsemarket-street, and Buttermarket-street widened. They were simply the leelehine-stock of everyone who came into the town. and</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>ho was suprised that the Chairman of the Finanse Com- mittee would sit quietly by and see a resolution passed that they go to arbitration for the spending of thousands and thousands of pounds where it was not re- quired, and would not be for a long numher of years. They had it at the last meeting that the property at one of the most dangerous points in the town could not be polled down because they got about a hundred a year from it. In Bridge-street they were letting people take shops costing hundreds and hundreds of pouild3, and it was the very nest property they would have to buy, instead of going and getting a pryer sum and going bi for the whole thing. They were dealing with Liverpool- road, Suutsford-road, and Wilderspool-road, and allow- ing the four main streets in the centre of the town to hang fire. The Chairman of the Finance Committee was allowing them to go in for what would cost them thousands of pounds. Alderman PLIIMPTON : It is the Street Improvement Committee. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST added that he was very much tr_mpted to 111070 an amendment to the resolution. It had bc en a disgrace to keep the streets he had mentioned in such a state, They would never have trams if some- thing was not done. Councillor BENNETT synapathiz2d with Councillor Smethurst' a pardonable warmth with regard to the slow pregress of the widening of the four main through- fares. The piecemeal and higgledy-piggledy fashion in which the work was proceeded with was deserving of the strongest censure. But while they should centre Chief interest on these streets, they could not close their eyes to thespossibilities in other schemes ef importance to which they were committed. The firm in question had submitted plans for a considerable extension. Two Couocil had defined the line of street and saw that firm must set back 60 feet. They said that would interfere with their premises, and they would have to ask the Corporation to purchase them. The alternative to that was to allocv them to come forward and nullify all they had clone in regard to the widening of St. James'-read. They (the Corporation) could not allow that,fer it would mulct them in twice the amount they would have t 3 Rey at some future time. Alderman ROBERTS said be must protest against the language used by Councillors Smethuret and Benn-tt in regard to the widening of the main thoroughfares of the town. A little knowledge was a dangerous thing. If either of those two gentlemen had the slightest personal exeerienc s of the difficulties which beset the committee having charge of this matter, they would not have said a hundredth part of what they had said. He would tell them who was responsible for the delay complained of, the professional men of their town, and they ceuld take it in any way they liked. Proverbial were the delays of the law, but when it was a case of the law with the other professions combined it made it impossible to carry out improvements of this kind. If half a d ;zen business men had had the question to setle between themselves, it would have been settled long ago. But when professional men were brought in they made diffi- cMties fee the sake of exacting fees. Tney seemed to regard the Corporation as a sort of much cow. In try- ing to meet the tenants on the one hand and the land- lords en the other they played into the hands of profes- sional men. They were, however, no worse off than in Wher towns. The widening of Deansgate in Mancherter was not finished yet, mud it had been going on for 30 years. Even Bolton, which town according to Couucil- lor Smethurst might be a very paradise but which was a long way short of it—(laughter)—could furnish instances of delays write as bad if not worse in regard to publ:c improvements than anything which had occurred in Warrington. COIMeiIIOY SMETHURST : I deny his assertion. Alderman ROBERTS (continuing) said they as a com- mittee were accused as if they were personally respon- sible for theme delays, and as a member of the committee he was not going to sit down under it. He was not going to say who was responsible, bu he cou'd give individual instances which were little short of scandalous in reeard to the difficulties put in the way of the committee. They had tried and tried hard not to do anything which would be unfair to the tenants. They had no right to impose conditions which might ruin a man. (Hear, hear.) He had a great respect for Councillor Smethurst's opinions on many matters. He was a successful man of business, but he had made the most impracticable remarks that evening with regard to the street widening that he had ever heard him utter. .</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST : That is your opinion. Alderman ROBERTS: I say that without the slightest personal feeling. Contiguing. he asked, where was the wisdom of not negotiating for the widening of Knuts- fera-road when a favourable opportunity presented it- self, heeause they had difficulties in carrying out their scheme of widening Bridge-strest ? It was absurd to think of it and he was glad the Street Improvement Committee were taking the opportunity of purchasing property that some day must be purchased for a public improvement. He did not hold a brief for anyone ;as he was not connected with the Mersey Bedstead Company but if the Corporation allowed the Mersey Bedstead Company to build across their proposed line of stroet, and they had no compulsory power to prevent them, they would have to pay five, six or ten times more than it would cost now. (Hear, hear.) Councillor SMETHURST rose to reply. The MAYOR: I think you are out of order. Is it a personal explanation Councillor SMETHURST : It is.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Then make it. Councillor SMETHURST said that Alderman Roberts had said his remarks were unbusiness-like. Let him bf-gin at the beginning. (No, no, and laughter.) If the Cor- poration had had compulsory powers to acquire the premises 15 or 20 years ago they would have been pulled down now.</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN: It ia absolute ignorance that is the cause of Councillor Smethurst's remarks to- night. The MAYOR: I object. That is very wrong. Councillor SMETHURST rose to a point of order. He was not going to sit down while language like that was used. The MAYOR: You ought to withdraw the word Coun- cillor Shaw Green. Counnil'or SHAW GREEN: Well, shall I say his want of knowledge proved by what he has said. (Laughter.) The MAYOR: That won't do! (Laughter). Councillor SHAW GREEN: For a man to be ignorant— The MAYOR: I ask you ti withdraw. Councillor SMAW GREEN: I withdraw the word", but I will prove it was ignorance. The Maxon: I don't coneider that a withdrawal. Councillor SMETHURST : I will leave myself.in your hands, Mr. Mayor. I will not be browbeaten. Councillor SHAW GREEN: I unreservedly withdraw the word ignorant or ignorance. I don't want to brow- beat anybody. The MAYOR: I was surprised to hear it from you. Councillor SHAW GREEN: We have no power to go into any other part of Bridge-street beyond Ryland's- street. .</p>
        <p>Councillor SmornunsT : Why don't you get it? Councillor SHAW GREEN: In the Bill now before the House of Commons we are taking the powers. Coun- cillor Smethurst says we ought to have taken. Councillor SMETHURST : You should have done that 20 years ago. Councillor SHAW GREEN: How many of us were here 20 years ago. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: I must object to this. I was hoping we should have a short meeting to-night, Councillor SHAW GREEN : I am amazed at the want of acquaintance with one's duties exhibited by Council- lor Smethurst. (Laughter). We are anxious to do what he is talking about,anctl protest against the introduction of matter altogether immaterial to the issue before us. Alderman SUTTON: I am surprised people cannot keep good tempers. (Laughter). The minutes were then agreed to. THE MEDICAL OFFICER'S ANNUAL REPORT. A TRIBUTE TO DR. GORNALL'S SERVICES. Alderman SUTTON moved the confirmation of the Health Committee's mlnuies, and said it was due to their Medical Officer that they should acknowledle their indebtedness to him fur the trouble he had bestowed in the preparation of the annual report and far such valu- able information it contained. They had the gratifying statement thdt the trade in Warrington generally was good. The Alderman was proceeding to read the chief items in the report when several members of the Council objected. _</p>
        <p>Althrman SUTTON thought they should take some notice of the report.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: You are simply reading the report. Councillor TINNION : I protest. Alderman SUTTON: How do you know every mem- ber has read the report COUBCiIIOr TINNFON : I would snggest that you read it from beginning to end. (Lanahter.) Alderman SUTTON: I leave it to the Mayor to say whether I am in order. Councillor Timms : You are never in order. (Lsughter.) The MAYOR: It IS a very important report and you have a right to comment upon it. Alderman SUTTON (continuing) referred to the fact thst Dr. Gornall had completed his twenty-first year in their service. On such an auspicious occasion as that he thought they might congratulate Dr. Gornall and themselves that the health of the town during these 21 years had so manifestly improved and express the cope that they might have his services for at least another 21 years and that they Might be attended with similar success. (Hear, hear.) Alderman ROBERTS seconded the adoption of the minutes. He should like to corroborate Alderman Sutton as fully as it was possible both as to the importance of the report and the able manner it had been prepared. IHe din not wish to single Dr. Gornall out for special praise because he did not think there were many Cor-</p>
      </div>
