      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 9, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 9, 1899.</p>
        <p>porations with better staffs than theirs. They had not a more painstaking and conscientious officer than their Medics! Officer. His duties were arduous and extremely unpleasant, and involvedhim in work which hardly fell to the lot of other officials. He thought the council would join in the wish that his health might continue and the he might have the opportunity of serving them with such ability for many years to come. Counciltor BENNETT expressed his surprise that some members at the end of the table should obi ct to the very moderate wish of Alderman Sutton to rater to the report.</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. ROBERTS : We don't object to com- ment on the report, but the Alderman was re iding the report. Alderman SUTTON protested, and said he was doing nothing of the kind. _ _</p>
        <p>0011116110 r BENNETT asked on what ground the com- mittee were deferring the construction of the proposed smallpox hospital at H,pughton Green? Councillor PLIMPTON :_.Money. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT That might not to stand in the way of its construction. If a hospital were vital and nec,ssary he did not think the question of money ought to stand in the way. . _</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. ROBERTS said he should not allow any member to put upon his shoulders the regponsibiiity of having said or done anything to cast reflection on Dr. Gornall's work. It was an insult to Dr. Gornall and the members of the Council for Alderman Sutton to presume that they had not read the report and to pr,ceed to read it. When Alderman Sutton came to comment upen the report and mak9 those highly eulogistic remarks no one agreed with them more than he (Councillor Roberts) did. It was because some of the members wished Alderman Sutton to come to these comments that they resented the insut to their intelligence that they had not read the report. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON denied that he had said that the members had not read the report. Councillor BURTON referred to the nuisance arising from a manure heap near the canal at Sankey, and contended that so far as the committee and himself were cmcerned toey had done all they could to put an end to it.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Steps are being taken t 3 stop it. Councillor BURTON read extracts from the report of the MedicAl Offic.l:,.r of [Stealth showing that the nuirAnce wal reap ms.ble for several cases of illness in the dis- trict., and observed that if anyone was overtaken with fever in Livc-rpool-road, and it was traced to the manure nuisance, the patient would be entitled to compensation from the Corporation. Councillor HEaToN : It is outside the borough, and we are not responsible. Alderman Surnx said at the last meeting of the Health Committee they had the culprit before them, and he had undertaken to remove the nuisance as quickly as possible. THE PAVING OF STREETS AND BACK PASSAGES.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved the coefirmation of the minutes of the Paving aecl Sewerage Co.nlnittee, and said he en- tered his protest against the last paragraph in the report of the Med,cal Officer. wWch read : "Paving of streets and back passages. The Health Cemmittee must be tired of hearing about this, but again, wearioonae as the reiteration is to myself, must I point outlits importance, and lament the slow progress made in every direction. Meanwhile toe rapid extension of house building is leav- ing toe efforts of tne street makers far behind." If any committee of the Council had tried to do its duty it was the Paving and Sewerage Committee. (Bear, hear.) He stronglg resented the paragraph in question, and was surprised the committee had passed it. Coaecillor EIEa.ToN see,oncied the motion, and pointed out that great progress was being marls in the paving of streets. Dr. Gornall might have said what he had to ea/ in better language. The committee bad done more pav- ing during the past three years than had been done for the previous six or seven years. Councilor PLIIMPTONT drew attention to a back pas- sage which had not been attended to, although the ad- joining property had been built for 25 years. The MAYOR Why did you not bring the matter up before?</p>
        <p>Councillor PLIMPTON : I haw, done so many tim,s. The passne is 30 Fards long and 4 feet wide. Councillor TINNIox : Where is it? Councillor PLIMPTON : At the back of Boatock- street.</p>
        <p>A derrnan ROBERTS did not think there was any inten- tion on the part of the Medical Officer to reflect on the committee. The statement was only a comparative one and intende I for purposes of comparison. He (the speaker) admitted the large amount of work which bad been dote by the committee, especially during the last three or four years, and which did them every credit. What the doctor lamented—and without any intention to reflect on the committee—was the fact that the build lag of new property was in exces4 of the capabilities of theeommittee to keep p tee with it in regard to the mak- ing of back passages and streets. The c Jmniittee started with a great amount of work which had accumulated through the policy of the previous committee, and they had Eotfbeen able to overtake the arrears.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: lan verfe:tly satisfiel with the es. planation which has been given. but I did feel hurt when first I saw it. The minutes were carried. THE MARKETS COMMITTEE AND MESSRS. MAGINN.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Markets Committae it was resolved that the new premises in Arpley-street erected by Messrs. Maginn Bros., be appointed a place for the sale of horses and cattle for a peiod of six months from this date, subject to the requirements of any other Com- mittee being complied with. Alderman HUTCHINSON, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said he had to ask the Council to allow him to withdraw the words" and cattle" in the resolution referring to Messrs. Maginn Bros. He painted out that there were several public and private schools in the vicinity of Arpley-street, and most of the cattle would be brought to town daring the school dinner laour,when the children were either going from or returning to school. The sewerage of the district was also very indifferent, and if they had a cattle market at all let it be a central one near a railway siding. (Elear, hear.) What was being done was a sort ot forward movement against the Markets Committee. Aldermat! SUTTON: How did the word "cattle "get into the =awe</p>
        <p>Alderman HUTCHINSON : lam not resp maible. I move that the minutes bs confirmed with the excvtion of the reference to cattle.</p>
        <p>Councillor JOHN ROBERTS seconded, and said he was of opinion that the place which had been selected was tbe wrong one for the sale of cattle. The buildings in question ought never to have been erected there. Councillor SHAW GREEN at this point handed a minute book across the table showing that the minute in ques- tion was signed by Alderman Hutchinson and contained the words "and cattle." (Laughter.) Alderman HurcEnNson : It was not read over to me, and the word cattle was never mentioned. I will prom- ise you I will be more cautious in future. (Renewed laughter.)</p>
        <p>iiderm'an SUTTON You are responsible. (Laughter.) Councillor TrzsrrnoN moved as an amendment that the minutes be confirmed with the exception of the resolu- tion dealing with the premises in Arpley-street. He was glad to tind Aldgrman Hutchinson on the stool ot repent- ance, and he hoped he would strike out the whole of the reso'ution. (Hear, hear.) If the place was not suitable why grant a license for six months? Why inflict a nuisance on the people for that period ? This was not a sAntimental objection. He contended that the building was not suitable obe licensed. Some time in the future they would have the Police Station there,and would not want horses and cattle:strolling about the place. This was the thin end of the wedge. If they got permission to sell horses they would get permission to sell cattle. This was the time to kill the thing once for all. Councillor F. R. ROBERTS seconded the amendment. He did not see much difference between horses and cattle as far as the nuisance was concerned. It seemed a great shame if they could not have one portion of the borough free from a nuisance. He supposed that if there was one corner of the town free from stink it was Arpley- street. If the Markets Committee had no power to refuse the license it seemed to him that Messrs. Drlaginn were foolish to come to them at all. He would like to knew if the Corporation had power to refuse the license? Councillor TINNION : If we have power to limit the license to six months, we must have power to refuse it altogether. _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON said it was quite evident the Chair man was responsible to some extent for the resolution which he did not know had been passed and that the other members did not understand what they were pass- ing. (Lauehter.) The wisest plan to adopt would be to carry the amendment and let the matter go back for further consideration. (Hear, hear,) They should do as the Health Committee did before granting a license for a slaughter-house—ask the opinion of the Medical Officer whether the place was suitable or not for the purpose for which it was to be used. He hoped that the committee would thoroughly understand the next reso- lution they sent to the Council, and Viet when the chair- man's name was attached to the resolution it would be evidence that it had been properly considered and un- derstood. (Laughter.) Aldernaan HUTCHINSON then withdrew the minute in question entirely, and the other minutes were c on- firmed.</p>
        <p>THE FEATHER'S HOTEL AGAIN. THE SUGGESTED DEPUTATION TO THE MAGISTRATES. At a meeting of the above committee the Town Clerk submitted a report on the land available and the steps to be taken for the rebuilding of the Feather's Hotel, in Midge-street. After consideration thereof, it was re- solved, that the magi3lrates be requested to receive a deputation to confer on the subjen. It was resolved</p>
        <p>OLD FALSE TEEM BOUUELT. Many ladies and gentlemen have by them old or die used false teeth, which might as well be Lamed into money. Messrs. R. D. &amp;J. H. Fraser,of Princes-street Ipswich, established since 1833, buy old false teeth. If you send your teeth to them they will remit you by return post the utmost value ; or, if preferred, they will make you the best offer, and hold the teeth over for your reply. If reference necessary, pply tolMessrs. Bacon &amp; Co., Bankers, Ipswich.—Advt.</p>
        <p>that the plan of the proposed footbridge from Slutcher's• lane new bridge to Wilson Patten-street be approved, and that the Town Clerk enter into an agreement with the London and North-Western Railway Company,on the terms indicated in the letter from the company's en- gineer ; and that the terms submitted for the completion of the new road from Slutcher's-lane new bridge across Arplcy Meadows be agreed to ; and that the contract for the purchase of the land in Museum street be approved,</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved and Alderman SUTTON ie-conded the adoption of the minuteg.</p>
        <p>Councillor F. R. ROBERTS referrei to the resolution respecting the Feather's Hotel, and contended that the committee was asking the Council to do an illegal thing. He had never heard of magistrates conferring with one side respecting the transfer of a license. It seemed to him that if an appeal were made to a superior court an arrangement come to under such circumstances would not be consideled a leaal one. He felt that the course suggested was an improper and a most illegal thing to do.</p>
        <p>Councillor EmasoN said that having once refused to transfer the license it was out of place on the part of the Corp)ration to send a deputation to the magistrates. Councillor TINNION said that at the la:it meeting of the General Purposes C3minittee he stronaly objected to the resolution, and he also declined to be one of the de- putation. He protested against the passing of the reso- lution, and they were inviting, if not a snub, something approaching it unless the magistrates were different to what he thought they were. Alderman SUTTON admitted there was a great deal of force in what Councillor F. R. Roberts had said with re- ference to the resolution. He could agres with him sometimes. (Laughter.) it would, in his opinion, be very inadvisable and illegal for a deputation to approach the magistrates on the qw,stion. The better course would be to allow the application to be made to the magistrates in the usual way. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BENNETT thought there was a good deal in what had been saki. The course suggested was open to criticism, and let them be like Ceesar's wife—above suspicion. He did not wish it to be thought that they had endeavoured to influence the magistrates in any way. At the last meeting it was suggst,4 that some steps should be taken to congratulate Her Majesty on at- taining her 80th birthday. The committee did not see their way to a public celebration of the event, and he thought it would be fitting if they passed smne sort of a resolution expressing their cordial congratulations to Her Majesty and hoping she might be spared for many years to come.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: lam with you. Alderman ROBERTS su4gested to the Mayor that he might see his way to with lraw the resolution relating to the Feathers Hotel. From the remarks which had been made he thought it would be more in consonance with the feeling of the Corporation to let the matter go to the magistrates in the ordinary way and not attempt to do anything which might seem improper. The MAYOR; lam perfectly willing. I have not the slightest feeling in the matter. The reiolutioe having been withdrawn the minutes were pass,d. THE COUNCIL SINGS THE NATIONAL ANTHEM. Councillor BENNETT then proposed a motion in accord. anee with the suggestion he had made, respecting the Queen's 80. h birthday. There was no necessity for him to make a speech. The Town Clerk or the Deputy Town Clerk could draft it, and forward it to the Queen. Alderman ROBERTS in seconding the resolution said Her Majesty had no morelloyal supporters than the mem- bers of the party to which he had the honour to belong. They appreciated more fully than they ever did before the fact that they had lived under the most beneficent reign in the history of their country. (year. hear). The resolution was unanimously carried. Councillor SMETHURST then led off with the singing of "God save the Queen," and the members joined in heartily singing the first verse, after which three cheers were given for Her Majesty and for the rest of the Royal Family, on the initiative of the Mayor. The Council then dissembled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>HE PUTS IT IN THE BAG.</head>
        <p>HE PUTS IT IN THE BAG.</p>
        <p>That'd what the post-office clerk does with your let- ter ; he puts it in the bag. And that's what you do with every mouthful you. swallow, solid or fluid;, you put it in the bag. In the latter case the bag is the stomach. It is shaped like a pear, with a bent and longish neck. Things go in at the top of the neck, then slide down into the bigger part. The sides of this bag are smooth, and if you don't stuff it too full, the =tents roll and tumble about in it without any trouble.</p>
        <p>And this operation begins directly your meal arrives. All over the inside surface of the bag are no end of little pipes, and as soon as the meat, potatoes, gooseberry pie, &amp;c., are all in these pipes (pardon me the word), commence to squirt a fluid all over the collection, and keep it up s 3 long as the rolling and tumbling about continues. Nature wants to make a mixture of the lot, and that is how she does it.</p>
        <p>When the job is done your weal is turned into a thick, gray-looking soup or broth, which speedily slips through the door or valve at the lower end of the bag, and goes on its road. - .</p>
        <p>Till's is the process when all is right. But suppose otherwise. Then, what? For, unlike the leather bags hich the clerks flip our letters into, the stomach is a lire bag and has fashions and feelings of its own. The mail big accepts anything, from a lover's note to a package of news papers, without a pout or a pang. But with the live bag it is another story, as Mrs. Hunt's experience shows. She says that for a long time everything she ate acted as if it had turned sour on her stomach. And that is precisely what it did. For some reason the live bag failed to turn the food into a broth- like mixture, but left it in the form it had been received. So it lay in the bag and went sour, just as it would have done in a bucket out in the garden.</p>
        <p>The lady finally got over this trouble, but not for a weary while ; and we will ask her how she found hersalf in the rnlantime,</p>
        <p>"I was low, weak, and heavy," ske answers, " and had no energy. I seldom felt like eating, even though faint from want of nourishment, and what I did eat lay on me like a weight." Yes, for the reason that the stomacn was not dealing with it. The stomalh was like a dog asleep in a corner with a sleeping child using him for a pillow. For the time being the live bag was a leather bag—no stir or disposition to attend to business about it. " I had an almost constant gnawing pain," Mrs. Hunt says, "and often fancied I should like to eat something; but when it was placed before me I couldn't touch it. I was much distressed with belching up a foul fluid ; and a nasty wind rose into my throat as if it would choke me.</p>
        <p>"It wasn't much that I slept, and in the morning and throughout the day I was exhausted, worn out, and practically good for nothing. With but little variation this was the manner of my life one year after another. " In November, 1894, I was so weak the doctor ordered me to ke,p my bed—an order sadly for me to ob?y. However, I usually managed to crawl out of bed and lie on the couch part of the day. I was very pale and emaciated, and for three months I scarcely had strength to move. " One day in February, 1895, I read statements made by different persons who told how they bad been cured of this very same complaint by Mother Seigel' s Syrup. As their cases were like mine, I said to myself I would try the medicine, fk4r the doctors were no able to do me any good.</p>
        <p>"I am glad to tell you that the Syrup proved to be as helpful to me as it had been to others ; for after using it a few weeks I could eat without any of the suffering I have tried to describe. My strength came back with my eating regular meals,and presently health the most precious of blessings—was mine once more. What my opinion of Mother Seigel's Syrup is anyone can judge."—(Signed) (Mrs.) H. HUNT, 4, Southern - road, 'Mame, Oxfordshire, February 1, 199. The old complaint, chronic indigestion, cured again by the remedy (perhaps the only remedy) that can be depended on to do it. Watch out for the welfare of the stomach, and keep the Syrup in the cupboard.</p>
