        <p>ANA ECONOMICALLY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>OPEN AIR FETE AND BAZAAR. THE CYCLE GYMEANI.</head>
        <p>OPEN AIR FETE AND BAZAAR. THE CYCLE GYMEANI.</p>
        <p>Brilliant weather on Satu;dav morning gave promise of an ideal day for the clnelusion of the Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club les:,e and the promise was full- filled in all respects. There was a large attendance and the sun was shining with great power when the opening ceremony was performed shortly before three o'clock in the bazaar tent.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR expressed the pleasure it afforded him t 3 be present. There were, he said, gentlemen connected with the club who were worthier ot the honour which had been conferred upon him than he was, but he sup- posed he had been asked to attend because he happened to be the Mayor of the town. He hoped Success would attend the efforts of the committee and that more thin £260 (Friday's proceeds) would be obtained that day. There were some people who seemed ti think that the bazaar Was being held as it were to help themselves as members. That was not so. and he felt strongly that they owei a deep debt of gratitude to the promoters be- cause they we:e not simply working for themselves but for thse who would succeed them, and thus have an open space for ever. In that respect a public wok was being done, and he was therefore pleased to be present to assist in the effort which was being made. He had much pleasure in declaring the ate open. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN DEWHITHST, in proposiog a vote oi 'thanks to hi i Worship, said he (the speaker) had been connec- ted with the club longer than he liked to own-36 or 37 years—and he had partaken of all its benefits, first as a cricketer, then a bowler and as something else after— (laughter)—and he must say that he never begrudgel any subscription he had paid to the club. If they paid two guineas instead of one, the recreation they got would be cheap. (Hear, hear.) Orie,inally this was a cricket club pure and simple. The ex-Mayor told them yester- day of the happy moments which bad been spent on the Bank Hall ground, and his mouth began to water when he spoke of the luscious grapes Lord Wintnarlei,ta used to send them while they were crickating. He was one of those who partook of teem. Now a departure had been made, and they had the fair daughters of Warring- ton with them at that beautiful enclosure. Naturally, as the town grew, they thought it advisable to secure that piece of land, on which they had spent £l,OOO or £2,000, en they did not, therefore, wish to lose it. It was a ground they loved dearly, and they wished to buy it.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. RICHARDS)N seconded the motion, and re- marked that a great deal of the success which had been achieved was due to the Mayor and others who had taken great interest in the scheme. Mr. Dewhurst had referred t) some observations which were made by Alder- man Fairclough on the precfdmg day, and he (the speaker) weuld like to refer to what Councillor Pierpoint bad said on that occmion, for a sum of £350 was owing to the bankers. That was a deb` they wished to wipe off, and he hoped that, as the result of the two days proceedings, they would be able to d 3 so and hvve som e thing left. (Hear, hear, and applause.) The motion was carriel, and the Mayor having re- turned thanks the sales were renewed, FANCY DRESS CYCLE PARADE.</p>
        <p>In the early afternoun a fancy dress cycle parade took place. The assembly was made at the head quarters of the Warrington Cycling Club, the Patten Arms Hotel, where a great crowd of people congregete 1 to witness the start. Beautiful weather favoured the cemplete suc- cess of the parade, which,though net numerically strong, ' was exceptionally picturesque. About 100 took part, including 40 members of the 'Warrington Club and re- presentatives from Widnes, Earlestown and Altrincham. Upon the formation of the procession a move was made to the front of the Town Hail, where the parade was organized. The procession was headed by three charac- ters on horseback. Messrs A Clay (Indian), J Leee(cow- boy), and Frank Croesley (Russian). They were fol. lowed by the Borough Band, and next came several members of the Orford Barracks Cycling Club. The ' cyclists in character costumes followed, and the rear of the procession wee brought up by a few mem- bers of the Volunteer Cycling Corp.. The route taken to the cricket ground was Sankey- street, Buttermarket-street, Scotland-road, Horse market-street, and Bridge-street. The streets were lined with spectators, and the parade wae wit- nessed with great interest and seemed to afford general satisfaction. Many of the characters were exceedingly good, and evidently the utmost pains had been taken by all the cyclists to make the turnout a creditable one. Among the characters who attracted the greatest atten- tion were a Japanese (PA Morris), Chinaman (E Picka- vance), Ally Sloper (A J Mottram), Neptune (Captain Waring), nurse (J Sullen), new woman (Percy Glynn). Captain Kettle (J Whitehouse), and Zingari (Miss M Baker), There were also several in courtiers' costumes, whose turn-outs were much admired. Others worth mentioning were Lord Kitchener, " Quaker Oats," "Tit Bits," and a broken down character on an ancient machine, labelled " 'cot tad for an old 'un." Upon their arrival at the Cricket Ground the cyclists marched round the field several times, and then drew up in the centre for inspection by the judges —Messrs A W Phillips (Southport) and B White (Liverpool). Toe adjudica- tions were as follows :—Neatest turnout (lady or gent) : 1, Victor G Roberts (Charles I.) ; 2, F A Morris (" Mikado ") ; 3, E Piekavance (Chinaman). Most humorous get-up ; 1, A J Mottrane (Ally Slope.* 2 Percy Glynn (new woman) ; best decorated machine (lady or gent) : 1, J F Sampson (Duke of Buckingham) ; 2. George Podmore. (Louis XIV) ; beet turnout : (boy) J Whitehouse (Captain Kettle) ; (girl,) Miss Baker (Zingari) ; (tandem) Messrs Bellian and Smith (cour- tiers) ; Secretary of Club with largest number of mem- bers on parade (Warringten Cycling Club excluclei), let I South Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>SPORTS.</head>
        <p>THE SPORTS.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon a hand-ball match, untier the directioa of Mr. J. Kilasley, was played bA9reen the St. Helens Gymnasium and Warrington Gymnasium, and created a great deal of interest. The teams, however, were not evenly matched, as the score for the first 20 minutes was Warrington seven, St. Helens nil. War- rington added two more goals in the sec and half, their opponents still failing to score. With reference to the cycle: gymkana and sports, the first event was a place kick (Rugby). Each competitor had to place his own ball, and was allowed two kicks. The competiter who kicked the longest distance from the place where the ball rested to the spot where it first touched the ground, had to ta:ke the prize.which was won by C Ashton. The Asqociation place kick was won by W Broughall. The winners of the bicycle slow race were :-1, F Allen ; 2, McCready ; and the egg and spoon cycle race, 1, Anderton ; 2,Broug- hall. There was plenty to occupy the attention of the visitors in addition to the sports, including coacerts,the performances of the Borough Band, the freaks, the shooting gallery, and " Aunt Sally." The sports officAals were :—Judges, Messrs John Evans and David Tinnion; clerks of course, Messrs J W Bibby and H H Evans ; stewards, Messrs G H Smith, E H Clare, W Litton, W Brown, C H Wood, W H Bellian, J Podmore', jun•, J Davenport, T J Hulse, A Booth, E Piclravance, jun., and David Davies.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the sports the priz,s were dis- tributed by the Mayoress, to whom, on the motion of Alderman FAIRCL01:1011, seconded by Mr. R. T. FAlR- cLoucar, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded. The MAYOR replied on behalf of Mrs. Wright, who, he said,liad taken a deep interest in all the work in which he had been engaged during his period of office. She did her best to help him to discharge his duties, and he was sure she was also deeply interested in the pnceediags at the cricket field. They hoped a substan:lal sum would be realised. (&amp;pplause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>TETE RECEIPTS.</head>
        <p>TETE RECEIPTS.</p>
        <p>The reneipts wen ag folio :—Friday, £260 ; Satur- day, £220; total, £4BO.</p>
        <p>D[SAPPEARANCE OF A ST. HELENS CHILD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>KIDNAPPED OR WHAT ? APPLICATION IN CHANCERY.</head>
        <p>KIDNAPPED OR WHAT ? APPLICATION IN CHANCERY.</p>
        <p>At the Chancery Court of Linc:ashire, sitting at S. George's Hall, Liverpool, on Monday, before Vice- Chancellor Hall, a remarkable case was mentioned, which arises out of the will of a Sc. Helens tradesman. It appears that William Swire, a clog-ironmaker, of Sc. Helens, died some time ago, leaving his estate to be equally divided among his children. One of the chil- dren, Sarah Swire, had mysteriously disappeared prior to the father's death, and had not been heard of since. The application now made to the court by Mr. Harold Mather on behalf of the trustees was whether this child should be presumed to be dead. Mr. Mather explained that in the month of July 1876, the child, who at that time was eight years old, and lived with the parents in Haydock-street,St.Helens, mysteriously disappeared. She was last seen in com- pany with another child in an adjaining street. Her parents made every inquiry, assisted by the police, who issued notices, and advertisements were inserted in the local papers, but no clue as to her whereabouts was ever found. Her father died in 1881, leaving his estate to be divided amongst his children. The question now arose whether the missing child was to be presumed to be dead.</p>
        <p>The Vice-Chancellor thought the child must have been kidnapped, or in same way done away with. Had she been drlwned in the canal or any water near St. Helens the body would have been recovered. The circumstances were very strange. He would order an inquiry in Chain- berg on the subject, and adverticements to be issued ,and would adjourn the rest of the petition until this bad been done.</p>
        <p>Mr. Roger Lawrence appeared for other parties inter- ested.</p>
        <p>It is announced that the price to be plid by G-ermany to Spain for the Carolines and other islands is about a million sterling. According to' Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the number of failures in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending June 3rd, was 73. The number in the correspond- ing week of last year was 61 showing an increase of 17. being a net increase in 1899, to date, of S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>179 Majority for Rainhill, 1.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>4.*4</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESIIIRE COUNTY ASSOCIATION. SZNIOR COMPRI/I'lON —THIRD ROUND. WARRINGTON NORTII V. WASIRINGT3N SOUTH.—That the meeting of these teams in the above competition has lost none of the keeness which has invariably characterised it in previous I?year z was amply proved on Saturday, when they met on the green of the Wigan Subv.ription Club, quite a large number of W,lrringtorilans joorneying to view the encounter. Splendid weather favo ire.' the proceedings, and the otßtials of the Wigan club are to be complimented' on the condition of the turf, for it was so rine that it pot the trundlera to a thorough test of their merits. The first four games resulted in the South gaining a lead of seven points. G Ball and H Fogg played an excitiug gam-, the contest extending over 28 ends leach player in turn took the lead, until at the lath end Ball, with joilk ie hand, scored a pan*, and won by three shots. J Gleay) and A J Jones were a stout pair of opponent), for ater the first- named bad seen the score read 14•8 in his favour Jones came again and at the 22nd end had reduced Gleave's lead to one (16-15). From this point, however, he had not another ()twice, for his rival took possession of the block, and ran out a winner by six. Two such trundlers as F Berry and T Balton were sure to nrove an interesting pair, and after it had looked the proverbial guineas to gooseberries on Bolton winning Berry played a roug sequence of grand wools and won by two D Doherty again justified his selec- tion for the North, retoining the lead 4fter five ends had been played, and winnins at the close be four points. The next n qvuearrtlt ot etßteogelnei. l-d vitthhe later oawgiy aieirlin a sensational victory o h 433 always be one of the most reliable of the North players, but on Saturday he found H;Shaw at his best From the first wood de:ivered Shaw took poaression of the tack, and though Bothwell Seared at an occasional end, it WAS a severe blow to his side when at the 19th eid the game was called 21-5 in Shaw's favour. A bril- liant exhibition of the trundlini, art was sh we., by R Ander- ton and T Hackett, and the intl.-3 was in the bolauce through- out the 29 ends over which the frame exten.led. Hackett at one period enjoyed a substantial lead, but the North skipper displayed a pertinacity which htd a juSt reward. Hackett is also deserving of praise for his brilliant play. J Swanton ac- counted for T Cooper in decisive style, early on taking a lead, bat the North player peeled with his opponeat at the ninth end only to see Swanton regain the lead, and win by seven points, It was a ding-dong battle leetween T. Bridge and C. Littlewood, and the gams which extended over 31 ends, went in favour of Littlewood by four shots. Thus at the end of the second series, the South.' enjoyed a lead of 25. A regu- lar eye-opener awaited the Soute team when .liermon Wil- hams gave R. Fairorother such a suparn try •iismissal. The former bowled splendidly, and at no particular patio I had Fairbrether a chance against his more youthful opponent. IThe majority of 15 speaks for itself. The longest game of the afternoon, that between J. Allison and T. Davies, went in favour of the latter, though the ex-champion appeared to have the game in hand at the 31st end, when, with jack in band, the score read 20-18. Davies, however, notched two, and maintaining the advantage won by a single point. J. Straw defeated W. Wood by 6 points, the issue of the game rarely being in doubt, and the concluding tie saw T. Locker defeat J. Ashton by two points, after an exciting game, Locker at one period held a command- ing lead, but Ashton play d pluckily and peeled with his opponeat at 19. Locker secured the jock at this point, and won as stated. It will be seen that the South wan eight games out of the twelve played. The bowling was of a high order throughout the afternoon. and the whole of the games were contested in a good spirit. Scores t wA.RRINGTON SOUTH. WARRINGTON NORTH. H Fogg J (Heave F Berry 11 Gibson Shia~ T Hackett 2 B Anderton I Swanton 2l T Cooeer l4 C Littlewold R Fairbrother T Davies... .. J Shaw T Locker 2l J. Ashton l9</p>
        <p>229 20a Mijority for the South. 21. HANDICAP AT onopr. On Saturday afternoon a handicap took place on the bowl. ing green at the General &amp;Hat Hotel. when Mr R G Green, the proprietor, gave £2, to which was adldd an entrance fee of 6d eath, divided into nine prizes. There were 31 entries The han licippars were Messrs II Hankinson and If Shaw Croft; C Unsworth. Nowchurch and a Raves, Warrington The game was eleven up. and limits given three. The green was in fair condition, and some excellent bowling took blace. The following were prize winners : 1, 255, S Taylor, Croft; 2, lbs. W Green, Croft; 3,7 s, T Houghton, Croft; 4,4 s. and 5,2 s, divided between It '.3aunderson, Croft, and J Daintith, Leigh. The following a'so received prizes : p Unsworth and H Hankinson, Croft ; U Eaves, Warrington. and T Rdgington, Winwick.</p>
        <p>CROFT V. FEA.RNILRAD (ORDER OF DRUIDS). Played on Saturday afternoon on the bowling green at the Joiner's Arms Hotel, Croft, between ten members of the lodge of the Order of Druids held at the aixove hotel and ten members of the lodge of the same order held at the Farmer's Arms Inn, Fearnhead. The game was 15 up, and the green being in good condition some excellent bowling took place. On account of one of the Fearnhead members being absent only nine members belonging to each lodge played, end the Croft lodge proved the victors by 27 points. At half-past six o'clock the teams sat down to a knite-aud-fork tea,after which bowling was resumed until about 8.30 p.m.. Wilf.n the Fearn. head members returned home, The following are the scores :</p>
        <p>CROFT. FR/RNEIEAD. S Street J Maines 3 F Young 15 T Fazakerley R Pooley P Unswortvt R raylor ... l5 J Harrison 2 B Hope D Fazakerley J Houghton J Taylor — R Johnson * — —. 118 91</p>
        <p>BARLBSTOWN v. RAINHILL. The first fixture of the season was played at Barlestown on Saturday afternoon. From the result it will be seen that the game was keeniy contested, Rainhill winning by the narrow majority of 1. The teams afterwards partook of tea at the Wellington Hotel- Scores :</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN. RAINHILL. T Mather ............ ............ 15 A Travis J Barlow l5 W Mawdsley l4 B Ashcroft 8 R Prescott ... 15 J Bedworth 3 Savage l5 T Parker 5 8 Thompson l5 W Lowe J Spencer 7 W ii Duxbury J Clough A James l3 John Ball I, itothwel G Arnold ................H J Yates l5 Joseph Hall 5 B Platt F Mottram 4 John Shacklady I Boon.. S Warburton l5 3 Lyon. It Anderton ..</p>
        <p>MUSIC IN OPEN SPACES.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening several bands of music played on the open spaces in different parts of the town. At Rowley there was a particularly large attendance when an excellent selection of music was gone through by the League of the Cross Band. The performance was much enjoyed, and its cue- cees will no doubt lead to similar performances:be-. lag given during the summer months. J. BAYLEY Isle° offers SPECIAL TERMS to SOHOOLS for his splendid PORK PIES for Walking Day.—Advt.</p>
        <p>DARESBITRY SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION. The triennial election of seven members to serve on the Dare:bury United District School Board took plc eon Smnrday, nine persons contesting. There were two polling stations, at Moore School and Daresbury S?hool, the polling taking place from twelve noon to eight p.m. The voters from Moore, Acton Grange, and Higher Walton polled at Moore, while those from Daresbury, Preston Brook,Hatton, Newton and K ckwick, recorded their votes at Daresbury. Several conveyances were early astir carrying the voters, the busiest time taking place from six to eight. The counting took place at Dares- bury Schoolond a little after nine,Mr. G. F. Ashton (returning officer) declared the following to be the result :—Mr C W Phillips, 447; Mr John Cooper,4l3; Mr Peter Tickle, 297; Mr Richard 1.4 Warburton, 296; Mr Thomas Billington, 220; Rev H T Clark (vicar), 174 W Mr Thomas J Weir, 137; Mr John Highfield, 125; Mr Pt.,ter Gleave, 29 . Miss B Dutton who had Iwithdrawn from the contest, ten votes. The first seven ;:.,entlenrui were declar3d duly elected,Messrs. , Warburton, Billington and Weir being new mem- -1 bers.</p>
