      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EVERY MAN</head>
        <p>EVERY MAN</p>
        <p>SUFFERING from Ner—ous and Physical Debility should send for a valua 'e pamphlet explaining how all nervous and organic arrangements may be successfully treated without stomach medication. The method is easy and pleasant. and will effect a perfect and permanent sure. 802 reeled, post free. Address— B. NORTON. 59, &amp; 60 Chancery Lane, London, W.O Betsblisbed 30 Yam.</p>
        <p>THOUGH it may be regarded The Political Position of Women. as a victory, the vote on</p>
        <p>IMr. COURTNEY'S motion, making sex and marriage no ineligibility for the position of Aldermen and Councillors in the London boroughs, in reality unsettles matters and renders it more than ever necessary to seek some logical basis and dividing line. Unless the House of Lords should strike out the words inserted, the admission of which is declared to be a fluke,owing to the unexpected way in which the Finance Bill was postponed,there will be two anomalies at once. There will be no women on the Council of the City of London, and there will be no women on the city and municipal councils of the rest of the kingdom. Agitation against these exceptions is sure to be almost immediately raised, and the political battle will be continued with more heat and emphasis than ever. Sir HENRY FOWLER seemed, in one part of his remarks,to be about to carry the distinction he had made to some logical conclusion. He endeavoured to make a distinction between legislative and administrative work, and he contended that women had displayed excellent qualities in the latter, on Boards of Guardians and School Boards. It must be admitted that women have done well as Guardians, and that a few of them have been of service upon School Boards. It can hardly be said, as yet, that they have been very distinguished in the work of the London Vestries, which is in some respects the same kind of work they will have to do on municipal cauncils. School Boards and Boards of Guardians have very little else to do besides actual administration. They are relieved from subjects which are classified under the head of law and or der. Is it impossible to make some distinction, after the fashion Sir a FOWLER essayed, which shall group the subjects which women shall be allowed to take in hand? Some of the provincial councils have employed women as health inspectors, and the Home Office had set the example long before of entrusting them with special factory inspection. Be- cause this work has been done well the great boroughs out of Landon have not been moved to take any decided action towards making women aldermen and councillors. There seems to be an approach towardi a dividing line on the side of the men, though the more active political women are intent upon destroying the dividing line of se ;.- altogether. L; is clear that the latter is not in accord with public opinion, and that we are far from reaching to any point neer- to it at present. Until we can reach some dis- tinction the whole subject will remain in a° anomalous position. It might be useful to ;all</p>
        <p>ME thE asE</p>
        <p>kr lui fai ot</p>
        <p>lif ti] ac</p>
        <p>fc</p>
        <p>that where women have the franchise they shall become entitled to everything that grows cut of it. They have the municipal franchise, and there- fore they thould have the chance of becoming mayors, aldermen, and councillors. But they have nob the Parliamentary franchise, and therefore the distinction between legislative and adminis- trative can be maintained. It hardly seems that We can go further than this, and even this appears too far, because it involves magisterial functions and questions of police in most cases. If these questions are to become barriers, the eligibility of Women for municipal councils generally is at once arrested.</p>
        <p>THERE is a distinct tendency Accidents and Compensation. of accidents to increase where compensation has come into force. It was pointed out, when the Act was being passed, that this was the case in Germany ; but the promoters of the measure were scoffing and sceptical. The figures now published shew that in this country—not generally, but in certain occupations—there has been an increase of fatal accidents. The HOME SECRETARY admits the fact, and seeks to explain it away. The improvement in trade always means the introduction of the less skilled into certain occupations. If this were so, it ought to be possible to show that there has been an increase, and that, if there are more accidents, the proportion to men employed has not materially increased. This demonstration has not yet been raade, and until it is complete we cannot accept the HOME SECRETARY'S explanation with perfect assurance, and we shall be driven to find some other method of accounting for the increased accidents. It must be sought for in he operation of three causes. Either the men must be more careless, or the masters are, or the money already spent on compensation has taken away the means for improvements in guards and fencing and the prevention of accidents. The first Point seams almost as unlikely as the second. Why should a workman be more careless because he knows that if injured or killed there will be Compensation for himself or his wife and family ? Why should the master be indifferent when he knows that he may have ta pay an annuity, or a lump sum, or a compensation to the wife and family of a killed ernploy6 ? These questions raise others, and we hardly ,seem able to arrive at any satisfactory conclusion upon either of them. Self- interest would seem to animate the two classes to avoid accidents, and if it does not de so there must be some other conditions to which our atten- tion has not been called, some psychological state Which cannot be inferred from statistics. Does workman intend to be incapacitated by some injury and get a pension, and risk his life and lose it in the process ? Does a master think that carelessness will cure itself if fatal accidents occur, and so the number will be re- duced? Prevention has always figured in Liberal arguments as being quite as important as com- pensation, though we are not aware that it has ever been regarded as more costly. Were it more costly the matter would le simplified. But how Many checks and fencings would one or two com- pensation sums for fatal accidents provide? Surely it is less costly to protect machinery, to protect men in doing certain things, to adopt automatic contrivances, than to have to pay hun- dreds of pounds in paying to widows and orphans? If the possibility of compensation may make the workman more careless, we cannot understand Why it should make the ultimate payer of it more callous. The whole thing seems somewhat of a puzzle, and perhaps the better plan is to leave it for the present. But if compensation permanently increases fatal accidents a very serious state of things will have arien, for which there will have to be found a remedy.</p>
        <p>The Committee of Selection for the second test match between England and Australia is already at work, and it is obvious that there will have to b. e some changes in the composition of the team, if public opinion is to be conciliated. We have no fault to find with the opening match, though the committee appear to take past reputation too much for granted, and that error will not have to becommitted again, if we are to merit success. It bas all along been said that in batting we should be supreme, but that was before the first match Fits played. England's gravest weakness, however, is in the bowling, and we presume to believe that this part of the eleven could have been more wisely chosPn. On one point, we imagine, nearly all cricket enthusiasts will agree. The matches ought to be played to a finish. We do not think that any encouragement of the devices and tricks which are so often used on the third day to stave off de- feat is in the best interests of the game. A four days' match would, no doubt, play havoc with county fixtures, and would not, perhaps, meet with the approval of professionals; but it would be more sportsmanlike, and would prevent such disappoint- ments as befell the hard-worked Australians a few days ago.</p>
        <p>It has been somewhat hastily assumed that the Dreyfus case is entirely without a parallel in tnodern history, that at least when men of pro- minence have been so unjustly convicted the mis- Farriage has not been found out. This, however, is a mistake. No doubt before the days of the Penny newspayer it was difficult to raise an agitation in regard to a convicted man who was kipposed to be innocent. But strange lights were Occasionally thrown on dark places. Mr. Albert Vandam has just recalled a case of this sort Which occurred in the year 1813. An officer named Jean Fabry, officers' paymaster at a military depot at Strasburg,found on his appointment to the post that the accounts had been falsified by the captain and that payments had been entered to persons who were mythical. Fabry was told in return not to make himself so zealous, and when he reported the matter to the Minister for War his adversar- ies wound the net round him, using the peculiar circumstances for an implication of guilt against him. His story was made out to be as unreal as that of a man who, being caught red-handed with the proceeds of a robbery, offers the explanation that the goods were given to him by another person whom he does not know. Fabry had alto- gether six years' imprisonment, against four-and- a-half suffered by Dreyfus. Then his innocence Was admitted, and 100,0001. damages was awarded !inn. But the victory was only on paper. The injured officer never got his money, and,worn down by his trials, he died a miserable cripple. And all these horrors were perpetrated for the honour of the army.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Trades and Labour Council 9lay congratulate itself on the fact that when there 18 any special business on the tapis the members recognise their responsibilities and attend in num- bers. This was evidenced at a meeting held this Week, when, in addition to other important busi- &amp;Bs, Mr. Malkin—to quote the words of a mem- uer—" gave an account of his 4tewardship," as the representative of the Council on the Board of Guardians. It was amusing in some respects. to see the keen interest and thirst for information in tkegard to the Workhouse inmates, evinced uy the running fire of questions directed tt Mr. Malkin. This was after his rep )rt had en read, and the information given respecting 'se administration of our poor-laws seemed to fr. eate a desire for more knowledge in that direc- gen. Questions respecting the amount of stone a casual had to break before he could leave the Workhouse and proceed on his way and others of nature were asked on all sides; and if the 44d their amusing side they showed the aympathY</p>
        <p>that animated the members for their more unfor- tunate brethren and exemplified the saying : "One touch of nature makes the whole world kin." No one doubts that Mr. Malkin does his share of the work expected from a Guardian but his ready answers to the questions he was asked were con- vincing proof of the strong personal interest he, takes in the welfare of the residents at the work- house.</p>
        <p>An admirable suggestion has been made towards the formation of a Late Opening Association. The theory is that at any rate the Early Closing Asso- ciation would do well to change its title to one of a Shop Assistants' Benefit Association, and then apply their energies to conserving the beginnings of days. What could be more delightful in the summer than a bicycle run in the early morning, before the sun is at its full heat, and when the roads are clean and moist! It is extremely diffi- cult to induce ladies to restrict their hours of shopping, but nothing will induce them to go out shopping early. Of course there is a gootil deal of work to be attended to in a business establishment in the way of dressing windows and sorting goods, but it need not occupy very long, and there is some reasonableness in the sugges- tion that a proportion of assistants might be allowed an hour extra in the mornings. Of course this is impossible where the concession is regularly made in the middle of the week or on the Satur- day. but in many parts of England this does not obtain,and the morning gift would be very greatly appreciated It is a listless spectacle that of a large staff waiting for customers who do not begin to arrive until close on noon.</p>
        <p>One cannot but admire the unflagging energy of the Russian Government in the consumation of great enterprises, though the morality behind that energy is itself of a very dubious character. The Siberian and Manchurian railway is barely well in hand before preparations are being made to carry out a scheme for the establishment of one long waterway between the. Baltic and the Black Sea. This huge undertaking is to facilitate the work of the navy in war time, and to increase commercial prosperity throughout the region to be traversed. The advisers of the Russian Government make no secret of the fact thq,t, in their view' the undertaking is primarily for hostile purposes. Hos- tile purposes lie at the back of three-fourths of Russia's improvements. She cares little for the welfare of her immense population, or for the wel- fare of the world. The Czar's Government has contributed to a fund for fighting the famine in the distressed districts, but for the bulk of the money Russia is coming cap in hand to European humanitarians, and we are practically asked for charitable contributions so that Russia may not be forced to divert any more money from her politi- cal projects. Had the news of the famine not leaked out, it is probable that the miserable people would have been left to their fate. At the present moment they are dying by hundreds of thousands, and they are not so much the victims of an unpreventible famine as the victims of a handful of Royalties and officials who thirst for world dominion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES,</p>
        <p>LBY A CORRESPONDENT] 'Loom, cloud-like gloom, o'er spread'st this town of ours, Death cold and grim destroys its finest flowers."</p>
        <p>During the past few days death has been busy amongst us, and in the persons of Colonel Ashton, and Mr. Philip Speakman two highly respected citizens have been removed. The awful unexpected- ness of the death of Mr Speakman has given rise to heartfelt expressions of sympathy with his widow and family; and, that she and they may be sus- tained in this their sad bereavement is the prayer of all.</p>
        <p>The argument upon the qualities of Board School education as opposed to Denominational School education which took place at the Runcorn School Board last week forma the subject of more than ordinary remark. Still it was an argument over which I can express nothing but sorrow; and one from which there is very little to gain. Mr. James Pritchard, as a progressive, allowed himself to be drawn into a false position. Everyone who knows him, knows he generally guards himselt against such actions but on this occasion it must be admit- ted he allowed himself to stray further away from his habitual track than is his usual wont. And this too he did in the presence of an overwhelming opposition and without his colleagues being in any way aware of his intentions.</p>
        <p>Still, for all that, there is a great deal to be said in favour of what Mr. Pritchard aimed at—even if his opponents were successful in hiding his true meaning under several side issues.—What Mr. Pritchard desired to say,if I am not much mistaken, was "that the conditions of the Board School scholar as compared with those of the scholar of the general ran of Denominational schools, (or Voluntary schools), in the way of facilities for acquiring education are far superior, and that the expenses incurred in providing such conditions are sound and valuable investments—and such that any community of ratepayers would be justified in mak- ing at any time That was undoubtedly his mean- ing in a broad general sense ; and the pity is, I say, that he allowed the diplomacy of his opponents to cause him to stray.</p>
        <p>Those were the facts to which Mr. Pritchard re- quired answer; but what did the answer resolve itself tinte—nothing; more or less than the recapi- tulation of the history of the Granville-street trans- action in as favourable a way to their cause as pos- sible; and invidious comparisons by the chair- man of 10 wide feet and 8 square feet, with the flaunting of the fact that a Voluntary School boy was at the top of the County Scholarship List. True, but the fact of haw ottan a School Board boy has been, and may be top, seems to be ignored.</p>
        <p>Finally I venture to make a suggestion to our worthy Board, which is, that before indulging in any more such scenes, say, when they feel the fighting spirit gaining strength, they should ad- journ to one of the gymnasiums, and have the "bad blood out at the end of some good boxing gloves. It would be as useful to the ratepayers, and much more convenient for the reporters. Otherwise they should draw the line altogether. " _est modus in re bus."</p>
        <p>The Magistrates in appointing Mr. Frank John- son as their clerk have made an eminently popular choice, and rewarded a straightforward industrious servant. For thirty years Mr. Johnson figured as the deputy clerk to the magistrates,and during that time has acquired an enormous fund of legal experi- ence. Amongst the masses Mr. Johnson is more popularly known as" Lawyer Johnson," and though not a practising solicitor his equal upon magisterial law would be difficult to find.</p>
        <p>I O.G.T. TEMPERANCE MISSION LODGE. On Wednesday evening, the above lodge received a visit from the members of the " Samaritan " Lolge, Haydock. There were also visitors present from St. Helens and Earlestown, and from the "Star of the East" Lodge, Warrington. Bro Plant, C.T. (Haydock) presided over an excellent attend- ance. The minutes of the last meeting were read and accepted. Bro. Roberts, L.D., announced a monster rally to be held at Wesley Hall, Liverpool, on Wednesday, the 14th inst., and an Electoral Con- ference to be held at Everton, on Wednesday, the 15th inst. The following programme was than ren- dered by the visitors :—Bro Davies, month organ solo," National Airs ;" Bro Parry, recitation, "What are we to do ;" Sist-r Williams, song, "The old arm chair ;" Bro rates, humorous recitation; Bro Plant, song, " Throw out the life-line ;" Sister Francis (St. Helens), recitation, " When will John Bull gat sensible ?" ; Bro Davies, mouth organ solo, "Men of Elarlecb ;" Bro Sharrock, recitation, "Two orphan boys ;" Sister Williams, song, " I cannot leave the old folk now;" Bro Nurse (Warrington), song, "Over the line." An address was then given by Bra. Plant, C Chaplain (Haydock). A vote of thanks was then tendered to the visitors on the motion of Bro J Sheath, seconded by Bro O'Brien and carried unanimously. The vote was replied to by Bros. Litherlani (Warrington), and Plant (Elaydr,ck). It was decided to invite the Ashton " Excelsior " Lodge on Wednesday, June 21st. The meeting was closed by Bro Plant, C.T., pro tens.</p>
        <p>WHAT WILL DRAW A Sr. HELENs CROWD ?—Large crowds besieged Boots, the chemists, at 6, Ormskirk- street all day on Tuesday, waiting their turn to get a free sample of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills and Daan's Ointment. This was the only opportunity offered to get these medicines free, and hundreds took advantage of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION AT HIGRFIELD TANNERY.</p>
        <p>The employees of the Hightield Tannery snowed their appreciation of the services of their late foretnan, and testified 0 the kindly feeling that existed between them and Mr. J. B. Hutton, by assembling on Taursday last at the Tannery and prmenting him with a valuable dressing case. As will be remembered Mr. Hutton .7ave up his position at Highfield Tannery to take up the duties ef manager of a yard at Warrington.</p>
        <p>SAD END OF A. WARRINGTON GENTLEMAN AT RuNcoaN.</p>
        <p>About 2 o'clock on Thursday morning a gentleman named Cnarles Burton, of Warrington, who had come over to see his brother-in-law 131 r. Pearson, of Irwell- lane, Runcorn, met his death in a tragic manner. At that time he was threading his way. about the Dock Locks with the intention of seeing his brother-in-law at .the tide office, and stepping on to something or other in the semi-darkness he was thrown forward and severely cut his head. Stumbling again he fell into the locks and although his body was warm when taken out of the water, it is believed the wound on the head was suffi- cient to cause death. Later the body was removed to the mortuary. The matter has been reported to the police, and an inquest will be held to-day (Friday). It has since transpired that Ur. Burton was a son of Coun- cillor Burton, of Warringt3a, manager for Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF MAGISTRATES' CLERK.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday the follow- ing magistrates assembled to make the appointment of clerk to the Bench. Messrs. Henry Reynolds (chair- man), James Handley, A. R. Norrno.n, F. J. Norman, G. L.. Davies, J. Ockleston, S. Taylor, W. Timmins, F. Wiswall, and Dr. Steel. There were a number of applications. On coming into Court, the Chairman said that morning they had assembled to appoint a successor to the late clerk and legal advise to tile Bench (Mr. J. Ashton)and he had to declare they had unanimously appointed Mr. Frank Johnson to the position. He hoped he would live many years to enjoy the post. His marked ability dur- ing the last 30 years as deputy clerk had thoroughly proved that be was worthy of the position, and he was sure that would always be so.—Mr. Burt on, solicitor, thought the appointment was in every way one which was warranted by the services Mr. Johnson had rendered, It was an appointment that would have the fullest con- fidence of tne Court, and those who had husiness there. —Mr. Fletcher said he would like to offer a word of congratulation to Mr. Johnson. He had known him longer than any other professional man practising be- fore the Bench. For over 30 years he had experienced the advantage of his courtesy, and he was sure the Bench would have done the same. They had made a worthy selection in their clerk. THE SUICIDE W IDNp.</p>
        <p>At the Adelphi--fi-ot-ei,--W-idnes, on Friiay, Mr. S. Brig- house, coroner, held an inquest touching the death of James Rawlinson, aged 34, 21. Warrington-road, Widnes, who committed suicide on Wednesday by cut- ting his throat with a razor.—Elizabeth Rawlinson, wife of deceased, stated her husband was a foreman at the Lancashire Metal Works. On Saturday, 27th May, he sprained his back whilst at his work, and Dr. Hutchin- eon's assistant attended him. On Tuesday night deceased was very restless while in bed. He got up and went into the back bedroom. Witness went to him and persuaded him to go back to bed. He had the idea that the police were alter him for having neglected his children.—That was a deluslion.—P.S. Williams here stated there were no grounds for de- ceased's belief.—Mrs. Rawlinson, continuing, said that about 2.30 a.m. she got up to get deceased a cup of tea, and went for her mother-in-law. She stayed with them until 5.40 a.m., and then returned home and sent her daughter, Mrs. Roscoe. Deceased was down stairs then. In about twenty minutes' time he went up- stairs. Witness did not follow time until she heard him mcan. She then found him in the back bedroom,lying on his back, lacing the two childm in bed, and trying to cut his throat with a razor. Witness endeavoured to stop him, but could not, and in the attempt got her thumb cut open. Her sister-in-law also tried to stop 'him, but he called them a couple of fools. Witness rushed out for assistance and got George Pope to go upstairs, but when witness jaw her husband again he was dead. Her husband had had the influeuzl, but had never had any delusions before.— George Pope. 19, Ellis3n-street, said that on the day named Mr. Rawlinson's little daughter came running out of the house and said that her "daddy was killing himself." Witness then went into the house and met the mother rushing ouf. She said to witness "ma upstairs Jimmy is cutting his throat." Witness went upstairs, and tried to get the razor away from deceased, but deceased caught bold of his wrist and prevented him, and continued cutting at his throat. Withess endea- voured to stop him bat was unable, as deceased was so vielent.—The Coroner did not think there was any necessity to produce any further evideace,as he thought the case was quite clear. There were only two points for them to consider—did deceased cut his own throat, and was be in a sound state of mini at the time?— With- out retiring the jury returned a verdict of" Suicide whilst temporarily insane."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WARRI:RuTON.</head>
        <p>WARRI:RuTON.</p>
        <p>SPITITUALISM.</p>
        <p>On Sunday Mrs. Nicholls, of Heywood, paid her first visit to Itha Spiritualist society. The lady gave two good addresses that, in the evening, Oa. The philosophy of life and death, 'especially commanding the atten- tion ot the audience. At the close of each address the speaker gave successful clairvoyance On Mon- day evening there was no address, clairvoyance occupying the whole time. NARROW ESCAPE FROM SUFFOCATION.</p>
        <p>Thomas Woreley, aged 34, of 10, Norman-street, was Ready suffocated on Sunday afternoon by sewer gas. It appears that he had been down 3 sewer near the Loagford Wireworks when the smell of gas rendered him unconscious. He was taken to the Infirmary for treatment and is progressing favorably. --</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT THE MERSEY BEDSTEAD WORKS. Harry McLoughlin (17), a brass fitter's labsurer employed at the above works, which are situated in linutsford-road, met with an accident on Monday afternoon. He was assisting a carter to remove a box when it fell on one of his feet, which was badly crushed, He was taken to the Infirmary for treat- ment, where he is likely to remain for some time. TESTIMONIAL TO THE REV. A. A. MARKHAM. Prior to the Rey. A. A. Markham leaving War- rington to take charge of St. Jude's Church, Liver- pool, the Rector, churchwardens, and sidesmen startei a subscription amongst the congregation of the Parish Church with a view of presenting him with a testimonial from those attending the church. A favourable opportunity did not present itself to publicly give it to him,and it was ultimately agreed by the Rector and the officers of the church to for- ward a cheque for .E2O, the amount received. The following letter has been received from Mr. Mark- ham acknowledging the same :—" St. Jude's Vicar- age, Liverpool, June 3rd, My dear Mr. Dawhurst, —1 am indeed very grateful to you and Mr. Craik for the kind expressions contained in your letter, and for the cheque which you enclosed as a present from the congregation at the Parish Church. I need hardly say that no present is necessary to keep Warrington fresh in my mind, but as a mark of goodwill and affection it is very acceptable and gratifying. You do not mention who the subscribers are, 150 I am not able to thank them personally. You must be good enough to do this for me. lam sorry we never achieved any leavetaking,but I really feel still rather part and parcel of the place though it is font months since I went.—Please thank all my kind friends for me, and with kindest regards to yourself and your brother warden and sidesmen, believe me always yours sincerely, ALGERNoN A MARKHAM." The Medical Profession recommend the moderate use of well matured PORT WINli in cases of Influenza. W. and A. GILBRY have specially selected the finest Wine from Oporto for this purpose and thus placed their 3,000 Agents in a position to supply their Invalid Port, at 2s. 6d. per bottle ineyery town.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ORANGE_ PEEL ON THE PAVEMENT-AN ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Edward Moore, aged forty-seen years, living at 6, Wright-street, St —e-FE J ena, was admitted to the St. Helens Hospital on Tuesday morning, suffering from a broken ankle. It appears that Moore, who worked at Ashton's Green Colliery, slipped over some orange peel in the street on Saturday afternoon and fell with his left leg under him. He was at once taken home and Dr. Jackson called in, who, on examining him, found that the ankle was broken, and he was ordered to the hoop.- tal on Tuesday morning;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY JUNE 9, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY JUNE 9, 1899.</p>
        <p>the recipient of heartfelt thanks from Sir John T. Brun.. ner, Bart., the representative. He also was associated svith North wind in other respects, being a member of the Salt Compensation Board, the meetings of which he regularly attendei, and greatly assisted in the arduous work which has had to bs performed by that body since its formation. He was a representative on tha Weaver Trust, where his business capabilities proved of great advantage th that authority. Ho was also invariably a representative on the various Committees sent by the County Council to make inquiries into any public im- provements to be carried out in the district. The urbanity and kindly feeling gained for him a host of friends in the salt district. At the Liberal Club the flag fluted at half-mast.</p>
        <p>REFERENCE AT THE BOA.RD OF GUARDIANS'</p>
        <p>MEETING</p>
        <p>At th 3 meeting of the Board of Guardians of Runcorn on Tuesday, the Chairman (Mr. J. Mosley Williams), saitit was a very great shock and with great regret he was informed on Monday of the sudden death of his neighbour and their County Council representative Mr. Philip Speakman. A very sudden shock and serious trouble had overtaken Mr. Speakman's family, by his sudden death. As Mr. Speakman was an old member of the Board of Guardians, and as their representative on the County Council, he (the chairman) moved a resolu- tion of condolence with the bereaved family. There were members present who had known Mr. Speakman in his private and public capacity longer and more in- timately than he he had done, hut he had known him as a very valuable public man and his loos would be very greatly felt by the community. Mr. T. Baxter, C.C., seconded the motion, observing that the late Mr. Spsakman had been a particular friend of his since he went on the County Council, and he looked, upon him as one of his most valuable constitu- ents. Mr. Speakman bad been a very active and useful member of the County Council and his death would not only be lamented by his family but by the entire county. Mr. J. Meadowcroft said he had known Mr. Speakman over thirty years. He knew him as a member of the old Runcorn Board of Improvement Commissioners and also as a member of the Board of Guardians and he had many conversations with him with respect to the busi- ness of the Board. He knew Mr. Speakman always took a warm intercst in the Board's proceedings and attended the meetings regularly. As a County Councillor Mr. Speakman took extreme interest in the business of the county, and his heart and soul were in the welfare of the Runcorn District, which had lost a very sincere and very warm friend by his death. (Hear, hear.) It would be difficult to replace Mr. Spsakman. In all cases of distress his hand was ever open and if he could help a young man or an older man into some position or other with advantage his services were always at their disposal. The whole com- munity and Runcorn division especially had lost a warm supporter and very sincere friend. The motion was carried unanimously. SYMPATHETIC MESSA.GE FROM THE LIBERAL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. PHILIP SPEAKMAN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. PHILIP SPEAKMAN.</p>
        <p>In the early hours of Monday ,mornin; there passed away in the person of Mr. Philip Speakman, of Fox Hill, Frodsham, a gentleman whose public and business connection with Runcorn, Frodsham, and the greater part of Cheshire and Lancashire was well-known and highly valued. A week ago 1 Mr. Speakman in his capacity as vice-chairman of the Runcorn Petty Sessional Division had stood up and referred with feeling to the life-long acquaint- ance he had enjoyed with that highly esteemed gentleman, the late Mr. Ashton. Then Mr. Speak- man appeared the picture of health, and none in that Court roam would have thought that he would be the next called away. Mr. Speakman was un- able to attend the funeral of the late Colonel Ash- ton on Tuesday owing to an important engage- ment at Liverpool, and it was whilst falfilling that engagement that he contracted a chill. The same night he was expected to fulfil his official position as president of the Runcorn Football Club, but we have already recorded his inability to do so. His slight indisposition, however, soon gave indica- tions of an alarming character and Drs. Burton (Frodsham) and Carruthers (Halton) were called in. Mr. Speakman's condition was then very critical and:he was stated to be suffering from bronchial pneumonia in an acute form. Professional nurses were engaged, and further medical assistance was obtained in the persons of Dr. Dobie, of Chester, Dr. Nesbit, of Liverpool, and finally Dr. Roberts, of London. Their efforts, however, were of no avail, and Mr. Speakman gradually sank and died about three o'clock on Monday morn- ing. His removal is the loss of a useful public man who filled many offices:with marked distinction and ability. A native of Runcorn, he had risen from humble circumstances to the highest positions a man can posslbly occupy among his fellows. He was in. the first place apprenticed to Messrs. John and Taomas Johnson, shipwrights, who formerly had a shipbuilding yard near Belvedere, Runcorn, and eventually he started in business on his own account at Castle Rock. When the construction of the Ship Canal was started upon he disposed of the concern and premises to the Canal Company. Prior to this he had given attention to the coal business, and it was in this direction that he now turned all his energies, with the result that he built up one of the most extensive trades in the country. For many years he was a most useful member of the Runcorn Board of Commissioners, the Board of Guardians and Rural Sanitary Autnority, and his colleagues on these bodies recognised his services by electing him to preside over their deliberations. At the elec- tion of the Urban District Council in 1894 the rate- payeratestified their appreciation of his work under the old authority by electing him at the head of the poll to represent the Heath Ward. On his retirement in 1897, according to the three years custom, he was returned unopposed. None the less distinguished has been his connection with the Cheshire County Connell. On the formation of the superior body in 1888 Mr. Speakman was returned as the colleague of alajor Hazlehurst in the representation of Run- corn. The election of 1892 saw Mr. Speakman elected unopposed for the southern division of the town, and the same thing happened again on his retirement in 1894. He was elected a county alder- man in 1895, and in that capacity he did splendid service, not only iLI the interests of the town but of the coanty generally. Some six years ago he was placed on the Commission of the Peace for the county, and in the discharge of his functions as a magistrate he was always actuated with a desire to temper justice with mercy. A further outlet for his ability was his appointment on the Cheshire Standing Joint Committee, the important work of which always received from him the most unremitting attention and consideration. To retuin to his native town, he was found to be a warm supporter of all institutions for the benefit of the community. As the president of the Runcorn Football Club he was deservedly respected and esteemed. Testimony to this fact was presented a few weeks ago, when he entertained the committee officials and players, to a dinner at the Wilson's Hotel. It was then stated that from no one had the club received the help they had frotn Mr. Speakman. He had been with them in their ups and downs And had stood beside them in prosperity and adversity. Mr. Speakman was a member of the Ellesmere Lodge (758) ot Freemasons, and formerly took an active interest in its operations. In politics he was a Liberal, and as such he was returned to the County Council. He was for several years connected with the Wesleyans of Runcorn. He identified himself with the Camden Sunday School, of which he was one of the superintendents. His interest especially in the infants of the school has remained unabated. He was also connected with the society at Camden Chapel, of which he was a steward for about six years. Mr. Speakman during his life was an active and vigorous Liberal, undertaking resfonsible work for the party at all times. As ohairm an of the Liberal ex- ecutive, and a vice-president of the Runcorn Liberal Club he was always well-known for the strength and zounflocss of his principles. He was a vice-president of the Runcorn Liberal Club from the time of its inception. THE MAGISTRATES' TRIBUTE TO MR. SPEAKMAN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, the Chair- man (Mr. Henry Reynolds), said it was his melancholy daty as representing the Bench of magistrates of that division, to allude to the sal event which happened that morning. He was entirely unable to find words which would adequately express the very great loss they bad sustained in the sod lon death of Mr. Philip Speakman. He was a man who took a very great interest in all pub- lics matters, and was undoubtedly a most excellent magistrate. He was an alderman ot th county, and a member of the Chesuire Standing Joint Cimmittee, and in these capacities he discharged his duty with marked ability. His great loss would be much felt, not ouly in Runcorn but also all over the county. They deeply sym- pathized with his family in the very great loss they had sustained.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Handley said on behalf of the Court, he would like to move that the clerk be in3tructed to send a letter of condolence to the mNnbers of the la Mr. Speakman's family. He scarcely knew how to express himself on that occasion. Last Monday, Mr. Soeakman occupied the seat which he (Mr Handley) now filled, and it was his duty then at his (Mr. Handley's) raque3t to make a faw remarks with reference to the great loss they had sustained through the death of their late clerk. He (the speaker) little thought then that they would have the melancholy duty to refer to the los3 they had sustained through Mr. Speakman's deatt. They sympathized very deeply with the family, al the Chairman had so fittingly put it, and they desired in the mlgt fitting way, to express their sense of the loss they bad sustained.</p>
        <p>Mr. Burton said they had indeed sustained a very great loss in the death of Mr. Speakman. He was always kind, considerate, and patient, and always desirous, if Possible, of bringing disputants together, and in carry- ing out his duties as magistrate he was actuated with a desire to temper juitice with mercy RS far as he could do el. He (Mr. Burton) would like to join himself to the expression of deep regret which the Bench prtposed to send to the widow. - - -</p>
        <p>Mr. Fletcher said in Northwich they sympathised very deeply with the family in the loss they had sustained. Mr. Speakman was elected vice-chairman of the North- wich Compensation Board, to which he (Mr. Fietcher) was solicitor, and he was quite sure all the members of the board would be deeply grieved to know of the loss they had sustained by his sudden removal from their midst. He was a kind-hearted man, who, he was sure, did as much for Runcorn as his means and abiiiPy en- abled him to do. He was always ready to discharge his public functions, and always punctual in keeping them, and by his death the whole county had sustained a very great loss indeed. He must say in coming to the Court that morning it was the rudest shock he could imagine to find that one who was so universally esteemed had been suddenly removed. He joined the Bench in ex- pressing his deep sympathy with the widow and family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>VICAR OF RUNCORN'S TESTIMONY.</head>
        <p>THE VICAR OF RUNCORN'S TESTIMONY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Society for the Propagation of the GaspPl, on Monday evening in the Boys' National School th 9 Vicar in his speech referred to the decease of one vino was very well known in Runcorn, who had passed away at 3a.m. that day. He meant Mr. Philip Speakman. He said he would not speak of his character or what he did, but he desired to express their deepest sympathy with the widow. He would like to add there was one ad- mirable feature in Mr. Speakman which was well worthy of imitation, viz., he did not forget his native place, and he was not above speaking to His old friends and uniting with them. SYMPATHY FROM THE RUNCORN FOOTBALL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>CLUB.</head>
        <p>CLUB.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Committee of the Runcorn Foot- Intl Club on Monday evening, the secretary (Mr. J. Ellison) was instructed to forward a letter to the family of the late Mr. Speakman, expressing their sense of the great lo;s the club had sustained by the death of thei esteemed president, and tendering the deep sympathy of the club with the family in their painful bereavement</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>NOIiTHWICH-.</head>
        <p>NOIiTHWICH-.</p>
        <p>The sad intelligence-a—the-de-ail-of Mr. Philip Speak- naad was received at Northwich with deep regret, and caused a feeling akin to sensation, it being known by comparatively few tbat he was unwell. The deceased gentleman was the chairman of the Northwich Parlia- mentary District Liberal Associatiqn,a position occupied by him for many years, daring which he rendered valiant service on behalf of the cause. He at all times showed an active readineis to do what he possibly could to further the interests of the party, and he was often</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>CLUB.</head>
        <p>CLUB.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Rancorn Liberal Club, held on Tuesday evening, a deep sense of the great loss sus- tained by the club through the death of Mr. P. Speak- man was expressed, and on the motion of Mr. W. B. Brimelow (chairman) seconded by Mr. dames Loa a vote of condolence and sympathy with 'Ws. Speakman and family was unanimously carried. Toe text of the vote was as follows:—" The committee and members of the R,oncorn Liberal Club beg to express their sincere sym- pathy with you and your family in the deati of your beloved husband and hope that an all wise Providence will sustain you in your sad bareavemont.—(Signed), W. B. Brimelow, chairman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The mortal remains of Mr. P. Speakman were placed in their last resting plam at Greenway-road Cem.tery, on Thursday last. The funeral procession left Fox Hill, Frodsham, at about eleven o'clock, and at anout 12.30 was met by a large number of friends, who were gathered to pay their last respect t 3 the deceased gentleman's memory, at the Crane, Halton-road, from whence the cortege wended its mournful way by Delph Bridge and Victoria-road, to St. Michael's Church,in the following order :—Magistrates, County Coancillors, Urban Councillors, Overseers, S&amp;c., Oddfellows, Liberal Club, friends and general public, members of the Run- corn Football Club, Freemasons, employes, hearse, and chief mourners. Large crowds lined the route of the procession. Throughout the dietrict there were vis- ible signs of the general regret felt and from all points flags could be seen floating at half-mast. Blinds were drawn and other indications of respect were visible on all sides.</p>
        <p>The funeral procession was met at the entrance of St. Michael's Church, Runcorn. by the surpliced choir and the Revs Canon Wood, MA., (vicar of Runcorn), H B Blogg, M.A., (vicar of Frodshans), C C Bluett. M.A., (formerly of Barlow-moor, Manchester; P.M. of the Wigan Ledge of Freemasons, and P G Chaplain, a mem- ber of the Lodge of which the deceased gentleman was Past Master, wad an old friend of :the family), E P Alexander, M.A., (St. Matthews, Stockport), A J Jameson, MA.. (St. Augustin's, Brinks ?fey. Stockport) and S Oliver, 8.A., (curate, Runcorn.) As they entered the Church the choir chanted the opening senteeces of the burial service. inside the sacred edifice the 39th Psalm was chanted to Feltoa, after which the Rev C C Bluett read the lesson. The procession then re-formed and proceeded to the graveside. As the coffin was being borne down the aisle the organist (Mr W Grounds) played: the Dead March from Saul. At the graveside, the remaining portion of the service (which was fully choral), was conducted by the Rev Canon Wood. At the conclusien the hymn " Oa toe res arc° cti m morn" was s ung. The following took part in the funeral proces;ion : Representing toe Police :—Superintendent D Pearson, Inspector E C Hicks, Sergeants Cooper, and Wsite; Acting-Sergeant Gregory, Constables Gregory, Wylie, Bencroft,Bratt,Wilsen,Carrington,Wilding and Edwards Magistrates of the Runcorn Petty Sessional Division ; Messrs James Handley, J Occleston, W Timmins, S Tay- lor, F J Norman, A R Norman, G L Davies, F Johnson (magistrates' clerk). Representatives of the County Council: Messrs W R Earø, E Imison, T Baxter, J Williams, county auditor. Representatives of the Run- corn Urban District Council : Messrs F Wiswall, chair- man; H J Grice. M J Sweetman, James Pritchard, T Dean. G Hewitt, E Greeley, S Walton, T Jones, W Handley. D Bisbrown, J W Lightburn, and J Wilson, Dr McDougall (medical officer), E Mar- shall, secretary; J D Jones, librarian ; T C Haddock, treasurer ; M Barker, surveyor; W Jones, T A Lewrin- son, head master Technical School; W Garrett, Market Inspector, J Percival, W Sutton, assistant overseer ; and J W Yates, assistant inspector. Amongst a large num- ber of employees present were Messrs J Moore, W Nield, A Dutton. Jo'en Carrot and Alexander Carrel. Repre- senting the Duke of Devonshire Lodge of Oldfellews M.U. were Messrs W E Dudley, P.P.G. M. and ti Toone, P.P.G.M. Members of the Ellesmere Lodge (758) Free- masons :—Messrs J Hill, trier; John Tudor, J Littler, T Hill, Albert Abel. T W Forster, I.G. ; G Semerdeld, P.M. ; J Collier, E Newten, J.W. ; %V Henkineon. T H Annett, James Looker, P.P.G.O. (Warwickelaire),W , R Wass, P.P.G.D. of C.; H Salkeld, P.M.; W Mellor, P.P.G.J.D. ; H Branch, W.M. ; James Cunnah I (No 148). Deputation from Runcorn Liberal Club :—Messrs W Brienelow (chairman), T Woods, T Lea, James Lea, J Elliott, J Jolley, H Hughes, Harry Wright and B 'Crosby. Runcorn Football Club: Messrs S Honghton (captain) S Walker, J Nickson, Owen Hughes, J Faulkner, Alfred Taylor, T Wilkinson, J Dyer, L Hailwood, Alfred Abel (assistant secretary), J Ellison (secretary). C Pownall (treasurer), A Lightburn, T Moores. T Robinson, G Robinson, D Jones, H Cawley, D Mackie. and T Deakin. Other local men present were Messrs G F Ashton, P Unsworth, J Nancollis, A Riley. J Mort, John Corns, C Dutton, J Toon, J Horsfield, J Douglas, T Boote, W flughes,'T Riley, F Beck, George Christie (representing Mr W Orrell. Postmaster and staff), R W Pierpeint, NV Fisher, J Morrison (district manager of the United Alkali Company), Robert Wright, G Bellamy, F J Moul- ton, J R Frith, J M isoa Frith, H W Handley, C Hand- ley (representing Runcorn S lying&amp; Bank), W Bate, W Joynson, H Chadwick, J Frodsham, W Shaw, captain C Davies, G Pearson, W Rushton. F Les.thwood, Captain Andrew Milling,ery, T Ford, John Pritchard, J Fox, J Darlington, Dr McLennan, and Dr Robinson. Toe following gentlemen met the cortege at St. Michael's Church : Messrs J Beckett, C.C., A.udlem; A T Wright, C.C., Dr Hewitt, C.C, (Brunner Mond and Company), Henry Brett, C.C., Nerthwich ; Dr Smith,l C.C., Weaverham ; R Potts. clerk of County Council, A Hornby, clerk to the County Asylums Committee; H F Bull, County Surveyor; J R Thompson,County Treasurer was unavoidabiy prevented fromattending; N A E Way, Chester; P W Brundrit,Liverpool (formerly of Runcorn); J A M'Culloch, Liverpool; W D Mack, Liverpeol ; W Newall, managing director Liverpool Lighterage Com- pany ; Henry Bloor. Birkenhead. secretary; Thomas Clarkson, Mossley Bill; William Nelson, Liverpool ; R W Manning, representing Liverpool Reform Club ; J Stone, Huyton ; Henry Swift, (Pilkington Bros. Limited, St. Helens; A Cross, Liverpool ; J Smith, Bickershaw ; George Holme,jun., Broadgreen; Rev Augustus E Farrar, County Asylum, Chester ; Joseph R Burrows, represent- ing the Winstanley Collieries Company,Limited,Woreley Mesnes Colliery Company, and Eccleston Hall Colliery Company; John Spencer, Liverpool; J J Evans, Liverpool, Joseph Nelson, Liverpool; Walter Simpson, Haigh, Ralph Peters, Astley and Tyldesley Coal Com- pany, Ltd. ; Treasurer of Manchester Coal Exchange; Joseph Hodgson, Tyldesley ; S J Bott, Stoke-on-Trent; Joel Settle, Alsager ; Charles W Simpson, Wigan ; Thomas Wright and S H Noden, churchwardens of Alvanley Church; W D Laird, Chief Inspector of Weights and Measures for the County; Sir Joseph Ver- din. Northwich ; A Fletcher. Northwich ; J H Coeke, Winsford ; C E Linaker, Frodsham ; H R Mortimer, Frodsham ; J J W Potter, P.M. 1908; W Tongue, Gars- ton Docks ; J Thompson, Chester; Joseph Spedding, James Beaten, Wigan ; G E Lomax and W M Camp- bell, Sutton Heath Collieries ; P H Barnes, representing Messrs J and W Barnes, Liverpool ; A Davies, Liverpool; C E Barlow. A E Barlow and J Forshew, Widnes Alkali Works; Edward Hughes, P.M. secre- tary of Wigan Masonic Lodge ; C Alvanley ; Edwin Grundy, White Moss Coal Company ; William Taverner, P.M. (2326) IN igen ; Simon S Brown,</p>
        <p>1W.M.(2326); Major Murray.P.M.P.G.T. Wigan; and Rev C R Killick ; A Jordan, F A Lake, R N Davies, J J Speakman, J ..'.,p3akman, Captain N Rowlands , R Rawlinson, S C Beckett, A Harrison, G Millington, J E Meredith, :John Frodsloam, all of Runcorn ; John Edwards,Hamer Lodge 1393 ; Joseph Spedding, Ellerby Collieries, Liverpool. The marshalls were : Messrs James Lea, W Miles, and F W Fitton. The arrangements for the funeral were carried out by Mr R Morris, draper, High-street, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The chief mourners were :—Messrs Ernest, Eaton, and Charlie Speakman, sons ; Mr Donetil, of Surbiton, soa- in-;aw ;Messrs W A Stearns. London, and W Carroll. A large number of beautiful wreatas were sent by the following: Bertha Eaton and Chirlie ; Ada and Victor , Kitty and Philip, Arthur. Bland and L Sir John ' and Lady Brunner, Mr C R Cowan, Mr James Heaton Wigan ; Shaw-street tenants ; Mr 1 0 Occleston, Water- loo; Mr Steele, Frodsham ; Mrs Garratt, Miss Croett- I waite. Mrs Mortimer, Mrs Ternperley, Dr Bowman, Mr and Mrs Arnold, Hampstead: Directors and Secretary of the Liverpool Lighterage Company, Limited, Mr and Mrs J smith, Bickershavy ; Mr and Mrs W Nelson, Liverpool; Mr and Mrs Ray, IMr and Mrs Joel Settle, Mr F E Moss ; Mr Orrell, Mrs Burrows, Liverpool ; Runcorn Football Club, committee and members (two), Dr and Mrs W C Burton.Frodsharn; clerks of Liverpool office, Mrs Ernest Speakman, Ches.- ter ; Mr R Abel, Mr and Mrs Whittaker, Mr and Mrs S J Boots, Stoke-en-Trent; police of Runcorn Petty Ses- sional Division, Mr W Tomlinson, M.P.. and Mr Walter Simpson, Winstanley Colliery Company, Worsley Mesnes Colliery Co.. Allerbeck Colliery Company,and Eccleston Colliery Company, Limited ; Charles, Fox Hill ; Collins Green Colliery Company ; Mary and Dot ; Runcorn Boating Club ; J and E Bell, Frodshain ; Mr and Mrs Thomas, Frodsham ; Servants, Manor Rouse; Rev C and Mrs Chorley, Mrs Alfred C Sterns ; Mrs Donezil, Nurse and Servants, Surbiton ; W Sothern, Mr and Mrs J J Crosfield ; Rev and Mrs Henry Blogg, Fredsharn ; and Maids at Fox Hill.</p>
        <p>...............6 RUNCORN BOARD OF GU A. RDIA.NS</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Board was held at Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday last. Mr. J. Mosley Williams (chairman) presided, and their were also pre- sent : Messrs S Taylor, JP, A R Norman, JP, T Lea, T Reynolds, J Mead owcroft, Dr Gastaldi, with the Dii- tricc Councillors and Mr George Ashton (clerk), and Mr J Cunnah (master).</p>
        <p>THE LATE CLERK-VOTE OF CONDOLENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mosley Williams said he was sure they would all join with him in saying that a very mournful task had fallen to his lot—one that he deeply realised—that was to announce to them the death of their late respected and honoured clerk (Mr. Ashton). He wished he could find words in order to express his own feelings, and in doing so he felt sure he would be expressing the feelings of every member of the Board. (Hear, hear.) They had all been associated with Mr. Ashton for a long time, and of all the public men he had ever been associated with be knew none who had paid a more conscientous devotion to duty coupled with an full a consideration for those around him. He never remembered Mr. Ashton—to use a com- mon phrase—"put in his motto"—prompting the Colin. cil to any action which the Council in its wisdom thought proper not to proceed with except where it had been illegal to do so and when it was out of ordere(l3ear, hear.) Of his public capacities enough had been said by the several public bodies, to which Mr. Ashton was associated, to show that in all his actions the late clerk was greatly honoured and hiehly respected, and they (the Board of Guardians) would not give place to any expression which had been uttered elsewhere in their confirmation of the eulogies of his private and public character. (Hear, hear.) Living in Frs•dshem, as be (the Chairman) did, he as a member of the Board for nine or ten years, frequently visited Mr. Ashton in the evenings when away from the office, ' and had taken counsel with him upon many cases and matters connected with the Board of Guardians, and with the Froisham locality, and he had never felt that his presence was in any way irksome, or that he was not perfectly willing to close up his newspaper and to give attention to what he had to say. In other words Mr. Ashton was always making him feel he was a wel- e 'lris guest at his house and he (the Chairman) had re- ceived from him the greatest possible consideration in a friendly manner in his private as well as in his public capacity. He therefore proposed—he wished he could do it in nr.re eloquent words—that they tender t Mr. Ashton's family tee deep and sincere condolence of the Board in their great sorrow. Mr. J. Stokes (vice-chairman) seconded, and said he had been associated with the late clerk for many years. He knew Mr. Ashton at the time he was a Guardian,aod in that capacity he was so well known and so deeply respected that be was madeCh Orman, and in that posi- tion he gave the utmost satisfaction to the members. He (Mr. J. Stokes) remembered the time when Mr. Ash- ton was appointed Clerk,and at the time it was observed that the Guardians were simply conferring honour where honour was due. He could assure the Board as a Guardian of long experience that Mr. Ashton's death had left a gap which would be hard to fill. The combination of good qualities which Mr. Ashton posseszed and displayed in his various offices, it would be hard to find in another man—(hear, hear)— for he carried out the duties in such a' satisfactory and pleasant manner. Mr. Ashton always met them with a smile. There were traits in the character of Mr. Ashton which many of them would do well to imitate. By his death they had suffered a very great loss. Mr. Ashton's funeral showed that he was a gentleman universally re- spected, that he was a man who stood out amongst men and his death was not only a loss to the Beard of Guar- dians but to the nation at large. (Her, hear.) Mr. T. Darlington said he should like to support the motion for the reason, especially,that he believed he had been more intimately connected with the late clerk— with the exception of one gentleman in the room—a near relative—than any other person in the room. While he was deeply grieved to lose a dear friend, he rejoiced to believe that Mr. Ashton was receiving a better reward than could be given by any human public body. (Hear hear.) Mr. Mclntyre supported the motion stating that Mr. Ashton, besides being an efficient clerk, was a man in whom they had every confidence, and upon whom they looked with the utmost respect—tie might almost say they towel him for his kindly, gentle, and sympathetic manner. (Hear,hear.) As the late clerk's son was now the acting clerk he hoped that the Chairm sn would send the letter of condolence to the family. The Chairman willingly adopted the euggestion and the motion was carried unanimously, the whole of the members standing in their places. Mr.Gecrge Ashton thanked the members of the Beard who bad proposed the motion for their kind expressions, and the whole of the members for the manner in which they had received the motion with respect to his late father. He was sure the whole of the members of his family could not help but thoroughly apprniate the Board's sympathetic feelings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>CLERKSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE CLERKSHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward propo3ed that Mr. George Ashton be applinted temporary clerk to the Board until an appointment was made. Mr. C. E. Linakar seconded and the motion was unani- imusly carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>LADIES' VISITING COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>LADIES' VISITING COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. R. Norman propasel that Mr. F. J. Norman, of Runcorn, he added to the metnbers of the Ladies' Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Reynold econded. Mr. Roylance also proposed that Mrs. 2113 a Armitage, of Warrington, be alio appointed a member of the same committee. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Bretherton secandel. The names of both ladies were unanimoasly receive 1. FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodgrard proposed the adoption of the finance reoirc. Out-relief was granted as follows ; Runcorn, £B5; Frodsham £23 ; Budworth £2O. The balance in hand that day was £2213 93 83. Mr. Wright seconded, and the report vieas adopted. THE SUPERINTENDENT REGISTRARSHIP AND THE CLRRIESHIP</p>
        <p>Mr. N. A. Humphreys, chief Clerk, General Register Office, Somerset House London, W C, wrote as follows : I am directed by the Registrar General to acknowledge the receipt of your letter of the 31st ult. reportlng the death of Mr. J. Ashton late superintendenr, registrar for Runcorn Registration District, and in reply to enclose the necessary form and instructions to enable the Run- corn Gusriians to appoint a duly qualified person to fill the vacant office.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated that the appointment must be made within 14 days or the power of appointment would lapse to the registrar general. Mr. Linaker proposed that Mr. George F. Ashton be appointed superintendent registrar and that the appoint- ment come up for confirmation at the neat meeting. Mr. Woodward sec3nlei the motion, which was carried unanimously. The Chairman pointed ou% that the B 3ard could ap- po.nt a clerk by giving notice or putting the office up for public competition. However, as he understood the great majority of the members were favourable to the appointment of their present able acting clerk, 1:: would be unfair to the outaide applic Ants to aiverti3e under such conditions. Mr. Woadward gave notice that subject to the ap- pointment of a committee to take a review of the salaries to be paid to the Clerk of the Board of Guardians, he would nominate Mr. George F. Ashton as clerk which would be confirmed nest fortnight. (Hear, bear.) The following was the committee to review the sala- ries :—The Chairman, Messrs J Stokes, T Wright, S Woodward, C E Linaker, J Knowles, J Mclntyre, A R Norman, S Woodward. and G Piling.</p>
        <p>POOR LAW CONFERENCE REPRESENTATIVES.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. H.E. Lawrence, secretary to the Association of Poor Law Unions in England and Wales, wrote remind- ing the Guardians that he had not received tho names of the representatives appointed by the Runcorn Union on the 30th May. The names and ad iresses of repre- sentatives must be seat to him not later than 15:h June to entitle them to attend the meetings of the association.</p>
        <p>The Cna!rrnan and Clerk were appointed the represen- tatives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
