      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>NEWTON TTRBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON TTRBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held on Monday even- ing in the Town Hall, Earlestown. Mr C B F Berroa 'LP, CO., presided, and there were also present : Messrs W Collingvvood. J W Emmett, J.P., N Siddeler, W Gilmour, F J Wood, J Simpson. Dr Patterson, J Forster, E Evans, T. Orraand, W Thompson, with Mr W W Shirley (clerk), Mr Bowes ksurveyor), and Mr H D Lloyd (gas engineer).</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE. At a meeting of this Committee the Gas Works exten- sion Committee reported that they recommended :—(a) That tenders for a photometer be invited; (b) that two horses and two carts to purchased for use by the Gas Department ; (e) That the tender of Messrs. Dempster and Sous of Eltand, for a condenser capable of passing 40,000 cubic feet per hour, for the sum of £414, be accepted; (d) That the tender of Messrs. Bryan, Dankin and Co., of tiermondsey, London, S.E., for an exhauster capable of passing 50,000 cubic feet per hour, for the sum of £266, be accepted ; and (e) That the Clerk be authorised to proceed with the necessary formalities to obtain a Provisional Order to further amend section 53 of the Newton District Improvement Act. 1855, as amen. ded by Provisional Order in 1876 by further increasing the borrowing powers of the Council for Gas works Purposes by £20,000, the Clerk to he empowered it necessary to consult Mr. Worsley thereoe. It was resolved that the foregoing recommendations of tee Gas Works Extensions Committee be approved and adopted. The tenders for cannel f)r the ensuing year were referred to the Gas Works Extensions Committee. The following tenders were s.ccepted :—Messrs. R. Evans and Go., Limited, of Haydock, Wigan 4 feet coal, 9s. 6d. per ton nett ; Arley nuts, 9s. 31 per ton nett Arley coal lls, per ton nett delivered :at the Crow6lane siding; the United Alkali Company Limited for the supply of brown vitriol (140 degrees) at 24,5, per ton, nett; Mr. James Howarth, St. Helens, Buxton lime at 12s. 63. per ton nett ; free on rails at Earlestown Station The question of adopting a standard pattern of street lamps was referred to the Gas Works Extensions Com. mittee. It was also ordered that the sum of £737 Is 1d being the amount of profit during the year ended 31st March, 1899, in respect of the Gas Works be transferred to the Reserve Fund Account.</p>
        <p>The minutes were passed on the proposition of the Chairman seconded by Mr. Thompson.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>This committoe ordered that the Clerk, Z—tti. the Medical Officer of Health, take an opportunity of seeing the Medical Officer of Health of Warrington as to pro- vision for further accommodation for scarlet fever cases from this district at the Warrington Isolation Hospital. It was also ordered that the Clerk write to those trade;- people who make a practice of obstructing the footpaths by exposing goods for sale, and request them tl discon- tinue the practice. —The committee resolved that legal proceedings be taken against John McCloud, of 81,Legh- strict, Earlestown, for keeping an unregistered common lodg;ng house, and against Susan Forshaw, of 13, Fair- dough-street, Earlestown, for overcrowding a registered common lodging house, and that Gabriel Moon, agent, Cross-lane, Earlestown, Mrs. Hazleton, Market-street, and W. H. Edison, of Lawrence-street; b 3 served with notice to abate the respective nuisances complained of and reported in the NUi99.llCe inspector's Journal, and if not abated within seven days of the date of such notice legal proceedings to be taken to enforce the provisions of the Public Health Acts.—The Surveyor having submitted his report it was resolved (a) that his recommendation as to the ventila- tion of Wargrave-road sewer be approved and adopted, and (b) that that part of his report referring to clerical assistance in the Surveyor's Department be referred to Mr. Collingwood, Mr. Borrort aid Mr. Ormand. Build- ing plans were passed for Miss E. Marrow, shop window to dwelling-house ; Dr. Patterson, conservatory ; John Naylor, five houses and shops, St. Helen's-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>THE_SE WAGE DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>THE_SE WAGE DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>A letter datedlBth April, was read before the High- ways and Building Committee from the 14;cal Govern- ment Board enquiring what steps the Council were tak- ing with regard to the purification of sewage ; also a letter, dated the lat of May, was read from the Public Health Committee of the Lancashire Clunty Council re- questing the Council to take stens within the next six months to efficiently treat the effiaent from their sewage works, and thereby prevent the pollution of Sankey Brook. After consideration of the foregoing letters it was resolved that the surveyor take sa.mples.at intervals net exceeding two weeks, of the effluent flowing into Sankey and Newton Brooks front the Council's Sewage Works, and that a diary be kept by him showing ap- proximately the quality of the effluent ; when the sam- ples were taken; the times of the cleansing of the tanka; an approximate quantity of the sewage treated ; and any other information as may appear to him necessary. Two letters, dated respectively May s:h and May 9:h. were read from the Golborne Urban District Council in reply to the Clerk's letters, stating that they had made enquiries of the Ashton-in-Makerfield Urban District Council, and found that they had nearly completed their plans for a sewerage works, which, when constructed, would prevent the pollution of Millingford Brook from the Asht)n District.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>UNLOADING OF MANURE.</head>
        <p>THE UNLOADING OF MANURE.</p>
        <p>The Highways and Buildings Committee recommended that in the matter of the complaint as to the unloading of manure from barges on the Canal at Newton Common the Clerk write to the petitioners and state that the con 3-• mittes will insist in the future on the manure being re- moved immediately on arrival, but that when they con- sider the consignments of the manure are essential for agricultural purposes they feel they would not be justi- fied in endeavouring to completely stop such traffic. APPLICATION FOR INCREASED WAGES. An applicttion, da.ted 25th April, was read before the Highways and Buildings Committee from the workmen on the roads (paviors, lab)urers, so tvengers, &amp;c.) for an increase of wages. It was ordered that the same be re- ferred to Mr. Collingwood, Mr. Borron, Mr. Ormand,and Mr. Thompson for their consideration and report. The minutes were approved. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. _ _ CHEAP TRAINS. _</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee a letter dated the 17ch of April, was read from Mr. Shaw, the District Superin- tendent ot the L. and N. W. Railway Co., in answer to the Clerk's letter of March 11th,1899, asking for certain concessions named therein with reference to cheap fares and a more frequent stopping at Earlestown Station of through trains to Wigan, Liverpool, and Manchester, and stating in reply thereto that the 1-10 p.m. train, Earlestown to Liverpool, will be made a cheap train on certain days in the week, but with regard to the stop- ping of the trains named he had considered this very carefully but regretted that he could not see his %say to accede to the application, whereupon it was ordered that the Clerk write to Mr. Shaw thanking him for his concession, and asking him to reconsider his decision as regards the stopping of trains. The Chairman moved and Mr. Evans seconded the adoption of the minutes and they were agreed to. FINANCE COMMITTEE. —MR. GILMOUR AND CAB FARES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormand moved the con—Crm. I.tion of the minutes of the Finance Committee. Mr. Siddeley seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour called attention to an item of lla 61 for cab hire, and inquired what it was for. Mr. Ormand read out the various items of Mr. Wal- ton's account for cab hire.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour: I want to knew who was in the cabs. Mr. Ormand : I can only say I wasn't in one of those cabs.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilmour: I think it is a proper question to ask. It is not fair for our Clerk to go to Newcou and take a cab when he thinks prop Er. The Chairman : I don't think I was in the cabs. (Laughter). Mr. Gilmour appears to ask a question the answer to which he already knows. Mr. Ormand : If Mr. Gilmour had given notice of the question, I would have endeavoured to obtain all parti- culars for him. lam not a walking encyclopedia for the Urban District Council, and cannot tell who has occu- pied these cabs. Mr. Gilmour: I know Mr. Ormand is not an ency- clopedia, but if I make any inquiry lam entitled to an answer. I think our Clerk has no right to go to Newtoa ill a cab.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormind : Mr, Gilmour ought to give notice of his questions according to the standing orders. I don't think he ought to corn h,!re and ask such questions without notics. Time after time he does so,and I don't know why. He has ample opportunity given hint ire committee to make any enquiries and satisfy himself as to the accounts. But he always fails to do do and comes here every month wanting an answer to some twopence- halfpenny questione.</p>
        <p>dihnour : It is only fair that we should know where our officials are going and how they are going. The Chairman: You have no right to make any in- sinuations as to who has used the cabs. It is a groat lack of taste on your part, The minutes were then confirmed, Mr. Gihnour only dissenting. PROPOSED FURTHER BORROWING POWERS FOR</p>
        <p>GAS WORKS. The Chairman moved " Tnat this Council do hereby decide to make application to the Local Government Bard for a provisional order to further amend section 53 of the Newton District Improvement Act of 1855, as amended by article 1, provisional order 39 and 40 Victoria 1876, ch. 97, by altering the said s)ction so as to enabb this Council to borrow for Gasworks purposes, subject to the sanction of the Local Government B lard; the sum of £44,000, in lieu of the sum of £24,000 au- thorised to be borrowed by section 53 of the said Act and Article 1, of the said Provisional Order."</p>
        <p>Dr. Pa'ttersln seconded, and ths resplution Wg3 carried.</p>
        <p>The Council then rose.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GCOD BOOTS.—Advt. CADBURY'S COCOA. is absolutely pure, being entirely frees from kola, malt, hops, alkali, or any foreign admixture Caution The nubile should insist on having CADBURY'S —sold only in Packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are oftea substituted for the sake of extra profit.—Advt. (4).</p>
      </div>
