      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES. FRAUDS ON TUE LONDON AND NORTH WESTERN RAILWAY</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES. FRAUDS ON TUE LONDON AND NORTH WESTERN RAILWAY</p>
        <p>COMPANY</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Friday morning before the Mayor (presiding), Alderman Smethurst. and Mr. H. Hepherd, a number of persons were charged with travelling on the London and North- Western Railway from Arpley Station to Latchford on the 29th April without having paid their fares and with intent to aveid payment of same.—The defendants, whose names are as follow, all pleaded guilty .—Ann Arstall, 52, Wash-lane, Latchford ; Mary Bell, Oak Villa Farm. Lymm ; Emma Wilkinson, Elizabeth Wilkinson, Mary Ellen Eccleston, Thomas Barker,and George How- croft, all of Sandy-lane, Lymm.—Mr. Fenna who prose- cuted said the circumstances were very sinaple but ac- cording to his instructiens it was a gross and systema- tic fraud, It bad been going on for years and chiefly amongst people residing at Heat- hay, Warburton, and Lymna. There was, in these cases, a deliberate intention carried out to the finish to defraud the company of 1d The circum- stances were somewhat similar in all the cases. D.tec- tives had been put on to watch these people or others who might attempt anything of the sort.—Walter Tim- parley, station master at Hatley and Warburton said he remembered Mrs Antall arriving by the 9.7 train from Arpley. Witness bad a communication made to him. Detective Howell brought Mrs Arstall back as she was leaving and said she had travelled from Arpley to Latch- ford without paying her fare. Mrs Antall said she had no money to pay her fare. She would come down next day. Witness said he would net accept it then. The following day she paid the lid excess fare to a porter who knew nothing about the mattsr.— Detective Howell said when he told Mrs Arstall She had travelled from Apley to Latchford without pay- ing her fare, she said she got on at Latchford. Witness reminded her that he saw her get in at Arpley, and she then admitted it.—The Chairman said thy were liable to a penalty of 403 and costs, but they were fined lOs and costs or seven days in default.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>THREE -MONTHS FOR THEFT.</head>
        <p>THREE -MONTHS FOR THEFT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Wednes- day morning, before Alderman James Fairclough (in the chair), Bridget White or Laney was charged with steal- ing a pair ot lady's boots value 34 from the shop door of James Macdonald, 49, Horsemarket-street. between 9 30 and 10-30 the previous day.—Superinteadent Monaghan said the boots were safe at 9-30, and about 11 o'clock Detective Barlow was going to a common lodging house in Ship-yard. when be star prisoner offering the boots for sale. She was brought to the Police Station and wean charged with the theft she said it was a lie. The boots were identified as the property of Mr. Macdonald. Prisoner, said Superintendent Monaghan, had been up 46 times before ; 8 timee for stealing.—The Bench commit- ted ber for three months with bard labour. SQUANDERED THE bTOLEN At)NEICAT NEWTON._</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day morninz, before Messrs. J. Cro3field (in the chair), J Ball and Claude Morris, Joh* Morrison was charged with stealing on the 22nd May, £9 in gold, the property of Henry McSawley,—Prosecutor stated he waa a collier, and lodged at 47, Clarence-street, Newton. Prisoner also lodged there. On the date named witness had £9 in eold in his box, and it was safely locked up. The next morning he found the box broken open and the money gone.—Mary Gormery, living at the same house, said that prisoner was her brother and was one of her lodgers. She saw the box on the 22nd May, and it was all right when she left the house at 2 p.m. There were her daughter and prisoner in the hOLISO then. Whee she returned home prisoner had gone.—Margaret Gormery, aged seven,daughter of last witness, said that when her mother went out her uncle and herself were left in the house. Her uncle sent her out for a pint of beer. She was away about ten min 3tes, and when she got home he was not there.—P.C. earke, stationed at Newton deposed to receiving privner from the Orms- kirk police on the 28th May. On b-iae charged, prisoner replied that be was sorry for what he had done, hut he had not taken the money out of the box.— Prisoner repeated that he was very sorry. If they would give him time he would repay all he had takon. He had squandered the money.—The Chairman: In drinking? Prisoner: Yes.—The Chairman said there was no ex- cuse at all for him. He would have to go to gaol for three months' hard lab3our.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Councillor Smethnrst and Mr. J2_llepherd. .</p>
        <p>ASSAULT AND THREATS—EIIen Harnblett was charged with assaulting Martha Massey, on the 23rd May and Amos Hamblett was charged with using threats to Mrs. Massey on the same date.—Mr. Browne, who represented Mrs. Massey. said the complainant and defendants lived next door to each other in Richardson's Row, Bank Quay. In consequence of Mrs. Matsey giving some information to the police one day, she had been subjected to much a..noyance by the de- fendants on the night in question. Mrs. Massey was putting her child to bed and heard some one come in the yard and shout. If you don't come down those b --stairs I'll come and pull your b—liver out.' It was Mr. Hamblett. Mrs. Massey went down stairs, and the female defendant then struck her across the face. Complainant sent for her husband who was in the signal box close by. Mrs. Hatnblett then got hold of complainant and knocked her against the shutters. —Martha Massey, wife of Mr. Massey, signalman at Sankey Bridges, gave evidence in support of this statement. —Fred Rigby, 275, Liverpool-road, also gave evidence, and in addition, Mr. Massey and his son.—The male defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months in one surety of £5, and ordered to pay the casts, or in default 14 days ; and the s.emale defendant was fined lOs including costs or seven MONDAY.—Before Mr. Alderman Sutton (in the chair). Alder- man Blaolchurst, Councillors Shaw Green and William Pierpoint, and Messrs Geddes, Birchall, and Samuel Wright. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—The following were fined for this offence .—Oatherine Coyne, 11, Dial-street.sB. and costs; Mary McLoughlin, 12. Haydock-street, ss. and costs; Kate Morgan, Silver-square, 58 and costs. OTHER OFFENCES.—John Houghton la, Phillip-street. and Joseph Cullen, 14, Blue Coat-street, were fined 2s 6d each for tossing with coins on the previous Monday night on the information of P C Whittaker.—Fred Whitfield, farmer, Hulme, was fined the costs (5s 6d) for having an unlighted lamp on the 31st May.—Fred Shaw. 50. Gaskell-street, and Herbert Houghton. butcher. 89. Laira-street, were bound over for committing a breach of the peace by fighting In Sankey-street. on the 30th May. FAST CYCLlNG.—Richard Blanchard, carter, o! 72 Aikin- street, was summoned for riding a bicycle at a furious rate at 4.30 on Saturday, May 27th. in Froghall-lane.—Detective Fred. Roper said he saw defendant riding a bicycle at a rate of about 18 miles an hoar. In turning a corner he went over the footpath and fell, smashing his rnachlue.—A county police officer who happened to be in the street at the time corrobora- ted.—The Chairman, in fining defendary. 2s 6,1 (including costs), said it was a very dangerous practice riding furiously through the streets. Some steps would have to be taken to put a stops to this kind of thing. He asqed the police to keep a sharp look out after offenders. It was really dangerous at times to cross z,ankey-street and other streets.—Mr Birchall said that was so WIREDRAWER'S STRANGE CONDUCT.—An elderly and re- spoctably dressed man named Thomas Monks, wiredrawer, of 3.% Milner-street, was charge 1 with having been drunk and disorderly in Sankey-street, and also with having as- saulted Albert Clare, 'bus conductor, at 12-10 on Saturday,— P.C. Johnson gave evidence.—Albert Clare said at 11-40 he was at the Black Horse and prisoner got into the 'bus and al- so some ladies. Prisoner caught hold of their hands and tried to kiss them,but witness stopped him. When they had reached the Woolpack Hotel prisoner struck him in the mouth unawares, and it bled for some time.—Thomas Green, 12, Orford-lane, said he saw prisoner on Saturday getting Out of the 'bus, and Clare storped him from falling. He then struck him in the mouth.—Superintendent Monaghan said</p>
      </div>
