      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>To MOTSERS.—Mrs. Winslow's Sooting Syrup has been need for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their children while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste : it produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain and the littk cherub awakes "as bright as a button." Of all Chemists is lid per bottle. —Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SERIOUS RESULTS OF A. COA.CEIXTAN'S ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS RESULTS OF A. COA.CEIXTAN'S ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>A SOMEWHAT serious accident which occurred to an in- habitant of Wilsford, near Salisbury, some eighteen Months Sißce, has bean followed by results that the " Wilts County Mirror" reports. A representative to the journal named found at Wileford House, the charm - ing seat of Mr. A. Newell, that gentleman's had coachman, Hr. A. Holbourn, who was the victim of the accident referred to. Mr. Holbourn, appearing none the worse for the accident, said he was only too pleased to be able to make the facts public property. "Some eighteen months ago," he said, "I was thrown whilst out riding, and received a nasty wound in the hip. I saw a chemist wire gave me some medicine, but as this seemed to do me no good I consulted another. This gentleman gave ma the rather chilling information that I had a diseased hip and had better consult a doctor as soon as possible. lat once proceeded home and as I got no better a doctor was called in. Sciatica, however was the name given to my complaint—not hip disease— at this stage, and although the doctor histerel the leg and tried his best to effect a cure,l appeared to be going from bad to worse, and, as I had a wife and family to keep, the situation looked a serious one. I had been un- able to attend to my duties for some months and I was afraid I should have to resign my post." Interposing, the reporter asked how it was he was Looking so fit, an i well then. Mr. Holbnuro, continuing, said he was about to add that things had almost come to his resigning his situa- tion when his wife persuaded him to try some of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. He did so and he WAS simply astonished at the:result. After taking one box he was able to ,get on his legs, and within a few weeks, much to the surprise of his employer, was able to re- turn to his work.</p>
        <p>Continuing,Mr. Holbourn addel he had given the pills a good trial.had been at work constantly for nearly a twolvemlnth past and felt not the slightest symptom of the accident to his hip. The cure was not a temporary one a. he had first thought it might have been, but a radical cure.</p>
        <p>The last remark is very charscteristic of the re suit obtained by the remedy to which Mr. Holbourn wes so much. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills do not produce a temporary result like ordinary medicine, which acts on the symptoms and effects of disease. They cure, on the contrary, by the rational method peculiar to them- selves, of removing the cause of disease, and thus give permanent cures in many disorders of the system, which by ordinary medicine would be treated as diseases in themselves instead of the mere indications of deeper seated injury. From this will be perceived by the intelligent reader the danger of substitutes, which are semetimes offered by retail tradesmen te customers whom they think it easy to deceive. A substitute is all profit for the seller, and hence is of;en pushed. When such are offered it is b3st to write to Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn-Vis.duct,London, enclosing the price—two shillings and ninepence. The genuine pills always bear the full name,in seven words,Dr. Willi ams' Prxx PILLS FOR PALE PEoPLE,and among the things they have cured may be named paralysis, rheumatism, sciatica, indigestion, heart palpitations, anaemia, con- sumption, and ailm&lt;mts of ladies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>OUR LOCA.L-CHETE.CHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCA.L-CHETE.CHES.</p>
        <p>GOSPEL HALL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, Mr. Wilhan B—e—es—le—y. of Bolton preached from Rom. 1, 16,19, pointing out two revelations, one was wrath,the other righteoueness, both from God. God is just and righteous and will ever remain so, yet full of mercy ; the Cross of Christ is the only piece where he can be a just God and yet a Saviour. He first revealed his wrath against sin in turning Agam out of Eden. Again at the Flood, at the Tower of Babel, often against the sin of the Israelites but especially when He poured out upon Christ on the Cross in the sinners stead, the wrath due to man's sin. God reveals his wrath against sin daily, magistrates dispense it. His reason for reveal- ing wrath is to make man understand the exceeding sin- fulness of sin. Feople deny the atonement and the divinity of Christ because they have no consciousness of their sinfulness and need of a Saviour. God reveals His righteousness in the Gospel. A righteousness in which the sinner is clothed and fitted for Heaven. Christ died for our sins rose again for our justification and thus God can be a jut God and a Saviour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S. A. SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S. A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The meeting of this society on Sunday was presided over by Mr. George Wallington. In the course of a short interesting address he r3ferred to tle Pace Con- ference at the Hague and spoke upon the imp-rtance of the dmision in favour of arbitration. It was an exellent rule to "arbitrate befors you fight " and he was glad that the principle was being adopted in lab -air disputes. Recently he was at Birmingham in connection with the dispute in the building trade and he was pleased when both employers and employees expressed their will- ingness to discuss together to points of diffarence between them. It was far better to arnrate before a conflict than after it. Mr Bet's of Manchester delivered an interesting address on " Living to Purpose." Miss Rothwell was the soloist and she gave an efficient render- ing of" 0 Divine Redeemer" and "I know that my Redeemer Liveth." The devotional exercises were led by Mr Wm; Beswick. Mr Thos Wallington referrsd te the arrangemeats which had been made for the Foresters' Celebration on the following Sunday when the mernb'rs would attmd the P.S.A. meeting and be address3d by the Chief Ranger. THE PEACE CONFERENCE.—IS IT PEACE? OR IS IT WAR?</p>
        <p>The above subject was dealt with at the Christa- delphian-room, Bold-street, on Sunday,by Mr. E. Ogden of Warrington, who stated that there would be no hesit- ancy in giving an answer to the question by any of his hearers who were conversant with the Scriptures for the testimony contained therein was plain and emphatic and if they believed the Bible they must answer that whatever the immediate result of the Conference might be, war, and that on a scale never witnessed before, must shortly break out on the Continent. The position of political matters was precisely what students of prophecy had long expected it would be, im- mediately prior to Armageddon. The summons iseced more than two thousand years ago to the Gentile nations to get ready for this war,had been, and was being fulfilled to-day. The nations were yield- Leg implicit obedience to the divine command. "Pre- pare war, wake up the mighty men, let all the men of war draw near, let them come up. Beat your plough- shares into swords, and your pruning hooks into spears: let the weak say, lam strung." There could not be a clearer fulfilment of this prophecy than that which they saw around them at the present time, and there was a divine hand behind it all for God was mustering the forces of the Gentiles for the great day of his wrath,and the day of the deliverance of his people. The Bible pre- dicted a time of peace as well as a time of war, but the world's great peace would not came till after the world's great war. The judgments of the Almighty would teach the people righteousness, and they would then have rulers who would be uninfluenced by popular clamour or by vested interests' and who would not tolerate any of the degrading practices which now debased mankind. God invited them to His King- dom and glory, to the honour and glory and immor- tality of this coming aee of blessedness and power, and they were urged to "Seek first "—before all other things "the Kingdom of God," and that righteousness which would qualify them to enter into it, so that they might be associated with Jesus in all the splendour of his reign when the law goes forth from Zion, and he is enthroned lathe city of the great Bing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held on Wednesday morning. Mr. A. J. Jolley presided, and there were also present: Mrs Clay,Mrs Edelsten, Rev WII Drummon•J, Messrs T H Ashcroft, T B Malkin, J Clarke, J Dignum, William Houghton, G Pilling, A Brooks, El Thornton, A Jolley, FI Hibbert, William Hickman, T Halton. with Mr J C Sutton (clerk). Mr S Sutton (deputy clerk), Mr Ward (workhouse master), Mr Wright (superiatenddnt Padgate Schools). and Mr Owes (architect).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>HOLIDAY QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE HOLIDAY QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The House C ommittee reported that Mr. Brobbin, the engine tenter was allowed seven d tvs' le tv 3 0 aosence aad his wages during that period,he finding a substitute. Nurses Maybrick and Please tendered their resignations which were accepted with regr3t. It was agreed that they should be awarded a good testimonial for past. ser- vices. An application havin;.! been made by the ma,.ris- trates of the /Inoue' of Warrington that juvenile offenders might be sent for t ?inPora.ry detention to the Union Workhouse pending their removal to a refor- matory instead of being seat t 3 a county gaol, recom- mended that this request be anciedei to. Toe question of holidays of taff inuring the present summer was con- sidered and it was recommended that they be as under : master,schoolmistress and superintendent nurse each 14 days; other nurses each 12 days, Mrs. Griffin, porter and port:cress each 10 days to be taken when convenient and at the discretion of the Master ; Dobson, Greenway and Powell each seven days and wages to be paid, the baker finding a substitute. The CIikIRMAN proposed the adoption of the minute 3 and Mr. Clarke seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. MALKIN said that the baker and engine tmter had both been faithful servants to the Board, one for six years and the other for tea, and had saved the rate- payers a large amount of money. He moved that their wages be allowed as well as their holidays, puting them on the same footing as the other officers. The CHAIRMAN : The recommendation is that their wages be paid. You perhaps wish to move that the wages of their substitutes to be paid as well. Mr. MALKIN replied in the affirmative. He thought the case of Mr. Brobbin very bard. He Was getting 34s weekly, and had been in the habit of getting his on to do his work while he was away. His son had now got a -good situation, and he (Mr. Brobbiu) would have to engage the services of a mechanic and pay him two guineas. He (Mr. Malkin) only asked that Mr. Brobbin's usual wages should be paid. He was a man who worked 60 hours per week, and was working the other Sunday when the speaker casually visited the piece.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W.H. DRUMMOND secondcd the amendment. He brought the matter forward at the meeting of the House Committee, and was defeated on it. He was glad Mr. Malkin had brought it up now. It only applied to two servants. All the others got their holiday without cost to themselves. A public body ought to be model employers and be generous. Tun extra cost on the rates would be very small, and if the servants were granted this they would do their work all the better for it. There was another matter in the minutes he did not agree with, but he would not move an amendment. He did not consider the nurses' holidays were adequate or in accordance with the scale of holidays allowed to nurses in general hospitals. There had been a slight increase from 10 to 12 days this year, and that was an instalment in the right direction. Mr. Amenorr supported the amendment. Taese ser- vants eheuld be put on the same footing as others. Mr. HIBDERT : Is it proposed that we should pay two guineas ? Mr. PILLING • Only the ordinary wages. . . .</p>
        <p>„ Mr. HinnEnT : I !Ave ploasure in supporting. I wish they could be given 14. days' holiday. Mr. CLARKE : I shall certainly vote against the amendment.</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKDIAN said tee matter was tharouglaly thrashed out at the meeting of the committee, and the decision of the committee was perfectly unanimous. It was only because a certain member could not carry his point that he said he would bring it up at the Board meeting. Mr. MALKIN : He has a right to do so. Mr. HICKMAN said that with regard to the nurses, the general arrangements were at present in a transient state. They would be getting into the new Infirmary shortly, and would then put the whole of the nursing staff on a proper basis. He thought it was quite as well that things should remain as they were at present. He would support the House Committee. Mr. PILLING supported the amendment. He did not think it was much of a holiday when the men had to pay for it. The CHAIRMAN thought the committee arrived at a very wise decision by a majority of ten to two. With regard to what had been said regarding the action of the committee being unfair, be would like to say that he dienot accept that statement. He ventured to say that the'Guardians treated the &amp;Room with fairness and con- sideration all round. . . -</p>
        <p>Eight voted for the amendment and eight against. The Chairman gave his cAsting vote against, and the amendment was therefore lost. The minutes were then confirmed.</p>
        <p>Tne CLERIC pointed out that in the case of most of the offic.rs their work was not interfered with by holidays. As Clerk of that authority he had to pull up any arrears of work when he came back. It was the same with other officers, but that did not apply to the two men whose holidays and pay the Board had been considering. There was another matter which should not be lost sight of, When the master or matron or an indoor officer was away they forfeited the value of their food although they were still held responsible for the work. It was no loss to the Union but on the centrary it was a gain be- cause the expmse of their maintenanc3 was borne by themselves in their absence.</p>
        <p>Mr. CLARKE called attention to the resignation of two nurses and said a great many seemed to Le leaving. The CHAIRMAN said in neither of the cases in question was their resignation due to any friction or their work. One of thn nurses was going tl a hosoital to qualify her- self tor the position of a superintendent nurse and the other was going to take up private nursing instead of institutional work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>GIFTS.</head>
        <p>GIFTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WARD (workhouse !ulster) replrted the receipt of a parcel of illustrated papers for the inmates and two boxes of tennis balls for the chil lren from Mr. J. Cros- field, Walton lila, and flowers from the Rev. W. H. Drummond, Cairo-street Chapel.</p>
        <p>THE HEATING AND HOT WATER SERVICE OF THE NEW INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gomm read the minutes of the Infirmary Commit- tee which recommended the acceptance of the tender of Messrs. KiHick and Cochrane, of Liverpool, for heating and hot water service. Mr: WILLIAM HOUGHTON moved that the minutes of the committee be printed and circulated amongst the Guardians before the next; Board meeting. Mr. PILLING secorded, and the resolution was cll.- red.</p>
        <p>Mr. OWEN Slid betore the Board decided upon the ac ceptance of M ars, %Mick and Cochrane's tender he would like to read them a letter be hld received since the last meeting of the committee. The firm stated in the communication that owing to the continual gre&amp;t increase in the cost of materials, especially in copper, they were unable to undertake the work at the tender submitted some time ago. They must now ask for the price to be increased to £1,750. Mr. THORNTON said there had been undue delay</p>
      </div>
