      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL AND MINING NEWS.</p>
        <p>RHODESIAN RAILWAY. EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>According to the latest despatch from Bultiw—ayo, the first turf of the railway extension for the further open- ing up of Rhodesia was turned on May 30th last. In addition to this highly satisfactory news, there corers the'statement made by the Chairman of the Austen (Gwele) Development Syndicate, Limited, at the first general meeting of the shareholders, held on Thursday, June Ist, that the first set of claims belonging to the Syndicate had been disposed of to the Ninety Mile Pro- prietary Company, Limited, on very favourable terms, as was foreshadowed in our issue last week. As the total capital of the Ausi-en (Gwelo) Syndicase is only £50,600, in shares of £1 each fully paid, this sale, and others that are being negotiated, should enable the syndicate to pay handsome dividends on thir shares, which are now dealt in on the London Stock Exchange WEST AUSTRALIA. at anboAuNtNLA2Nss. ASSOCIATED,. De, The attention of the speculative investor, on toe look out for low-priced shares in West Australian companies whose properties are favourably situated for striking the rich lodes -contained in the Great Boulder, Ivanhoe, and Lake View Mines, should be directed to the £1 fully paid shares of the Hannan's Associated Mines, Limited, which are now dealt in oe the London Stcch Exchange naus withH an an a' at about 103 each. This company owns 11 mining leases, 8 iereFaoldf about 228 acres, situated on the fa ,Kalgoorlie, only 160 chains south of the Great Boulder, and Lake View Mines. Mr. W. G. Brockman, the discoverer of the Great 'Boulder and other successful West Australian mines, states in his re- port as follows : " You have an extremely valuable combination of leases, which will, I have no doubt,prove to contain tha Great Boulder Main Lodes, the Ivanhoe Main Lodes, and others, and probably the Lake View Lode. I congratulate yeti on securinf,r it, and I have no hesitation in recommending this prowrty as one which will yield handsome dividends."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>G RAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>G RAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>pig SACRILEOE AT GRAPPENHAL CM:MM.—At the Warrington Occasional Cocrt, on Monde y. before Mr. A. Talbot, John Daley was charzed with having broken into Grappenhall Church. on the 30th May, and stealing the money from the offertory boXee.—Jamea Mainwaring, sexton, stated that at 8 o'clock he went to the church and saw that the boxes were all right. Re then went int a tae belfry and was there until about nine o'clock, when he came down and locked the church. Next mornine he went to the church and found that the north door was slightly open, wtrich he had left shut the night before. Hs also found that the offertory boxel had been opened and the contents taken. fie gave information to the police.--Charles Peacock, farmer, of Grapnenhall, sail he was passing the church about 8-15 n rn., when he caw prisoner go into the churchyard. He turned hie head awey for a Mo" ment and when he looked again the priaoner wag gone. —Sergeant Goaling said ho received prisoner in custody from the Meager palice. He charged him with having feloniously entevd the chureh.—Priaoner mai,le no reply to the charge, and was remanded until the 13:h iestant.</p>
      </div>
