      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Katers to tbe C-Ditor.</head>
        <p>Katers to tbe C-Ditor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. .11utliDrs et letters intended for publication must in at cues send their names and addresses to the Editor. Matters intended for publication must be written on one side only ot the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>THE DAY OF REST: MORE PROFANATION, ,1 Sir,—Until a fortnight ago the rural districts of Pad- I gate-lane and Fearnhead enjoyed a fair immunity from Sun lay traffic. The milk carts were, of course, always in evidence: men and. women cyclists were to be seen on the highway pursuing their peculiar pastime ; and now and then the rumbling of a vehicle heavily freighted with pleasure seekers would grate harshly on the ear. But to these drawbacks we have now, unfortunately, to add another and more disturbing one. A regular line of omnibuses is now plying between the Central Station and Fearnhead Cross. The running of these 'buses from Bin the morning till between 11 and 12 at night is no mill nuisance to many of the dwellers in the quieter parts of the route owing to the noise and dust which they ereate. All this, however, might have been 'once with if the promoters of the line had kept their hands off the Sabbath. But they have not seen their way to do that, and so the Day of Rest, is now flagrantly outraged for nine or ten hours in the districts mentioned. As the ancient and Divine Law which pro- hibits every kind of trafficking on the Sabbath day is well-known I need not use it here to combat the new departure in profanation. But I may be permitted to occupy a lower place and denounce the traffic on the ground of cruelty to men and animals. The drivers and conductors engaged in the traffic drag out a hard life. Through all weathers,in cold and heat and in rain and snow they are obliged to be at their posts for fifteen or sixteen hours every working day. But it is not the drivers and guards only who suffer through the Sunaay traffic ; the noble and patient animals in their charge share a full measure of the wrongs and oppression to which they are subjected. When the Day of Rest comes round they too are Divinely entitled to cease from their labours. But unfortunately for them their fate on that day is to drag multitudes of pleasure seekers through the crowded streets and along the highways until a late hour in the night. It was said of old that a merciful man is merciful to his beast. But the 'bus horse knows little or nothing of that heavenly quality ; and the illtreatment which it so patiently endures is all the more cruel inasmuch as it has no vice to utter its wrongs and appeal for justice. 'Three hundred years ago an English juige wrote : " A Sunday well spent Brings a week of Content. Ani health for the toils of the morrow ; But a Sabbath profaned, Whatso'er may be gained. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>.. – Is a certain forerunner of sorrow."</head>
        <p>.. - Is a certain forerunner of sorrow."</p>
        <p>If the view here expressed is correct, and I believe it is, how discourasing is the outlook for the future of this country ! We may boast of our wealth and glory in our fleets and armies, but the time will assuredly c)me when the Judge of all the earth will reckon with us for the way in which we have oppressed the poor and violated His holy day. No nation ever did or ever will enjoy true prosperity and happiness where Go iis dis- honoured and men are cruel and unjust to their fellow creatures, and to the dumb animals which serve them so faithfully.—Yours truly, lONA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>1899 LIBERALISM.</head>
        <p>1899 LIBERALISM.</p>
        <p>Sir.—As one who during the troublous years from 1885 to 1889 has fought no less than six contests, as the s?lec ted candidate of the Local Liberal-Radical Associations, may I be allowed to ask the following questions? (1) So much haying been done at the instigation of, and by the sacrifice of, the Liberal Party in order to secure " Justice to Ireland" is it still necessary tkat Borne Rule on the basis of " Ireland a Nation "shalt r 3 - main the stumbling-block to Liberal Re-union ? (2) As the vital interests of the commercial, whole- sale, and retail trading, manufacturing and wage- earning classes, are fatly embodied in Liberal principles. is it essential that the ce-operation of the Irish Party shall be aeain purchased at the costly pr:ce of Liberal Dig-union? _</p>
        <p>_ (3) Is it not a fact that there are more Liberals who are rendered opposed to or apathetic towards Liberal success through anticipation of Home Rule (on the basis of "Ireland a Nation ") being used as a stepping- stone to separation than there are, at the present time, Irish voters who will oppose Liberalism on the giounda that their earlier demands have not been fully granted 9</p>
        <p>(4) 1g not the general advancement of Liberal prin- ciples of such paramount importance t 9 all potions cf trade and labour that the total abstinence party should, in the present state of pubiic opinion, be content to moderate their demands rather than by parsistent in- sistence on the whole, imperil the lezisla,tive promotion of temperance principles a.nd create apathy tlwards other Liberal measurea P—Your obedient servant.</p>
        <p>JAMES B AYSMAN. (Lihera: Candate Brentford 1885-6, St. George's West 1887, Mile End 1892-5). Enchley-road, Hampstead, London. N.W•</p>
        <p>"HONOUR TO WHDNE HONOUR IS DUE." Sir,—ln reply to the letter of " On Looker " upon the above subject, permit me to state that there was no question of honour involved. I was asked a plain ques- tion, and I gave a straightforward answer from the facts as I knew them. That these fact i are true is at- tested by the letter of" Onlooker "himself. He quotes my statement that there was no spiritual work carried</p>
        <p>on at the Y.M.C. A. when I cams and he concludes by stating that there was—previous to my arrival. Where comes in his grievance ? I fail myself to see any. In conclusion I may remark that the work of the Y.M.C.A, suffers from a plethora of "Onlockers "and it would be better if these peotole, instead of looking on, would ren- der practical assistance.—Yours truly, ALBERT E. WINDLE, General Secretary, Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>PERMITTING DRUNKENNESS AT WESTON.</p>
        <p>A LANDLORD FINED-EXTRAORDINARY EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>At the Runcern Police Sessions, on Monday, before Messrs. James Handley (presiding), W Timmins' and John Ockleston, George Walters, licensee of the Royal Oak Hotel, Weston,was summoned, at the instance of the police for permitting drunkenness on his licensed premises,on the Bth of May.—The proceedings were of a protracted character, and occupied the court from about noon to 0 p.m., with half an hour's adjournment. —Mr. Fletcher, Northwich, prosecuted, and Mr. A. A. Tobin, barriater,Liverpool (instructed by Mr. J. Burton, solicitor, Runcorn) defended. Mr.Fleteher,in opening the case, said, it seemed that the house was in the neighbeurhood of the Weston Canal, and the persons who were alleged te have been found on the premises intoxicated were boat-people em- ployed on the vessels that ply on the canal. On the Bth of May. he would proceed to prove, that at 12.15 Joseph' Marson, his wife, and a man named Millington, entered the house in a sober condition. They remained in the house until 3.30; during which period they were con- suming beer. When they left the house at 330, the people were drunk. He would submit evidence to prove that they were in that condition whilst they were in the house, and that a police-constable had previous to that entered the house; and, although, he would not swear they were actually drunk the constable would state that the people were in such a condition as to justify his cautioning them. Edward Wellines, boatman, 22, Britland-etreet, said on the Bth of May he was in the Royal Oak. He entered the house about 12 °Week and a short time afterwards Joseph Matson, his wife, and James Millington, came in. The woman had a child with her. He saw tee boat people supplied with quarts of beer, but did not take notice as to how many quarts they had.—ln answer to the Chairman he said he was in the same part of the house.—Witness paid for his own drink, and only had two pints. He left the house at 3-30. Whilst he was in the house he heard the "servant" say that they—the Marson party—had had enough. The people appeared to be all right as far as he ceuld say and were not drunk. On coming out the woman fell down. Marson was drunk and the woman tumbled.—Mr. Fletcher: You understand my question whether the woman was drunk or not ?—Witness: She was drunk, but Marson seemed to be all right and took the baby from me.—Witness saw the woman was not fit to wry the baby, so he took the baby from her. Millington seemed all right. As he was not going in the sante dire ction,he (witness) gave the baby up to Millington, and he took the baby aboard. Mrs. Marson was outside when she fell. She got up herself and walked down the brow un- til opposite Mr. Tudor's house when she fell a second time, and seratehed her head. The boat was lying at the Soapery.—Cross-ex trained by Mr. Tobin : Witness said he judged the woman was the worst because she could not find her legs. He had not known the woman for long ; but he did know that she was a woman that was subject to epileptic fits,which was a fact well known among the boat people. He remembered asking Marson " whether the woman was in a fit at the time of the second fall ?" Oa seeing her fall it was his first thought that she was in a fit.—Counsel: What made you think it was a fit, if what you have sworn was true, that she was unable to find her legs because ebe was drunk ? Witness: She tumbled again and lay like one that was dead.—At the time she fell did you think the woman drunk 1)-1 thought she was drunk and had taken a fit in drink.—Continuing witness admitted assisting the woman whilst she was in the fit. He re- membered the policeman coining to the public-house and the constable said to Marson who was singing "This is not a place to sing in you must stop your singing." It would be about 2-30 when the police constable came in but he did not take any notice of that. John Trelfer, waterman, residing in Gas-street, Run- corn, stated that he was in the Royal Oak with the boat yeople. He left the hotel at half-past two at which time the men and the woman were drunk. Mrs. Marson tried to get up from her seat, but was so drunk that she fell back. The officer when he came in said to Marson "Hold your noise ; it is not a dancing or singing saloon" , and Marson stopped the singing whilst the officer was in but restarted it after he left. They were all drinking together and whilst he was there he saw four quarts served and just as he was leaving at 230 he saw another quart coming in. Witness paid for his own drink. He had heard Miss Clay. say" she would fill them no more drink." He (witness) had no doubt, whatever,but that the men and the woman were drunk.—The Police-Constable had stated : " Do not fill these parties any more drink : they are all drunk," add- ing "I will report it." On being re-examined by Mr. Fletcher witness reverted to his original statement made in the examination in chief. He also stated that he paid for a quart for the boat people so as to entice them to go to their boats.</p>
        <p>Annie Richardson, married woman, of Ford Bank Ter- race, testified to seeing the people come out of the Royal Oak in a drunken condition. She saw the people from her door step. She lived abut 30 yards from the hotel, and when the people came to within about 3 yards of her house, the woman fell ; but scrambled up again. Her husband remarked to her that she' would be satisfied now that she was drunk."</p>
        <p>Mr. Tobin objected to this as evidence, and some con- siderable legal argument was indulged in by both aelici- tors.</p>
        <p>The Chairman subsequently decided to only take as evidence any act that was connected by the peaple out- side the public house that would throw light upon their condition; and to ignore any conversation that might have taken place in the absence of the def )ndant. Continuing, Mr. Richardson stated she was sure the woman was drunk, but did not take any notice of the men.</p>
        <p>M's. Preece, Campiny's Raw, Wet)n, testifi3d in a similar strain.</p>
        <p>James Tudor, manager of the Weston Worke.etated he saw the woman lying in the gutter aboat 200 or 300 yards away from his house. At the time he was com- ing trom Weston Point. The time would be about 4 30. The woman was lying in the gutter ; her face was bleed- ing and bruised. He walked up to her, though in the first instance he considered she was dead. She was ly- ing with her head down hill and her clothes very much disarranged. He put her clothes over her with his foet. Turning round he saw the woman's husband about 50 yards away. Asked what he considered the state nf the woman to be with regard to sobriety witness said he should consider she was in a beastly etate of intoxica- tion. He went into hie house and changed his coat and on coming out about five minutes afterwards saw Mill- ine,ton:and the husband Musing the woman.—Mr. Tobin: again objected and remarked that the licensee was res- ponsible for what took place in his house. Mr. Fletcher contented that it was material that the condition of the people should be shown ; and that it was only by their acts that it was possible to judo) whether they were drunk or sober.—Mr. Tobin ' disagreed. He stated it was a well-known fact that a person might get soaked in bear and never show any ' symptoms of drunkenness until either the sun and air affected him. After some further discussion the witness continued. He saw the woman there and saw Milling- ton and the husband come up. The woman was shouting and swearing. The two men had hold of her by a leg each and were pulling her up the hedge cep. The woman could not walk and they were pulling and maul- ing her about. Witness sent a messenger for the police. All that time witness was only 20 yards away, and was in a position to judge of their sobriety. Beth men and the woman were drunk. He went down to the works after sending for the police. About half an , hour afterwards the two men and the woman walked down t 3 the canal bank. It would then be as near five o'clock as noseible. He had just been speaking to Watson. There were three narrow boats in the canal abreast of one another. The woman got across the first boat to go aboard her own boat which was the outside one, and after two or three unsuccessful attempts, fell into the canal. She hung on to the side of the boat, and was up to her waist in water. The husband stood there and took no notice, and said let her drown. Witness rescued the woman from the canal.—Cr )83-examined : W.tness did net think it necessary to go and interfere or to help the wemen. Heel be had some one with him he would have gone. He thought the woman was being shoekingly ill- treated. Out he went down to his business.</p>
        <p>Thomas Watson. foreman at Weston Works, and re- siding at Norman-road, Runcorn, gave corroborative evidence as to the condition of the people on the canal bank.—P.C. Wylie stated that he visited the Royal Hotel about two o'clock in consequence of a man singing, or what appeared to be drunken brawl going on. He gave corcob)rative evidence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tobin for thi;-ciTelence contended that the people were net drunk. The woman was subject te fits and he submited that it was upon the fact that the woman had fallen down that the attention of people was called to the party. It might just as easily have been a fit that the woman fell into and he would call evidence. George Walters (licensee)was cal/ed,and stated he was manager to Mr. Jones. On the Bth May witness's wife V 744 ill, and he went to Runcorn for Dr.McKeozie. He returned with the doctor about one o'clock. He left the doctor with his wife and went into the bar, where he served Marson and his wife, and Millington, with a qaart or beer. Witness had not heard any drunken voices in the meantime. He heard the musical box go- ing in the bar. Witness's wife was seriously ill, and the doctor came to administer morphia. Marson was a rouh sort of man,but their was nothing in his conduct to cause witness to intervene.—Cross-examined : He told thelbarmaid to serve the people with one more quart and no more. He then went upstairs into the far kitchen.—Sarah Clays, barmaid in the employ of the defendant, said she had been at the house eight months. There was a musical box in the bar—a penny-in-the-slot arrangement—and Marson sang to amuse himself. The policeman said if he could not have a glass of beer with- out making a less noise, he must go outside. The men were not drunk. The policeman did not speak to wit- ness at all. He did not caution her about serving more liquor to these men. Mr. Waters cautioned witness about serving them with too much drink, and after that she served them with another quart. Masson and his wife did not show any signs ot being drunk. They</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.— Before Mr. T. J,Giilespie (in the chair), and</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Jackson.</p>
        <p>MI9CELLANIOUR OFFENCEi.—The following were fined for being drunk —Edward Grady, Newton, 27th May, 55 and costs ; Mary Murray. 27th May, Newton, 25 6cl and costs; RO3O King, Newton. 27th May. 23 C 1 and costs ; and William Green, Golborne, 27th May,2s 6d and costs.—Abrahani Harri• son and Henry Gough were fined 53 and costs and 25 6d and costs respectively for being drunk add disorderly at Newton. Willuim Wain was tined 35 6d including costs, for allowing his dog to be at large.—For playing at" all fours" with ca-ds on the 28th May, John Roe was fined Is and costs ; Thomas Leo 21 6d and costs; and John Edward RO3O 2s 61 and costs.</p>
        <p>- DESERTION —William Smith was charged with being a deserter front H.R.'s ship" Jupiter."—Defendant pleaded guilty.—P.o Benson said he anprehended prisoner in Suez- stree, Earlestown, suspecting him to be a deserter. Witness had looked in the "Police Gazett " and found according to a description given, prisoner had deserted on th 31st July, 1897.—Tbe Beach remanded prisoner to await escort.</p>
        <p>PIG DgALEIt HEAVILY FINED.—R D Garrett, pig dealer, of Bedfordshire, was charged with removing six pigs without a license on the 26th and 27th May within an infected area without having first obtained a removal license ; and Thomas Green was charged with missing the swine to be removed without first obtaining a remoeal license—The defendant Garratt did net appear.—Mr Turner of Birmingham, repre- sented the Great Ceutral Railway Company.—Thomus Sutton, engine winder, Penny-lane, Haydock. said on the 19th May last he received a swine fever declaration from In- sventor Scott. He forwarded it to Mr Garratt and wrote a letter Pot rf rig out that Haydock was an infected area and a day aft's. he received a letter from Mr Garratt to say he was se.iding the pigs on. There was no license in existence at the time.—GeJrga West, station master at Ashton station, said he received the pigs on the 27th May. There was no licence with them. Witness knew Ashton and Haydock were infected areas. The p7gs were kept at the station until a license was ob- tained, --Ittr. Turner said he appeared on bth i alf of the rail- way company who were implicated in this case. The de- fendant Gat ratt had sent the pigs by the Great Central line to tiardock and Ashton. They had heard quite sufficient in the case to see that the Railway Company were not exactly blameless bnt it was of a different character than Garratt's. —The magistrates tined Garrett £3 for each pig and the costs and Green was fined the costs. ASSAULT ON A WOMAN.—William Burrows WAS charged with assaulting Jane Rogers on the 2nd June.—Defendant pleaded not guilty.—Mr Smith represented the complainant and Mr Fox appeared or the defendant.—Mr Smitn said the charge in this case was one of common assault bat according to his instruetions the assault was an indecent ;one. On Friday last a wav,onette as plying between Barlestown and Haydock. There were three men and four women including complainant on the v.hic e. When the wagonette tweed the Wood Pit defendant knelt down on his hands and knees and committed the assanit complained of.—Jane Rogers, 379, Church-road, Haydock, bore out her solicitor's statement.— The Bench after hearing evidence on both sides fined defend- ant 20s and costs.</p>
        <p>THEFT OF FOWLS.—Thomas and Blizabeth Monaghan %were charged with stealing two hens and one cock from a hen-cote on the 4th June.—Both defendants pleaded not guilty.— Martha Clarke. 24, Blac tbrook-road, rtaydock, said about 7.30 on the 3rd inst. her poultry were safe. On the following morning the door of the cote was open and the fowls were missing. They were value Bs. The cock and the wing of the hen produced were her propertv.—Ann Oritchley stated that about 12.33 on the 4th of this month she saw the female defendant empty a bucket ha her (witness's) ashpit.- Simon Critclaley also gave evidence to the same effect. He watched hercome back across the fields. He went with a man named Atherton to the spot from where she had come and found the wing of a fowl.—Mrs. Baines Rave corroborative evidence. —E. C. Hearn, stationed at Haydock, said ha searched prisoner's house and found some feathers on the kitcheu. door and some in the ash bucket in the pantry. The two prisoners were present, so he apprehended them and charged them. They both replied "Yes."- The bench fined them 103 and costs or 14 days each.</p>
