      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 9, 1999.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 9, 1999.</p>
        <p>were supnlied with sixpenny beer.—Cross-examined : Marson paid her twice for drink. He paid for a quart after the officer had been in.—Peter Darlington, engine driver, John Ray, fireman, Weston, and William Darlington gave similar evidence. The police- man came at one o'clock and witness left at 1.30. —Joseph Marson said he went to the Royal Oak at 12.30 and left at 2 30. He remembered the policeman coming in. He wanted to know what the noise was about ;he said singing was not allowed there. Witness told him it should be stopped. After they left the house they went in tte direction of Weston Works. When they had , gone about 80 yards his wife said "Joe, I'm going to I be bad ; get hold of the baby." James Millington took the child. It was not true that his wife was wheeled in a barrow. When his wife fainted he did his test for her. Witness was not drunk when he was in the house. His wife was subject to fits from her birth, and she had been attended by Dr. McKenzie. None of the party I were drunk.—Cress-examined : The Royal Oak was the place where he had his drink. He paid for four quarts of beer; they had six quarts altogether. He pleaded guilty before the magistrates because he was told they would take the word of an officer sooner than a man. The police did not tell him that. He saw Mr. Tudor at 3.30. His wife was getting across the boat when she fell into the water. He went to get her out. He slid not say "Let her drown." He should be sorry to say that. His wife did not fall within a few yards of the public-house; it was about eight yards from Mr. Tudor's house. When his wife had the fit witness shook her, and his mate bit her ear, and "brought her to hr- self a bit."—The wife of the last witness stated that when she left the Royal Oak she had a fit. She felt she was going to be bad, and she asked someone to take her baby. She fell down in a fit. She had had these fits all her life. She did not know what happened while she lay on the road.—Cross-examined : There was only one glass, and witness took her turn with the rest. She did not knew how long she was engaged at that occupation.—Dr. McKenzie gave evidence as to attend- ing Mrs. Waters at the Royal Oak on the Bth May. He got there about one o'clock, and left about 2 30. He heard no drunken voices in the house. He saw Mamma and another man leave the house. He should not say Marson was drunk. There was no indication of drunken- ness on them. Mrs. Marson was subject to epileptic fits with premonitary symptoms. She might feel the fit coming on and topple over.—Thonsas Jones said he was a yearly tenant of the Royal Oak, and defendant Waters was the manager. Waters had been there two years. He was a paid servant, and received in addition five per cent. on all takings over £2O A week. The tak- ings as a rule were slightly over £2O. There had been no complaint against the house since he had had it.— Cross-examined : The defendant was simply his servant, and everything about the place belonged to witness, except the furniture. Witness received all the profits out of the liquor sold.—Mr. Fletcher expressed surprise at the arrangement, and the witness replied that lots of houses in Runcorn were on the same footing.—The Bench retired, and, after the absence of 15 minutes, the Chairman said they considered the prosecution had made out their case, and defendant would be fined 40s. and 48s. Bd. costs. The license would net be endorsed. They allowed an advocate's fee of two guineas.—The hearing of the case occupied nearly six hours.—Joseph Marmon. Eliza !damn, and James Millington were charged with being drunk (Ao licensed pomises on the Bth May. Evidence was given by Mrs. Richardson and P.C. Wylie. The defendants pleaded not guilty.—The police withdrew the case against Marron, and his wife and Millington were each fined ss.</p>
        <p>railing sinilax drooping artistically round the uppermost tier, it presented an imposing appear- ance. The artistic details stood out charmingly and the monograms of the happy couple could be seen nicely entwined on both sides of the centre tier. It was supplied by Mr. T. Woods, of Bold-street Liverpool, in whose hands the whole of the cater ing was entrusted, and wall carried out. LIST OF PRESENTS.</p>
        <p>Bride to Bridegroom, travelling bag Mr S Taylor, cheque Mr j R Frith. cheque Mrs Frith, glass, sewing machine, quilt, wool heartkrug, and door mats M.l and Mrs J Mason Frith, case of cutlery Miss C Taylor and Mr J H Taylor " Schiedmayer "piano Mr Ernest Frith, silver ink stand Miss Ivy and Master Arthur Frith, silver salver Master Frank Filth, afternoon tea cloth Maggie Lucy and Edward, Rydal Mount, china tea set Baker (Belle Vue), silver toast rack Annie (Rosnes.th), silver-mounted marmalade jar Annie and Pattie (Sunnyside), Rower vase and ornaments The Police of the Runcorn Petty Sessional Division Cheshire Constabulary, case of silver afternoon tea spoons Directors Frodsham Gas and Water Co., silver afternoon tea tray Employes Runcorn Gas Co , case of afternoon tea cutlery Halton-road Wesleyan Choir, oak chime clocsk Mr Arthur Riley, Misses Lillie and Annie Riley, cream sugar stand • Mr and Mrs William Hughes, fruit dish and pickle jars Mr and Mrs Weston, casket of scent Mr and Mrs Whittaker, Sheffield, silver and ivory crumb scroap Mr and Mrs F J Notman. pair of silver candlesticks Mr and Mrs James Handley, case of silver salts Mr and Mrs Posnett. ease of silver Rower vases Mr Duncan McKechnie, case of silver fruit dishes Mr J Howitt, silver tea service Mr and Mrs A R Norman, case of silver bon-bon dishes Rev R J and Mrs Clifton, silver bread fork Mr and Mrs Bate, (Tarnorley). ease of silver tea spoons Mr and Mrs Robert Collinge, water jug Mrs McLean and Miss Riley, oriental tab'e centre Mrs and Mrs W B Johnston pair of Doulton Vases The Misses Shirt, satin eiderdown The Misses Downes, case of silver salts Mr John Johnston, Halton, barometer Mr R L Armour, silver butter dish in case Mr and Mrs Booth (danchester). Doulton dinner service Mr and Mrs Sheard, silver tea and coffee service Mr A Jackson, and Miss L Booth, silver nut dish Mr Walter Booth, silver mounted jam dish Miss Booth, cream and sugar stand Misses Ada and Nellie Booth,silver muffineers and mustard in case</p>
        <p>Mr Frank F Downes, silver shaving set in case, and silver fruit dish Dr Bowman. brass inkbtand, and candlesticks in ease. Mr Francis Downes. eau-de-Calague in silver case Mr and Mrs Sam Riley, silver sugar basin Mr and Mrs Graham, brass clothes brushes on bracket Mr and Mrs Sowerby. silver egg stand Mr and Mrs Ir_av, silver tea caddy Mr and Mrs J B Chadwick, silver bread tray Mr and Mrs Soulby, pictura Mrs McWhannell, tray cloth Mr and Mrs Walter Timmins, rose bowl Mr and Mrs Allen. Case of meat and game carvers Mrs Tom Taylor. bracket Mr and Mrs Wiekstead. biscuit barrel Mrs Handley. silver-mounted j im dish Mr P Speakman, silver-mounted marmalade dish Mr and Mrs P T Leigh, 3 pictures Mr and Mrs J Horsefield, silver breakfast stand Mr William Handley. silver-mounted jam dish Mrs Brimelow, biscuit barrel Mr Stanley Norman, water colour picture Mrs Lightfoot and Miss Norman, Photo frame Mrs Thomas Handley and family, silver egg stand Mr and Mrs William Riley, clock Mr and Mrs Rowland, case of fish knives and forks Mr and Mrs Pope, cosy Mr and Mrs Beck and family, case of fish and fruit knives and forks Mr and Mrs Mount, silver spirit kettle Mr and M-8 Bellamy. barometer. gong, ke., on bracket Miss Johnson and Mr Ellison, silver pie servers in ease Mr and Mrs Dear, silver muffineers in case Dr and Mrs McLennan, bronze card stand Mr and Mni J Meadowcroft, silver table bell Mr and Mrs T D Timmins. copper fern pot Mr and Mrs Lake, celery dish on silver stand Mr and Mrs Imison, silver toast rack and brook Mrs Fielding. cake stand Mr and Mrs Fisher, silver water jug Mr and Miss Pritchard, silver crumb scoop Mr and Mrs Gleave, Crown Derby tea service Mr and Mrs Holland, inlaid oak table Mr and Mrs Cagrnan, silver potted-meat dish Mr and Mrs Barker, silver cake dish 7Mr and Mrs Tate, Crown Derby flower-pot Mr and Mrs Baker, Worcester pot pourri jar Dr and Mrs Raschen, silver butter dish In case Mr and Mrs J W Lightburn, silver tea urn Mr and Mrs Evans, biscuit barrel Mr and Mrs Cunningham. book Mr and Mrs Whittaker, silver serviette rings in Case Mr and Mrs Miles, silver tea pot and hot water jug Mr and Miss Mo:rison, Doulton celery dish Mr J A Johnson, silver table lamp Mr and Mrs J Rooker. silver-mounted scent bottles Mr and Mrs J J Rooker, jun., silver serviette rings in case Mr and Mrs Boyle, silver-mounted scent bottle Mr and Mrs A Lightburn, tomato dish Mr James `Wilding. flower and fruit stand Mr an 4 Mrs Beswick, table cloth Mr and gra Seatsee, silver breakfast cruet 111. Mr and Mrs r Riley. decanters Mr and Mrs Reid, silver-mounted magnifying glass Mr and Mrs Harold Chadwick, silver breakfast cruet Miss Pope, glass water jug and tumblers Mrs and M's Short, silver entree dishes Mr W H Pickering, silver egg stand Mrs Young (Portsmouth). Coalport cup and saucer Mr and Mrs Harry Wright, silver muffiaeers Mr and Mrs Callender, umbrella Mrs Pierpoint. silver-mounted sugar sifter Miss Fierpoint, spinning chair Mr and Mrs Wiswall, salad bowl Mrs Knowles, copper fern pat Mrs Palmer, biscuit barrel Mr and Mrs W Collier, Crown Derby afternoon tea s er- vice Mr and Mrs Poole, afternoon tea cloth Dr McDougall, silver salts in case Miss 3 McLean, celery glass Mr and Mrs Rothwell. Ash carvers Mr :..nd Mrs Layland, flower vase. Miss Bayley (1-lightoryn), jam dishes on silver stand Mr and Mrs Breeze, Acton Bridge, silver mounted china ten tray Mr and Mrs C H R Harrison, dinner gong Mr and Mrs Horace W Handley. jam dish</p>
