      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FASHIONABLE MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN. FRITH-TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>FASHIONABLE MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN. FRITH-TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>A strikingly happy function WR,9 celebrated with due solemnity at the Halton-road Wesleyan Chapel, on Wednesday afternoon last, when the marriage of Mr. George Stevenson Frith (gas engineer and uecretary of the Frodsham Gas Works) the third son of Mr. J. R. Frith, the respected manager of the Runcorn Gas Company, to Miss Janet Taylo r (second daughter of Mr. Sanuael Taylor, J.P., of_ Rydal Mount, Runcorn), the highly respected vice chairman of the Runcorn Urban District Council and undoubtedly the most popular gentleman in Runcorn, took place in the presence of a large and fashionable congregation. Indeed, the attendance of witnesses was perhaps the largest that has been seen at such a ceremony in Runcorn for very many years, a circumstance due more particularly to the popularity of the families of the contracting parties. The guests and house party alone numbered about 200.</p>
        <p>The bridegroom entered the church a little before 2-30, the time arranged for the ceremony. He was accompanie I by Mr. Frank Downes (Chester), who acted as best man, and Mr. Ernest Frith (brother)' who acEed as groomsman. Shortly after, The bride, looking exquisitely pretty in a gown of ivory satin, the front of which was tastefully trimmed with embroidery and chiffon, and wearing a full court train suspended from the shoulders, de- corated with chiffon and orange blossom, surmoun- ted with a wreath of orange blossom and white heather, entwined in her hair from which was sus- pended a full bridal veil of Brussels lace, entered the sacred edifice leaning on the arm of her father, who gave her away. She carried a bouquet of white roses, Whet; of the valley, and w te heather, her only ornament being a pearl and diamond brooch the gift of the bridegroom. She was attended by her bridesmaids: Miss Clara Taylor (sister), MRS Booth (Manchester), Miss Ivy Taylor _ _ . _</p>
        <p>(niece), and also by Master Arthur Frith (nephew) as page. The bridesmaids' dresses (Misses C. Taylor and Booth) were of cheniele trimmed with chiffon, with picture hats to match, daintily set off with plumes. They carried bouquets of heliotrope orchids. Miss Ivy Frith was attired in a dress of white silk material,andwore a large three-cornered hat with plumes. She was escorted by Master Arthur Frith whose costume was full court of ivory satin and lace, This dainty juvenile couple looked exceedingly elegant, and carried garlands of roses and lillies of the valley suspended from crooks. The bridesmaids wore pearl brooches and the page a pearl breastpin the gifts of the bridegroom. The ceremony which was fully coral, opened with the singing of "The voice that breathed o'er Eden," and was conducted by the Rev R Clifton. At its conclusion the hymn "0 Father all creating" was sung. The procession to the vestry moved off to the strains of Wagner's bridal march from Lohengria, and as the happy couple proceeded to leave the chapel after signing the register Mendels- shon's wedding march resounded through the building. On emerging into the sunlight showers of confetti deluged the new-made man and wife and it was only when the shelter of the old homesteadl received thero:that they were free from that blissfa torture. Then came the congratulations of numer- ous friends.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon Mr. Samuel Taylor and his daughter (Mrs. J. Mason Frith) were at home, and received the guests in the drawing-room at Rydal Mount. The arrangements for the accommodation of the numerous guests were most elaborate. A large marquee had been erected on the lawn for the purpose of partaking of refreshments, and the grounds had been most happily and tastefully laid out, with a view to every comfort. A band else dis- coursed sweet music throughout the afternoon. At about five o'clock, however, the happy couple took their departure, accompanied by every good wish, for Preston Brook Station, from whence they tra- velled to Ilfracombe, North Devonshire, where the honeymoon will be spent; and upon their return they will take up their residence at Rosneath Frodgham. The bride's going-away dress was o pale green cloth trimmed with silver braid,the fron being of pink silk trimmed with Ecru lace, and she wore a hat of white with black and white plumes The whole was a most charming costume. The ether dresses of the house party were ex- quisite in design and style but are too numerous to come under spacial note. The mother of the bride- groom wore a dress of black satin with front of em- broidered chentele, and a bonnet of heliotrope whilst the bride's sister (Niri. J. M. Frith) was at, tired in a dress of pale green voile over pink silk the bodice being tastefully trimmed with embroi, dered velvet straps. She wore a tasteful bonnet- and both ladies carried choice bouquets. The following was the programme of music played by Mr. W. Cooke, A.R C. 0., in the chapel : Before the arrival of the bride, March in B fiat (Silas); Allegretto in B minor (Guilmant); Capricco (Capocci), Andantino (H Smart) ; procession to vestry "Bridal March" from Wagner's "Lohengrin" at close Mendelssohn's "Wedding March." The whole general arrangements were carried out to perfection. From the home of the bride's father to the chapel a special :passage was made and the whole was laid with:red cloth, and over, arched with triumphal arches and festoons of bunting and flags. The interior of the chapel was elegant in a profusion of plants and flowers. The whole district, too, would undoqbtedly have been en fete, but for the melancholy filet that a dear friend of the family Mr. P. Speakman, had passed away, and that the country side mourned his loss eveu in the midst of rejoicings. The bride's cake stood over 12 feet high, and was a perfect model of the confectioners art. *Rising in three tiers, each supported by gothic pillars, upon the summit of which rested a representation of the vestal temple, containing natural flowers—orange blossom, roses and lilies of the valley, with the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>EARLESTOWN- &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN- &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY RESCUE WORK.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a meeting in support of the Salvation Army Rescue Home for Women, was held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom, Earlestown. There was a very fair attendance. The Rev. James Allett presidesi, and he was supported by the Revs. E. Bocock, J. F. Miller, Ensign Salt of Liverpool, and others. Earnest addresses were given in sup- port of the movement, and at the close a collection was made, realising a little over ..E2. CHILD DROWNED IN A TUB AT EARLESTOWN. On Wednesday afternoon a little boy named Joseph Norman Colledge, 1 year and 9 months old son of Joseph Colledge, a wagon builder, living in Crossley-street, Earlestown, met with his death by drowning in a shallow tub containing abotp three inches of water. The child's mother is an invalid, and about 4 o'clock was taken oat for an airing, the youngster playing about st the time. Aboat a quarter-of-an-hour afterwards, a Mrs. Hatch, living next door, saw a child's feet protruding above the top of a tub in her back yard, and on lifting the child out found out it was Mrs. Colleige's son. She sent for a Mrs. Harrison, a professional nurse who endeavoured to resuscitate the child by artificial respiration, but Dr. Mouncsy who arrived almost immediately pronounced life extinct. It is sup- posed the child was after a piece cf stick which was in the tub. AN EARLESTOWN MAN'S ATTEMPT XT SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Charles Netting, a boiler makTra, -about 30 years of age, lodging at 289, Wargrave-road, Earlestown, made an extraordinary attempt to commit suicide on Monday morning. Netting is a Northampton ' man, but has work.ld at the Vulcan Foundry for some time. About 8 o'clock on Monday morning he was Seen by a man named Robert Forster, to climb the side of a bridge over the main line of the Lon- don and North-Western Railway, near to Collins Green Colliery offices at Collins Green. Nettle tken deliberately jumped on the railway a distance of about 35 feet. Fortunately there were no trains at the time, and the unfortunate man after jumping on the rails roled clear. Forster raised an alarm, and men from the Collins Green offices were quickly in atteoance. The Railway Company were also informed, and Netting was removed to the Provid- ence Hospital, S. Helens, in charge of Mr. Dawson, Station Master at Collins Green. and Constable Bate- man. Dr. Jackson examined Netting at the hospi- tal, and found that besides a severe shock, his left wrist was broken. He appeared to have been drink- ing, and to have been strange in his manner for some time. A minute or two before he threw him- self off the bridge, a Manchester express passed along the line, and it is thought he had miscalcula- ted the time the train passed, or he wouid have succeeded in his rash endeavour.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE MIDSUMMER QUARTER SMSTONS.</p>
        <p>NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVER. that the GENERAL QUARTER SESSION OF THE PEACE for the County of Lancaster will be hol4en at the Castle of LANCASTER on MONDAY. the Twenty-sixth DAY of JUNE. instant, at Ten o'clock in the forenoon; and by adjournment at the following places and times, viz : At the Court House in PRESTON, on WEDNESDAY the Twent7-Eighth day of JUNE, instant, ta Ten o'clock in the Forenoon.</p>
        <p>At the Assize Courts. in MAITCHESTER, on MONDAY, the third day of JULY next, at Eleven o'clo2k in the Forenoon.</p>
        <p>At the County Sessions Rouse In LIVERPOOL. on TUESDAY, the Eleventh Day of JULY next, at half-past Ten o'clock in the Forenoon.</p>
        <p>The Solicitors engaged in Prosecution at the said Sessions are requested to take notice thnt all Depositions and Instructions for Indictments are required to be sent to the Clerk of the Peace's Office seven days at least before the said sessions respectively. C. IL SADLER. Deputy Clerk of the Peale. Clerk of the Peace's Office, Preston. 7cla hue, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN SCHOOL</p>
        <p>BOARD.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER VIEW OF THE PROGRESSIVES.</p>
        <p>'BO!tRD ,SCOOL tIVO ADVISPIGE3 OV&amp;R</p>
        <p>VOLUNTARY."</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Runcorn School Board, on Thursday evening, there were present : Rev Canon Wood (chairman) and Messrs S Taylor, J.P., E Imison, F J Norman, 3.P., J Littler, W J Cunningham, James Pritchard, with Mr J W Light- burn (clerk) and Mr A Lightburn (assistant clerk). Mr. Imison m3ved the adoption of the minutes of the School Management Committee, which recommended that the resignation of Miss Watts, assistant mistress at Victoria-road School, be accepted,and that Miss Beatrice Robinson be appointed in her place at a salary of £65 per annum, and that the tender of Mr. William Nickson, for the alteration of Granville-street School be accepted, subjeot to the approval of the amended plans by the Education Department. Mr. Imison observed that the amended plans ban resulted in the reduction of about £3OO in the tenders.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Pritchard monded the resolution and pointed out what appeared to him to be somewhat ex. traordinary considerations. At one time the Granville. street Schools were denotninational schools whilst now they were Board Schools. They had been judged unfit for maintenance at one time unless the trustees of the Wesleyan Schools made some alterations to them. How- ever permission was obtained by the Wesleyan Trustees, to carry on the work of the school until accommodation for the children could b 3 found at Victoria-road Schools, by an application to the Efucation Department. Ho ehould now like to make a great distinction between the denominational schools and the Board Schools. Had the truste 3S of the Wesleyan Schools gone to the expense of about £lOO it would have been possible for them to have continued them as denominational schools. As denominational schools they had certified accommoda- tion for 200 girls and 180 infants; and it would have gone on in perpetuity in that way, and under those conditions woulld have done for 380 children. They found now from the plan of the Board submitted to the Education Depart- ment that whilst as a denominational school it was certified to accommodate 330 children, the Department would only certify it for the accommodation of 285 children as a Board S3hool. Therefore with the same building ac3ommodation for a Board Scool there was a reduction of 95 out of 330. He thought that would illustrate to a certain d gree the difference that existed between denominational schools and the Board Schools, especially in the superior conditions under which the children were taught. For that Board to elucate 285 junior scholars it became incumbent upon them to spend the large suns of £1.500 to make the schools fit, and give them the necessary improved conditions to comply with Ithe Educational Departments requirements of a school that belonged to the people or the nation. Those were, he thought, two facts that distinctly showed the advan- tages of the Board Schools over the denominational schools. He was then speaking in the presence of denominational school managers and was open to cor- rection if he was wrong. He believed that under the old conditions it would be possible to teach 100 scholars in the space that they as Board School managers would only be allowed to accommodate 80.</p>
        <p>At this point considerable discussion task place as to the way in which the education of the children was affected.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pribchard and Mr. Norman contendei that the eonditions were more heilthful, whilst</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Nilson and the Rev. Canon Wood ridhuled tha idea of a extra space or" elbow room improving the education."</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Taylor left at this stage of the proceedings re- marking that he was going fur his tea. (Laughter.) Owing to a request having been made by Mr. Ellison that the information in respect to the Granville-street School be laid upon the table, considerable time having been spent in arriving at the figures concerned in the transaction, it was eventually agreed that the cottages brought in £l7 151. a year and that there was an annual ground rent on the school awl cottages which had been apportioned: £8 on the schools and £2 12s. 61. on the houses.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. J. Norman complained that when figures of that sort were given they shou'd be given correctly. If he had not been preset that wght it would have gone forth that in the first place the trustees asked a certaia figure. They then found they had nothing to do with the building,and that they had to give it up to the rate- payers, that a compact was then -nade with the Board and the trqstees that they were to have practically the same sum for the cottages than they asked originally for the whole buildings. These wfire not the facts. The trustees asked for £l,lOO for the school and cot'ages which was not an exc.ssive figure, and eventually the cottages were purchased for £450. It was told the Board that the cottages only brought in an income of £l7 10s., and that a ground rent of £lO was to be paid out of this money—a most erroneous figure !s,o get to the public, The Clerk observed that it was very unfortunate that no notice was given of this. He had not had time to look up the conditions.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman said what ho wished to point out wig that this school canse to the ratepayers without a penny. The ground rant wag apportioned, and a large house in Granville-street and two cottages at the hack were sold for £450. The Board had the rental from these houses as long as they to keep them, and they were only saddled with an opportioned ground rent of £2 12s ESi With regard to the general point Mr. Pritchard had raised, what he had to say was that personally he did not think they had indulged in a penny of extravagance. H 9 did not b'ame the Department for ma4inv them spend £1,300 or £1,400 on this property. Ho did not think tke Board would consider there was anything Un- necessary or extra7a2ant. It was only a clear 'antra- Lion of the fact that when the Department had to deal with a pubic body like the B )ard, they did what was right and square, and they said to them "if you a,ll going to educate children rem must eriucatl them under healthful conditions," and the ratepayers had to decide whether they were going to have them taught under the insanitary conditions they were taught under in the old days, or under these new conditions at an extra cos'.. He thought they would say "let us have the health of our childred retarded ac well as anything else. Mr. Pritchard seconded Mr. Norman's amendnaenf that the deed be laid on the table. He asked whether there was any monetary consideration. The Clerk stated there was not.</p>
        <p>Mr. Imison said tho reason there was no monetary considation was that £450 belonged to the public before- hand,and this was why there was no option. Origin- ally the offer was that if the trustees repaid to the De- partment this £450 the Board would nay the managers £1,150, or £720 if the grant of £450 had not to he repaid. N,ithor of those two arrangements as possible. The Department insisted that the schools were public property, and that if they were to be transferred to the Board they were to be transferred for either nothing or a nominal figure of 53. Then arose the position which Mr. Norman denied. The Board, he repeated, were anxious to make SOMI concession. They might describe it as dishoorst or whatever they liked, but the Board were anxious to make some return to the Wesleyan managers for the care, expense, and supervision they had given to their school for many years. They felt the ratepayers were to some extent under obligation, and that, consequently, although they could not pay them money for the building they would pay them as much as they possibly could for the property which they were free to dispose of. Bearing that in mind, they gave the architect instructions that he was to look favourably upon the proposal they wished to bring about, and, as any business man knew, a man went accord- ing to his instructions, and he could take a favourable view of the value of the property if he wrre so disnosed. They had a difficulty even than. So convince.] were the Department that the public had an absolute right to the whole of these premises on the terms of the original lease that the Board had a diffi• culty in getting them transferred at all, and Sir John Brunner and Mr. Pritchard went to London to :see the Department on the subject. Mr. Pritchard now said this wal an investment the public had reason to he grateful for. It was not an investment that would com- mend its..lf to a business man. The investment cost them £5lO, and this gave them a net return of £7 103, and if they sat there until Christmas they would not be able to alter the facts.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said Mr. Norman did not make this qualification when he was speakiu.g. Anybody listening, who was unacquainted with the education question. would have gathered from Mr. Norman's remarks, and from Mr. Pritchard'a remarks also, with regard to any voluntary scholl which was transferred for nothing to the Board, that the managers in no circumstances have power to sell. Voluntary schools were either the pro- perty ot trustees under trust deeds, and those trust decds bound thsm ; or, if not so, they might be ham- pered by having received a building grant from G )vernment in time past. Supposing a voluntary school had recPiwici a building grant, that voluntary , school was bound, before its managers could put it int the market to discharge the debt which it ewPd to the country. Siippo;ing the Wesleyan School was held under a trust deed for educational purposes only, he contended so long as the Wesleyan teaching was given in that build- ing, as soon as it became impossible for Wesleyan teach- ing to bi given in that buildinz the trustees had power to sell, and if they negotiated with the Board they could sell for the full value ef the property. He was anxious that no misleading statements should go to the 'public. If the Board thou ?lot they could have any Voluntary S2hools for nothing they would be making a great mistake. If any of the Church Sihools of which he was chairman were to come to the market they would have to pay Mil value for them. It was a very great rnistako to argue from the particular to the general, because if they did that they laid them- selves open to a complete answer such as Mr. Pritchard was going to get. He said in his argument that inv. much as ten square feet were required in a Board School and eight square feet requirei in a Voluntary School, that, therefore, all sorts of terrible consequences M to Issastitary oenditien muit Doceisarily follow. NA</p>
        <p>only that, but he suggested, though he withdrew the remark wisely, that the education which was certain to follow would be bad in consequence. He wanted to point ow, in the first place that to say that the depart- ment insisted upon Btt. in one ca,e and ton feet in another was by no means sayiag that all Voluntary Schools only gave eight feet or that School Boards neces- sarily gave ten feet. It WAS assumed in argument that it was so. but as a matter of fact it was not so. He could take them to schools where there was 25 square feet for each child, He knew Voluntary Schools with accom- modation for 253 or 300 children, and not more than 150 or 100 children in attendance. &amp;ad this was net be- cause of competition by Board Schools close by,for there was no Bard School in the neighbourhood. The fact was many of the finest schools in ths country were Voluntary Schools. Because Government allowed Vol- untary School managers to consider eight feet square sufficient for a child it did not necessarily follow that they acted upon it. Mr. Pritchard ; I used the term older Voluntary Schools.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : lam very pleased there is a modi- fication of your view. Mr. Pritchard . There is no modification of what I have said. I have had no reply to what I have stated so far.</p>
        <p>After some further dismission the minutes were agreed to, and the meeting concluded. We understand that at the conclusion of the meeting the Sohool Board Office was the scene of a fulther argu- ment between the members and doubtless we have not heard ths last of this affair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>. . ' – – --sioarra- – 4 . ,</head>
        <p>. . ' - - --sioarra- - 4 . ,</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS, LEIGH AND BOLTON LIGHT RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>PRO TEST FROM NEWTON. A meeting of the principal residents and ratepayers and prop3rty owners of Newton-le-WillJws was h3ld in the Taws* Hall, Newton, on Monday evening for the purpose of protesting againir, the Light Railway which it is propssed to carry from 'St. Helens through Newton t 3 Leigh and Bolton. Mr. R. Barton presided. and he was supported by Mesa T J Gillesp'e, F J Wood, J E Pardey, G Clarke, J Simpson, J Liptrott, Rev J Ryder. There was_a large attendance.</p>
        <p>The Chairman-said that meeting was the outcome of a semi-public meeting wWch was held last Monday night. As far as he could understand the legislation, these so-called street railways were intended to provide Light Railways for di3tricts that were not served with railways at the present tine. He believed that was the fundamental part of the Act of Parliament. If it was he did not see why a railway, should come through Newton at all. They had three railways close to them, and although some people thought they did not do all that they ought to do, his experience of the London and North-Weitern Company was that when they were ap- proached in a proper spirit they were courteous. If they had this street railway what would it do ? It could not deliver goods in London to-morrow morning. It might do so at St. Helens but it could not do it at a less rate than was already in force. Why should they have such a taing as a light railway coming through qigh-street which was as nice a street as any in Lancashire. Again, in Caurch-street, if a railway wont through Newton all the trees in the street would have to be cut down, and all the trees in High-street would have to be taken away. It was a very busy street, a main street, and there was plenty of traffic thr;ugh it. besides which there were plenty of children playing upon it. But apart from that it was not a light railway, but a heavy railway. They intended to have 4ft. Eli gauge, which meant they could bring heavy trucks through the street, and they need not use electric power alone, they could use steam, and , what would it be like. They should ha 4e no rest at night, as although they could take passengers during the day, they had to carry goods at night, and the resi- dents would have no rest at all. He maintained they should ask the District Couecil to oppose the Bill in every way. They did not want railway accommodation as they had plenty, and again it would spoil the Prettiest street in the district. He moved that " the property owners in Newton Ward stronely protest against street railways, and:strongly urge the District Council to oppose the Bill at every stage." (Applause.) Mr. J. Liptrott seconded, and said he protested with all his might against a railway coming anywhere in that district unless it was protected at both sides. It would be dangerous to children and all pedestrians, and the ratepayers would derive no benefit, all the benefit would go to a syndicate.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Ica-ye aglvd if the Railway was not a continuation of the train line front Haydock to eresclt.</p>
        <p>. . The Chairman said if it was, might he ask where it would go through.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kaye said it would, he supposed, go through to Leigh. and they would find that the train from Haydock to St. Helens would carry a parc.ll tor twopence, whereas the Railway Company charged sixpence, and that would boa saving of fourpence. _ _</p>
        <p>rho Chairman said the legislature intended these light railways for places where there was no accommoiation whatever.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Ryder asked them to remember two points. In the first place this scheme had been promo- ted by a private company, and as they had a kind of strong impression that certain of the promoters were by experieace, men who were bent upon obtaining capital for their own privee purposes, it sounded to sense that if there was to be any eapital to be obtained from this Railway, it must be either from trade or else from the sale of the Railway after it had been laid and arranged. He did not know whether any precautions had been definitely made to prevent the Railway Company fr3m selling their enhems after carrying it out, but he thought it was for Newton to consider whether it would be to the advan- tage of the village from a trade standpoint. He certainly thought that the shopkeeper would suffer very materially. They would be the first, and secondly the ratepayers generally would feel themselves to some extent handicapped in many ways by a scheme like this being carried out and a railway coming through their midst. Whatever may have taken place in St. Helens, Haydock or Leigh, that was no concern of Newton They were Newton people and were looking after themselves, and they ought to give a very unanimous and emphlatic voice from Newton that they strongly urged the District representatives to oppose the scheme in every way possible. (Hear, hear.) Mr. J. E. Pardey said this was not a light railway scheme but one for a heavy railway, wh re the rails were the same weight as in an ordinary railwaysand the the Company had taken power to go through the streets with carriages. If it was to run from St. Helens to Leigh, he asked was there not pleaty of provision already. The Company would have no power to take private property, but the District Council would have power to take any portion of private property. He did nob think the railway would be an improvement to the distriet,and he did not think the railway would be made. He thougot the gentlemen who were promising it, after they got their concession would sell it as a valuable asset. He hoped the Council would see that the district was protected. (Applause.) Mr. Adams asked if the Council were not committed to this scheme already. Councillor Simpson said that by the action the General Purposes Committee had taken 'they had practically scotched the scheme. They had laid down certain pro- visions for the Railway Company to sign, and if they refused, the Council would oppose the bill all they could. Mr. Gillespie said there seemed to him not the slightest doubt that this railway through Newton was perfectly unnecessary, and it would be an abominable disfigure- ment and would depreciate the value of property very materially. They had abundant accommodation by railway, and the only thing they had heard in its favour was' from one gentleman who said he could get his pack- ages carried for twopence instead of sixpence. There was no doubt the village would be destroyed if they allowed this Railway to go on, and they should oppose it in every possible way. (Applause.) Mr. Kaye said he would like to point eut that they did not live on trees. People had their living to think about, without so much consideration about trees. Mr. Speakman wanted *o know what it would cost the ratepayers supposing they opposed it. Mr. Barton could not say. Mr. Speakman said it W3f4 news to him to hear that it was net to be a light railway, but a heavy railway, something on the lines of an ordinary railway. He thought it was a tramway, and if so he thought a tram- way would be a distinct advantage to the district. In the winter mornings it would have the effect of carrying people to Earlestown and Newton, and in this respect it would be an improvement. As to the depreciation of the property that would simply mean lowering the value of the houses which would mean reduction of rent and th at was sadly needed in Newton. On the ground of utility he gathered it was a scheme that had money in it, it had money in for the towns, and only because it had money in it was it Ipinte promoted. He moved as an amendment that the matter be left in the hauls of the District Council, in whom the ratepayers had every confidence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Kayo seconded, and s ski he found that the neople in Earlestown wished the railway to gl through Earles- town and if it did it would be a great improvement. Councillor Simplon sail when the scheme was first mooted it was understood that this was a light railway, but it sesmei they required a 4ft. E 3., gauge.and it could not go through Earlestown, as the streets were too narrow.</p>
        <p>The amendment was put and lost by 16 votes. The motion was declared carried.</p>
        <p>Councillors Wood and Simpson said they would do all in their power to oppose the scheme as at present before them.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks to the Chairman terminated the proceedings.</p>
