        <p>this was prisoner's eighth appearance.—He was fined 5s and costs in each case. A POLICE SERGEANT Ass atlLTED.—Witliain Cairns, private of the King's Liverpool Rejmeat, Orford B arraeles, Wl3 charged with being drunk and clisorderle in Winwick-street, on Saturiay night, atel with asseuiting Cheney in the execution of his duty. —Defendant slid he did not remember the aspault.—Serg,ant Ch _rney said hi was leassing Central Station in plain clothes who he saw a young woman run- ning away from defendant and screaming. Defendant was going to assault her. Witness re-nonstrated with defendant who struek him in the mouth and knocked him down. Witness got up and told him he would have to come to the Police Station. Defendant resisted and they fell to the floor and a companion of defendant's came and tried to get him away. A young man came to witness's assistance until two other o'Ecers came up —Constable Heso and Sergeant Lewis also gave evidence.—Patrick Owen. V. Orchard-street. and William Lucock give corroborative evidence.—john Greenosigh, cab-driver, said he 51ev nefendent strike the sergeant in the mouth.—The Bench im aosed a fine of 10s and costs, or 14 days. TUESDAY.—B,fore Mr. Bircball (in the chair) and Alderman Henry Roberts. DRUNK AND Dseennenrx.--John Culledea, for being drunk and disorderly in Jackson's entry, Church-streA, the pre- vious night, was fined 5s and costs or seven days.—F e• a similar offence, Elizabeth Hedgecock was fined 51 and costs The defendant had been remanded from the previous day, when she appeared to answer the charge whilst under the influence of drink. WEDNESDAY.—Befote Alderman James Fairclough (in the chair) and Messrs. Geddes and Birchen. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Sophia Eggington was fined 5s ana costs fcr this offence at a quarter past tea on Tuesday night in Liverpool-road. RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Messrs. James Handley (presiding), John Ockleston and W. Timmins. ANTI-VACCINATION.—Mr. George A. Hayes. of Brackley- street, Run:•orn applied for a certificate of exemption from vaccination in respect of his child Edith Hayes. He objected to vaccination because he corascientously believed it was not a mitigating agent cat smallpox ' • but would rather be injuri- ous to the health of his child. He bad had personal experi- ence —The application was granted. OBSCENE LANGUAGE IN THE STREETS.—WiIIiam Whit- tingham pleaded guilty to being the user of obscene language in Coach-road. Runcorn. on the 27th May last.—The case was proved by Police Constable Nichols, and a fine of 10s includ- ing c )sts WAS inflicted—Joseph McDonald charged with committing a like offence in Ellis Court. did not appear On the evidence of P.C. Wilding a warrant was issued for his apprehension. FIRST APPEARANCE —Henry Clare, and Evan Lowe. two respectable looking young men, were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Mersey-street on the 3rd of June.— Both pleaded guilty.—The case was proved by P.C. Gregory. —ln consequence of it being the first offence, the chairman said they would be let off with a fine of 5s ; and he also ex- pressed the hope they would never appear again on a similar charge. A STREET SINGER FlKED.—Henry Summers, street minstrel, was charged, in custody, with being drunk and disorderly in Mersey-street, on the 3rd June. He pleaded not guilty.—Sergeant Gill stated the prisoner was seen by k him at about 8 o'clock. He was drun and creating a dis- turbance.—Police Constable George Carrington corrobora. ed. adding that the prisoner was previously cautioned.—Prisoner was fined 2s 61 and costs, as nothing previously was known of him. AGGRAVATED BY THE CROWD—William Dorsett pleaded guilty to being drunk in Bridge-street. Runcorn. on the 3rd of June.—The case was proved by P.C. Carrington, who stated there was a crowd of people running alter prisoner.— Prisoner contended that he had a drop of beer, but bad he not been followed and dogged by the crowd, there would have been no disturbance. He had already been confined in the police cation sines Saturday, and hoped the Bench would consider that.—Fined 2s 6d and costs or 7 days hard labour LICENSING TRANSFER.—Temporary authority was granted to Thomas Barker to sell intox!cating liquor at the Red Lion Inn, Halten, lately oecupied by Mr. Taylor. 7Nyv /./.•/;W /ANN •</p>
