cket, and in beautiful weather. Turton and Rylance were the first two batsmen. and they played steady cricket for a time, runs coming very slowly. Only nine runs had been registered when Turton had his wicket dis- turbed by Power. Storer filled the vacant crease and Ise at once got to work. His first stroke of note was a fine on drive to the boundary. Rylance however, treates the bowling with great respect, and 31 had been compiled before he had his stumps spreadeagled by a good ball from Stoddart. The third wicket fell at 81 sad eventually the whole side were dis- missed for 142. out of which total Storer had contributdd 40 which included seven fours, a three, a two, and seven singles.—G-arnett and Greenshields opened the visitors' Innings, and the former drove the first ball for a couple repeating the stroke a minute later. Garnett continued to bat freely, and he had scored 12 before his partner opened his account. Numerous bowling changes were resorted to. Fifty six runs were put together before the batsmen were parted. Garnett retiring for a well- hit 29. Eventu- ally the homesters were soundly thrashed by five wickets. scores : NEWTON. LIVERPOOL. Turton t Power Rylance b Stoddart ll Rylance Storer c Garnett b Green- Greenshields lbw b Storer 44 shields 4O Edmondson c Turton b Jones a Tilney b Green- Storer shields 24 H Harrisson b Stableford 5 Stableford 4. Edmondson b James b Stableford 0 Stoddart 4 Stoddart not out ll Wright rim out Chandley b Greenshields... 4 Kinnear, Tilney, Power, H Hindley b Stoddart Parks a Hampshire b bat 9 Stoddart l5 Henderson b Stoddart C W undies' not out 9 Extra Total</p>
