        <p>RUNCORN RUR A.L DISTRICT COUNCIL. The usual tortnigh'ey tneetine of the above Council was hid at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, ween there were present : Messrs. JJseph Knowles (chairman) S Woolware, M Williams, T Wright. T Baxter. C.C., 0 E Linaker, J G Kvird," C Rsylaares, J Spruce, E Clarke. CeV Davenport, J Stokes, T Darlene:on, W Bradford. P Gleave, Joseph B4lseaw, W Ellison, S Horton,? Leather, W Turner. T Lewis, J Mcbityre, E Bretbertee, H Mawdsley, with Mr George F Ashton (clerk), and Messrs. W Diggle and J Swinton (surveyors.) THE LATE CLERIC. Mr. Knowles feelingly referred to the death of Mr. John Ashton and proposed that a vate of condolence with the family be passed. Messrs S Woodward and C E Linaker also added a fit- ting tribute to the memory of the late Clerk. The latter observed that many of the members present had lost • friend. It would, however, be a satisfaction to himself (Mr. Linaker) to see Mr. George F Ashton installed in his late father's place. (Hear, hoer.) Mr. Mosley Williams suggested that which the vote of condolence should be incorporated with that the Board of Guardians had passed earlier in the day. The suggestion was agreed to and the vote of condo' lance unanimously passed. THE CLERKSHIP OF THE COUNCIL. Mr. J. Mclntyre proposed an i Mr. C. E. Linaker seconded the appointment of Mr. George F. Ashton ee temporary clerk to the Council. Which was unanimously agreed to. Mr. S. Woodward said there was no one disguising the fact of their intentioase He would give notice th 36 Mr. George F. Ashton be nominated as Clerk to the Council subject to the amount of eatery fixed by the Committee appointed similarity to the one empowered Se aut for the Board of Guardians. Mr. Darlington and others objected to the Committee being too small and subsequently the names of Messre• Beetter, Darlington, and Mawdsley were added to the committee for the purpose of revising the salaries of the officers. KELSEY AFFAIRS. The Beesley Parochial Committee met at the National Schools, Heleby, on Monday. A resolution of deeP sympathy with the family of the late Clerk of the Row corn District Council (Colonel Ashton) in their sad bereavement was peeved. After some discussion it wail resolved that Mr. Radford's scheme for the sewerage oe Helsby be sabmittel to an engineer.—The Nuisance Inspector having submitted a special report upon the watsr supplies to certain property in the village, the report was referred back to him with a request that be should obtain fresh analysis of the supplies, and that be be asked to definitely state whether there wai a good aed sufficient supply in each case. The minutes were confirmed. WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week Ending Saturday 27th May, 1899. During the week the following births and delthe were registered : Births. Deaths. Warriegton Registration District 32 13 The excess of births ever deaths was 19. The Birth Rato was equal to 26 per 1,000 of the population. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 10.5 per 1,000, against 18.1 the averae of the previous 4 weeks, and against 16'D the corresponding period of last year. The death' rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal te 16 per 1,000. against 46 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks, and against 1-6 the corresponding period et 1898. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 op. 5 3 1 2 2 13 The deaths included-2 from measles, 4 from lung diseases, 7 from all other causes. Toe following cases of Infectious Dieease have beefs reported during the week :—Scarlet fever 8 case* enteric fever 1 case, erysipelas 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital week ending 20th May, 48. METEOROLOGY—The mean temperature was 52.1 Fab.' and therefore 3'l degrees higher than the preceding, week. The maximum temperature 66F., was recordea on the 18tle inst., and the minimum temperature 41P.0 on the 19th inst. The Rainfall measured *55 inch. The general dirge' tion was S.W. JOHN H. GORNALL, - Medical Offioer of Health</p>
