        <p>IFATALITIES AT WARRINGTON. 1 THE RESULT OF A FALL. I Mr. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at the i Police Station on Friday afternoon on the body of 1 Richard Boardman (75),- who had a fall on the step of the Loudon and North-Western Railway on Thursday afternoon Deceased who was a beer- house keeper of Eccles, was returning home after a visit to some friends. He was told to proceed to the far end platform, after buying a ticket, but instead of going so went up the first flight of steps and on being told of his mistake retraced his steps and fell. He was conveyed to the infirmary but died on toe way. Matthew Boardman, son of the deceased s,id he last saw his father on Wednesday morning. Deceased lived at 21, Regent-street, Eccles, and was going to Grappea- hall to see his mother's grave. Witness never saw him alive again. Hugh Andrews, employ 6 of the London and North- Western Railway and a plumber, said he saw the de- ceased on Wednesday last about 3.30. He was on the wrong platform for his train and witness told him to go down the steps to the left. Deceased was sober. The steps had been recently repaired on both sides. De- ceased was found on the steps of the bookstall side,and there was a handcart at each Mee. He was bleeding from the nose.—By a juror : Witness did not know whether deceased had tumbled.—The Coroner said he knew the steps very well and they were very level. Arthur Taylor, porter, in the employ of the company, said he had just came along the paesage under the station when be heard ath ud. He turned round and found the old gentleman on his face at the bottom of the steps, evidently having fall( n down. Deceased was taken to the Infirmary, but died on the way. Detective Barlow, coroner's officer, said he inspected the steps that morning and found them in perfect order. The drop off the steps was very small, there was plenty of room on the steps, and there was a break halt way. The jury returned a verdict of "Accident death." DROWNED IN THE MERSEY. An inquest was held at the Police Station, on Friday afternoon, by Mr. Brighouse, coeciner, on the body of Patrick Finnerty, who was found ia the river Mersey, at Howley Quay, on Wednesday afternoon. Bridget Cosgrove, 18, Ship-yard, said the deceased was her brother. He was a farm labourer, at Padgate, and was 38 tyears of age next birthday. Witness saw him last on Whit-Monday. He came to visit her on the Saturday, and stopped over Sunday, and on the Monday started out to Padeate. She had not seen him since ex- cept when dead that day (Friday). Charles Forster said on Wednesday about a quarter past twelve he was passing along the river side at How- ley and saw a body floating in the water. The police were informed and with assistance the body was re- moved from the water. Witness belonged to a boat which had a crane on and the beat must have disturbed the body in the water. From the appearance of the body it must have been in the water about a fortnight. Sergeant Evans said he examined the body and searched it. There was a small comb and a small oval mirror and clay pipe in the pocket. Witness showed the articles to deceased's sister who identified them as the property of her brother. Deceased's body was con- veyed to the mortury at the Cemetery. The jury returned a verdict of " Found drowned, but how deceased got into the water there was no evidence to show." ito LICENSING. THE NEW GREAT CENTRAL HOTEL. At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Mon- day moraine, before Alderman Sutton (in the chair), and other magistrates Mr. A. Browne applied for the final grant to Thomas Edward Drum- bell, of a provisional license which was made to the Blacksmith's Arms, and which is to be known in future as the Great Central Hotel. Mr. Browne stated that the license was granted for the purpose of rebuilding the house, and changing it from a beerhouse into a public-house. The plans had been submitted to the Chief Consteble, and he and Mr. Owen, himself (Mr. Browne), and Detective Monaghan had been through the premises, and he had a representative from Messrs. Owen, architects, who would tell the Bench that the buildings had been completed in accordance with the plans. They would ,be ready for occupation almost immediately. The magistrates at the Sessions required them to give 21 days' notice of the application,and that it should be advertieed in the newspapers, and notice given to the police. That had been done. Mr. Forrest, from the office of Messra. William and Segar Owen, produced the plans. The new building, he said, had been completed in accordance with those plans with two small exceptions, which had been approved by the Chief Constable. Mr. Browne: We have closed all the houses we undertook to close for this one. We want the beerhonse opposite the Police Station for a waiting-room. Councillor Pierpoint : The hotel will be completed before you go in ? Mr. Browne replied in the affirmative, and added that the hotel would be called " The Great Central Hotel." It seemed inappropriate to call such a good house" The Blacksmiths' Arms." The application was granted. The following transfers were also allowed :—Golden Grove Inn, Lyme-3treet, from John Rigby, to Tom Bel- shaw ; refreshment rooms, Bank Quay Station, from Henry Taylor to Arthur Fitzroy Waters ; Mersey Hotel (beerhouse), Mersey-street, from William Henry Bailey to Edwin Butterworth; off-licence, 82, Longford-street, from Mary Bloomfield, deceased, to Robert Bloomfield ; off-licence, 21. Mill-lane, from William Allen, deceased. to Mary Allen ; off-licence, 60. Priestley-street, from Annie Parker, deceased, to William Walker ; off-licence, 29, Froghakl-larse, from Adelaide Roberts to Hannah Walker. The application in the case of Mr. Bloomfield was made by Mr. Jenkins, while in the case of the refreshment rooms at Bank Quay Station a representa- tive of the Company stated that Mr. Taylor had retire 1, and his place all along the line had ben taken by Mr. Waters,</p>
